window.BOOK_QCH=[["01 Sensation, thresholds and coding",18,22],["02 Active and passive perception",23,26],["03 The individual senses",27,31],["04 Visual pathways and visual development",32,35],["05 Gestalt, features and object recognition",36,39],["06 Attention, salience and hallucinations",40,45],["07 The brain basis of consciousness",46,49],["08 Mind and body",50,53],["09 Sleep, dreams and body clocks",54,58],["10 Altered states of consciousness",59,62],["11 The cognitive unconscious",63,66],["12 Mistaken introspection and attribution",67,70],["13 Brain damage, arousal and delirium",71,76],["14 Learning in brains and species",77,81],["15 Reward, punishment and emotion",82,85],["16 Classical conditioning",86,90],["17 Thorndike, Skinner and operant learning",91,94],["18 Generalisation, shaping and schedules",95,99],["19 Observation, Piaget, Vygotsky, Bruner",100,103],["20 Changing behaviour and gaining knowledge",104,107],["21 Learning and clinical problems",108,112],["22 How memories are made and recalled",113,117],["23 Memory in the brain and LTP",118,122],["24 Sensory and working memory",123,127],["25 Short-term and long-term memory",128,132],["26 Types of long-term memory",133,136],["27 Forgetting and false memory",137,141],["28 Memory disorders and assessment",142,146],["29 Intelligence and general factor g",147,150],["30 Measuring intelligence",151,155],["31 Hierarchical models and gf/gc",156,160],["32 Speed, control and intelligence",161,165],["33 Practical, emotional and multiple intelligences",166,169],["34 Culture and intelligence",170,173],["35 Genes, environment and IQ",174,178],["36 Concepts, heuristics and dual process",179,182],["37 Executive functions and reasoning",183,186],["38 Reasoning, bias and dissonance",187,190],["39 What shapes our decisions",191,195],["40 Problem solving and its obstacles",196,199],["41 Theories of creativity",200,204],["42 Building blocks of language",205,208],["43 Animal language and social learning",209,212],["44 Language, thought and experience",213,216],["45 Language development and sensitive periods",217,220],["46 How language connects to thought",221,224],["47 Communicative competence and non- verbal cues",225,229],["48 Motivation: drives, arousal and rewards",230,234],["49 Body systems, reward and the hypothalamus",235,239],["50 Hunger, satiety and obesity",240,243],["51 Emotion and motivation",244,247],["52 The biology of threat and aggression",248,251],["53 Culture, cognition, sex and belonging",252,255],["54 Maslow's hierarchy of needs",256,260],["55 Need for achievement",261,265],["56 Emotion, appraisal and basic emotions",266,269],["57 What emotions are for",270,273],["58 James-Lange versus Cannon-Bard",274,278],["59 Stress and the body",279,283],["60 Life events, vulnerability and personality",284,287],["61 Coping, control and resilience",288,293],["62 Attitudes and persuasion",294,298],["63 Attribution and person perception",299,302],["64 Theory of mind and autism",303,306],["65 The self and its disorders",307,310],["66 Leadership, power and media",311,315],["67 Conformity, obedience and groups",316,320],["68 Communication in relationships",321,325],["69 Group performance and prejudice",326,329],["70 Group structure and identity",330,333],["71 Aggression",334,337],["72 Helping, altruism and fairness",338,342],["73 Interpersonal attraction",343,346],["74 Social class and mental health",347,350],["75 Sick role and illness behaviour",351,354],["76 Family life and mental illness",355,358],["77 Society, environment and mental health",359,363],["78 Life events and their meaning",364,367],["79 Institutions and institutionalisation",368,371],["80 Criminology and penology basics",372,375],["81 Stigma, racism and bias",376,379],["82 Ethnicity and mental health",380,383],["83 Culture, meaning and help-seeking",384,387],["84 Acculturation and multicultural society",388,391],["85 Culture shapes how illness looks",392,395],["86 Ethics, law and philosophy",396,400],["87 Frameworks for development",401,404],["88 Genes, environment and adversity",405,408],["89 How development is studied",409,412],["90 Genes, epigenetics and environment",413,416],["91 Imaging the developing brain",417,422],["92 Psychoanalytic theories of development",423,427],["93 Attachment, separation and deprivation",428,432],["94 Childhood adversity, loss and divorce",433,437],["95 Families and parenting",438,441],["96 Temperament, personality and resilience",442,446],["97 Play, peers and popularity",447,450],["98 Moral development",451,455],["99 Emotions and childhood fears",456,459],["100 Gender and sexual development",460,464],["101 Adolescence and puberty",465,468],["102 Adult life changes and grief",469,472],["103 Pregnancy, birth and the baby",473,476],["104 Identity across adult life",477,480],["105 Ageing and later life",481,521]];window.BOOK_QA=[[19,"What is transduction, and does it belong to sensation or to perception?","Transduction is the conversion of physical energy into nerve impulses by receptors. It is part of sensation, the first step, not perception.",0,1],[19,"How did Gregory describe perception in relation to the raw sensory signals?","Gregory saw perception as the brain's best guess, or hypothesis, about what is out there, built from the incoming signals.",0,2],[19,"What did Muller's 1838 doctrine of specific nerve energies propose?","The quality of a sensation depends on which nerve is stimulated, not on what stimulates it. Today this is called labelled-line coding.",0,3],[19,"A 45-year-old man in a GP surgery rubs his closed eyes and sees flashes of light. Which principle explains this?","The doctrine of specific nerve energies (labelled lines). Any stimulus that activates the optic pathway is felt as light, giving phosphenes rather than a psychiatric hallucination.",0,4],[19,"Why can a single sensory fibre not signal a stronger stimulus by producing a bigger action potential?","Action potentials are all-or-none, so their size is fixed. The fibre signals intensity by firing more often instead.",0,5],[19,"What is rate coding and who first demonstrated it in single nerve fibres?","Rate coding means stimulus intensity is signalled by firing frequency. Adrian and Zotterman showed it in single fibres in 1926.",0,6],[19,"Apart from firing rate, how else does the nervous system code stimulus intensity?","By population coding: stronger stimuli recruit more fibres, including higher-threshold fibres.",0,7],[19,"How is the absolute threshold defined in psychophysics?","It is the weakest stimulus that a person detects on 50% of trials.",0,8],[19,"What is the just noticeable difference (JND) and what does Weber's law say about it?","The JND is the smallest change in a stimulus that can be detected. Weber's law states it is a constant fraction of the starting intensity.",0,9],[20,"If 105 g is just noticed as heavier than 100 g, what weight is needed against a 200 g standard?","210 g. The Weber fraction is 5%, so the JND for 200 g is 10 g.",0,10],[20,"How does Fechner's law describe the relation between stimulus intensity and perceived magnitude?","Fechner proposed that perceived size grows with the logarithm of stimulus intensity, building on Weber's law.",0,11],[20,"What is Stevens' power law and what method did Stevens use to derive it?","Perceived magnitude equals stimulus intensity raised to a power. Stevens used magnitude estimation, asking people to assign numbers to felt strength.",0,12],[20,"In Stevens' power law, how do the exponents for electric shock and brightness differ?","For electric shock the exponent is above 1, so doubling intensity feels much more than double. For brightness it is below 1.",0,13],[20,"According to signal detection theory, what two separate factors determine whether a signal is reported?","Sensitivity, measured as d-prime, and response bias, the criterion or beta. Green and Swets described this in 1966.",0,14],[20,"A 38-year-old female NHS radiologist, warned about litigation, reports more abnormal films. What changes in signal detection terms?","Her criterion becomes more lenient, so hits and false alarms both rise. Her sensitivity, d-prime, stays the same.",0,15],[20,"What is sensory adaptation and which senses adapt quickly?","A steady stimulus gradually stops being noticed, then is noticed again after a break, because receptors mainly signal change. Smell and touch adapt quickly.",0,16],[20,"How does sensory adaptation differ from habituation?","Adaptation is a receptor effect that recovers after a break. Habituation is a learned decrease in response that is reversed by a novel stimulus.",0,17],[21,"On a signal detection diagram, what happens when the criterion is moved towards stricter responding?","False alarms fall but misses rise. Sensitivity does not change because the two distributions stay the same distance apart.",0,18],[24,"How does active perception differ from passive perception?","Passive perception treats the eye like a camera that simply receives images. Active perception holds that our own movements create the information we use to perceive.",1,1],[24,"What did Held and Hein's 1963 kitten carousel experiment show?","Only the kitten that walked, not its passive partner with the same view, developed normal visually guided paw placing. Self-produced movement is needed for normal visual-motor development.",1,2],[24,"What did Gibson find about active touch compared with passive touch?","People recognise shapes far better when they actively feel them than when shapes are pressed onto their skin.",1,3],[24,"What is Gregory's constructivist theory of perception?","A top-down view in which the incomplete retinal image is interpreted by forming and testing hypotheses, filling gaps with stored knowledge.",1,4],[24,"What is Gregory's main evidence for perception being hypothesis testing?","Visual illusions, which show the brain making wrong guesses about what is out there.",1,5],[24,"What is Gibson's direct or ecological theory of perception?","A bottom-up view in which the optic array reaching a moving observer is rich enough to specify the world directly, without inference.",1,6],[24,"Name the sources of information in the optic array according to Gibson.","Invariants, optic flow and texture gradients, which together specify the layout of the environment.",1,7],[25,"What are affordances, and to which theorist do they belong?","Affordances are the action possibilities an object offers, such as a chair being sit-on-able. They belong to Gibson, not Gregory.",1,8],[25,"What is a major weakness of Gibson's theory, and who combined the two approaches?","Gibson struggles to explain illusions. Neisser combined top-down and bottom-up processing in his perceptual cycle.",1,9],[25,"What is perceptual set, and is it selective?","Perceptual set is a readiness to perceive things in a particular way. It is selective, so some features are noticed and others ignored.",1,10],[25,"What factors did Allport list as influencing perceptual set?","Expectation, context, motivation, emotion, values and culture.",1,11],[25,"What did Bugelski and Alampay (1961) demonstrate with the rat-man figure?","People shown animal pictures first saw the ambiguous figure as a rat, while those shown faces saw a man. Expectation shapes perception.",1,12],[25,"What did Bruner and Minturn (1955) show with an ambiguous B or 13 figure?","The same shape was read as B between letters and as 13 between numbers, showing the effect of context on perceptual set.",1,13],[25,"What did Bruner and Goodman (1947) find when children matched light spots to coin sizes?","Poorer ten-year-olds overestimated coin sizes more than richer children, showing that value and need alter perceived size.",1,14],[25,"What did Gilchrist and Nesberg (1952) show about hungry participants?","Hungry people rated food pictures as brighter, showing that motivation influences perception.",1,15],[25,"What is perceptual defence and what did McGinnies (1949) find?","A raised threshold for emotionally upsetting stimuli. Taboo words needed longer exposure to be reported, yet galvanic skin responses rose first.",1,16],[25,"What was the main criticism of McGinnies' perceptual defence findings?","Participants may simply have been embarrassed to say taboo words aloud, a response bias rather than a perceptual effect.",1,17],[25,"A 30-year-old woman with anxiety in a UK clinic sees neutral faces as hostile. Which concept explains this?","A perceptual set biased towards threat, driven by emotion and expectation, so she may genuinely perceive hostility.",1,18],[28,"What does Hering's opponent process theory propose about colour vision?","Colour is coded by three opposing pairs: red-green, blue-yellow and black-white. This explains afterimages and why we never see reddish green.",2,1],[28,"A 25-year-old man stares at a red square, then sees a green square on a white wall. Which theory explains this?","Opponent process theory. Prolonged stimulation fatigues the red side, so a neutral surface produces the opposing colour, green.",2,2],[28,"Why is two-point discrimination much finer on the fingertip than on the back?","Fingertip receptors are densely packed with small receptive fields and a large cortical representation.",2,3],[28,"What is lateral inhibition and what illusion does it produce?","Each active receptor suppresses its neighbours, sharpening edges. It produces Mach bands, extra light and dark lines at borders.",2,4],[28,"What does place theory of pitch say, and where are high frequencies coded?","Pitch depends on where the basilar membrane vibrates most. High frequencies peak at the base, near the oval window, and low ones at the apex.",2,5],[28,"Why is high-frequency hearing lost first in age-related hearing loss?","Ageing damages the base of the basilar membrane first, which is where high frequencies are coded.",2,6],[28,"What is the volley principle in hearing and who proposed it?","Groups of auditory fibres fire in rotation so together they can follow frequencies faster than one fibre can. Wever and Bray proposed it.",2,7],[28,"How do the semicircular canals and the otolith organs differ in function?","The semicircular canals sense head rotation. The otolith organs, utricle and saccule, sense tilt, gravity and linear acceleration.",2,8],[28,"What does Melzack and Wall's gate control theory propose?","A gate in the substantia gelatinosa of the dorsal horn is opened by thin A-delta and C fibres and closed by thick A-beta touch fibres and descending brain signals.",2,9],[29,"A 7-year-old girl bumps her knee and rubs it, easing the pain. How does gate control theory explain this?","Rubbing activates large A-beta touch fibres, which close the spinal gate to small-fibre pain signals. TENS works on the same idea.",2,10],[29,"What role does the periaqueductal grey play in pain?","It drives a descending pain-inhibiting pathway using endogenous opioids, so naloxone partly reverses this analgesia.",2,11],[29,"A 52-year-old man after arm amputation feels his missing hand when his cheek is stroked. What explains this?","Reorganisation of somatosensory cortex: the face area lies next to the hand area and takes over the deafferented region, as shown by Ramachandran.",2,12],[29,"Which sense reaches the cortex without first relaying in the thalamus, and why does this matter?","Smell. It projects directly to the piriform cortex and amygdala, which helps explain why smells trigger emotional memories.",2,13],[29,"What are the five basic tastes?","Sweet, sour, salty, bitter and umami, the savoury taste of glutamate. Most flavour is actually smell.",2,14],[29,"What does the Young-Helmholtz trichromatic theory explain, and how is it reconciled with opponent theory?","Three cone types tuned to short, medium and long wavelengths explain colour matching and colour blindness. Trichromatic coding occurs at the cones and opponent coding in ganglion cells and the lateral geniculate nucleus.",2,15],[29,"What is interoception and which brain region is linked to it?","Interoception is sensing the body's internal state, such as heartbeat, breathing and hunger. Craig linked it to the anterior insula, and it is studied in panic and anorexia.",2,16],[29,"A 60-year-old woman with B12 deficiency is steady with eyes open but sways with eyes closed. What sense is impaired?","Proprioception, carried in the dorsal columns. This is a positive Romberg test.",2,17],[29,"Loss of which sense can be an early sign of Parkinson's disease or dementia?","Loss of smell.",2,18],[33,"Describe the main visual pathway from the retina to primary visual cortex.","Signals pass along the optic nerve, nasal fibres cross at the optic chiasm, relay in the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, then reach V1 in the occipital lobe.",3,1],[33,"Which thalamic nucleus relays vision to V1, and which relays hearing?","The lateral geniculate nucleus relays vision. The medial geniculate nucleus relays hearing. The pulvinar is not the main relay to V1.",3,2],[33,"What did Hubel and Wiesel find about cells in primary visual cortex?","V1 contains feature-detecting cells that fire to edges or lines at particular orientations.",3,3],[33,"What did Ungerleider and Mishkin (1982) propose about the two visual streams?","A ventral stream to the temporal lobe codes what an object is, and a dorsal stream to the parietal lobe codes where it is.",3,4],[33,"How did Milner and Goodale reinterpret the dorsal stream, and what did patient DF show?","They saw it as vision for action. DF could not report a slot's orientation but posted a card through it accurately, so action guidance is independent of conscious perception.",3,5],[33,"Which visual areas mainly process colour and motion, and what is the binding problem?","Colour is processed mainly in V4 and motion in V5. The binding problem asks how separately processed features are combined into one object.",3,6],[33,"What solutions to the binding problem were proposed by Treisman and by Singer?","Treisman proposed that focused attention binds features. Singer proposed temporal synchrony of firing between neurons coding the same object.",3,7],[33,"What did Helmholtz mean by unconscious inference?","Perception involves the brain rapidly and automatically inferring the most likely cause of sensory input.",3,8],[33,"A 70-year-old man after stroke cannot recognise his wife's face but knows her voice. What is the likely lesion?","Damage to the fusiform gyrus (fusiform face area), causing prosopagnosia.",3,9],[34,"What is size constancy?","Perceived size stays stable even when the retinal image changes, because the brain scales image size by perceived distance, as with a car driving away.",3,10],[34,"How does Gregory explain the Ames room illusion?","The viewer wrongly assumes the trapezoid room is rectangular, so people within it appear to grow and shrink. A sensible hypothesis is misapplied.",3,11],[34,"What is the carpentered-world hypothesis of Segall and colleagues (1963)?","Growing up among straight walls and corners teaches people to read angles as depth cues, so rural Africans were less fooled by the Muller-Lyer illusion.",3,12],[34,"Which sense is least developed at birth and what is newborn visual acuity?","Vision is the least mature sense at birth, with acuity of about 6/180. Hearing already works in the womb.",3,13],[34,"What did DeCasper and Fifer show about newborn hearing?","Newborns sucked harder to hear their own mother's voice, showing prenatal auditory learning.",3,14],[34,"Outline the approximate timeline of infant visual development in the first six months.","Faces preferred at 1 month, depth at 2 months, familiar faces told apart at 3 months, colour and accommodation at 4 months and near-adult acuity by about 6 months.",3,15],[34,"What method did Fantz (1961) use and what did he find?","Preferential looking. Babies looked longer at patterned and face-like pictures than plain ones.",3,16],[34,"What did Campos find when young infants were placed over the visual cliff?","Their heart rate slowed, suggesting interest rather than fear. Gibson and Walk had shown depth perception by crawling age.",3,17],[34,"What did Hubel and Wiesel's eye-closure studies in kittens show?","Closing one eye in young kittens caused lasting poor vision in that eye, but not in adult cats, showing a critical period for visual cortex development.",3,18],[34,"What did Blakemore and Cooper show by rearing kittens in vertical stripes?","Early visual experience shapes the visual cortex, as the kittens later responded poorly to horizontal lines.",3,19],[34,"Why must a childhood squint or cataract be treated early?","Because of the critical period for visual development. Delay risks permanent amblyopia.",3,20],[37,"What is the phi phenomenon and who described it in 1912?","Apparent motion seen when two still lights flash in turn, as one light jumping. Max Wertheimer described it, founding Gestalt psychology with Koffka and Kohler.",4,1],[37,"What central Gestalt idea does the phi phenomenon illustrate?","The whole differs from the sum of its parts, because the movement exists in neither light alone.",4,2],[37,"What does the Gestalt law of proximity state?","Elements that are close together are perceived as belonging together.",4,3],[37,"Evenly spaced dots in alternating red and blue rows are seen as rows. Which Gestalt law applies?","Similarity. Equal spacing rules out proximity, so grouping is by colour.",4,4],[37,"A 68-year-old woman describes a ring of short dashes simply as a circle. Which Gestalt law is this?","Closure, the tendency to fill gaps so an incomplete figure is seen as whole.",4,5],[37,"What do the Gestalt laws of continuity and common fate describe?","Continuity groups elements that follow smooth paths. Common fate groups elements that move together.",4,6],[37,"What is the law of Pragnanz, and is familiarity a classic Gestalt law?","Pragnanz, the law of good form, says we perceive the simplest, most stable shape. Familiarity is not a classic Gestalt law.",4,7],[37,"What does Rubin's vase demonstrate?","Figure-ground reversal. It can be seen as a vase or two faces, but never both at once.",4,8],[37,"What are the two binocular depth cues?","Convergence and retinal disparity, the small difference between the two eyes' images.",4,9],[38,"A 40-year-old man loses sight in one eye after trauma. Which depth cue does he lose?","Retinal disparity, which needs two eyes. Monocular cues such as motion parallax, overlap, size and texture remain.",4,10],[38,"What is akinetopsia and what lesion causes it?","Loss of motion perception, so poured tea appears frozen. It follows bilateral damage to area V5 (MT), as in Zihl's patient.",4,11],[38,"Describe Selfridge's Pandemonium model of pattern recognition.","A feature net in which feature demons detect lines and curves, cognitive demons respond to matching letters and a decision demon chooses the loudest.",4,12],[38,"Why are feature-based models preferred to template matching?","Breaking shapes into features copes better with changes in size and style than matching whole stored templates.",4,13],[38,"What is Biederman's recognition-by-components theory?","Objects are recognised from about 36 simple 3-D parts called geons. Deleting the joints between parts impairs recognition most.",4,14],[38,"How does apperceptive agnosia differ from associative agnosia?","In apperceptive agnosia the person cannot copy a shape. In associative agnosia they copy it well but cannot identify it, though they can name it by touch.",4,15],[38,"What did Attneave (1954) show about redundancy in visual information?","Information is concentrated at changes of contour, such as corners and curves, so a drawing from a few such points remains recognisable.",4,16],[38,"What did Barlow argue about redundancy in early visual processing?","The early visual system removes redundancy so images can be coded efficiently.",4,17],[41,"In Atkinson and Shiffrin's multi-store model, what moves a sensory trace into short-term memory?","Attention. Rehearsal then maintains material in short-term memory and transfers it to long-term memory.",5,1],[41,"What did Cherry (1953) find using dichotic listening and shadowing?","People noticed physical changes in the unattended ear, such as a male voice becoming female, but not changes in language or meaning.",5,2],[41,"What is the cocktail party effect and what did Moray (1959) find?","Hearing your own name in a noisy room. Moray found own names broke through from the ignored ear in about one third of trials.",5,3],[41,"What is Broadbent's filter model of attention?","An early selection model in which a filter passes only one channel, chosen by physical features such as ear or voice, before meaning is analysed.",5,4],[41,"How did Gray and Wedderburn's Dear Aunt Jane study challenge Broadbent?","Words split across the ears were grouped by meaning, showing that the unattended channel is analysed for meaning.",5,5],[41,"What is Treisman's attenuation model?","Unattended input is turned down rather than blocked. Important words such as your name have permanently low thresholds, so they break through.",5,6],[42,"What did Deutsch and Deutsch propose, and what did Corteen and Wood find?","Late selection, in which all input is analysed for meaning. Corteen and Wood found skin responses to shock-linked words in the ignored ear.",5,7],[42,"How does Lavie's perceptual load theory reconcile early and late selection?","Under high perceptual load selection is early; under low load it is late.",5,8],[42,"What is feature integration theory (Treisman and Gelade, 1980)?","Single features pop out in parallel regardless of display size. Conjunctions of features need slow, serial focused attention.",5,9],[42,"What are inattentional blindness and change blindness?","Inattentional blindness is failing to notice an unexpected object, such as a gorilla, when attending elsewhere. Change blindness is failing to notice changes, such as a person being swapped.",5,10],[42,"How did Shiffrin and Schneider (1977) distinguish automatic from controlled processing?","Automatic processing is fast, parallel and effortless. Controlled processing is slow, serial and capacity-limited. Consistent practice makes controlled processing automatic.",5,11],[42,"What does the Stroop effect show?","Reading is automatic in skilled readers, so word meaning interferes with naming the ink colour.",5,12],[42,"How do Kahneman's and Wickens' models explain divided attention?","Kahneman proposed one flexible pool of capacity. Wickens' multiple resource theory explains why tasks using different senses interfere less.",5,13],[42,"What did Mackworth's clock test show about sustained attention?","Vigilance declined after about 30 minutes.",5,14],[42,"On a continuous performance test, what do commission and omission errors indicate?","Commission errors, responding to non-targets, indicate impulsivity. Omission errors, missing targets, indicate inattention.",5,15],[42,"Which tasks show attentional bias to threat in anxiety, and what does Easterbrook's hypothesis say?","The emotional Stroop and dot-probe tasks show threat bias. Easterbrook's cue utilisation hypothesis says high arousal narrows attention.",5,16],[42,"What does poor P50 suppression indicate in schizophrenia?","Weak sensory gating, as the response to a second click is not suppressed normally.",5,17],[42,"What is Kapur's aberrant salience model of psychosis?","Excess dopamine makes neutral things feel charged and significant. Delusions are attempts to explain this, and antipsychotics dampen salience before beliefs fade.",5,18],[42,"How do prediction error and signal detection ideas explain hallucinations?","Hallucinations may arise from over-weighting expectations over sensory input. Voice-hearers show a liberal response bias and source monitoring errors, not altered sensitivity.",5,19],[42,"An 84-year-old woman with macular degeneration sees vivid people but knows they are unreal. What is the likely diagnosis?","Charles Bonnet syndrome, where visual cortex deprived of input generates images. It needs reassurance, not antipsychotics.",5,20],[47,"What are the neural correlates of consciousness?","The minimal set of neural events jointly sufficient for a specific conscious experience, such as seeing a face.",6,1],[47,"Who launched the scientific search for neural correlates of consciousness, and when?","Crick and Koch, in 1990.",6,2],[47,"Why are binocular rivalry and masking useful paradigms for studying consciousness?","The stimulus stays constant while conscious experience changes, so brain activity linked to awareness can be isolated.",6,3],[47,"Is a neural correlate of consciousness the same as its cause?","No. A correlate shows association only, not causation or explanation.",6,4],[47,"Describe Baars' 1988 global workspace theory using the theatre metaphor.","Many unconscious specialist processors work backstage. A spotlight of attention selects content, which is broadcast to the whole system and becomes conscious.",6,5],[47,"In global workspace theory, what does broadcast content become available for?","Report, memory and planning, which is what makes it conscious.",6,6],[47,"Who developed the global neuronal workspace model?","Dehaene and Changeux, who turned Baars' theory into a brain model.",6,7],[47,"In masking studies, what distinguishes seen from unseen words in Dehaene's model?","Unseen words cause brief local activity. Seen words cause late, sudden, sustained, all-or-none spread across fronto-parietal cortex, called ignition.",6,8],[48,"What is Tononi's integrated information theory?","Consciousness is the amount of integrated information a system generates, measured as phi. It requires many possible states unified as a whole.",6,9],[48,"Why does the cerebellum contribute little to consciousness according to integrated information theory?","Despite having many neurons, its wiring is poorly integrated.",6,10],[48,"Where do Koch and Tononi place the substrate of conscious experience?","In a posterior cortical hot zone of parietal, temporal and occipital cortex. Frontal activity mostly reflects the task and reporting.",6,11],[48,"What is the role of brainstem arousal systems in consciousness?","They set the level of consciousness, or arousal, rather than its content.",6,12],[48,"What is a no-report paradigm and why is it used?","It infers what someone perceives from automatic signs such as eye movements or pupil size, removing confounds from reporting, decisions and memory.",6,13],[48,"What happens to frontal activity in binocular rivalry when report is removed?","Much of the frontal activity disappears, supporting the view that it reflects reporting rather than awareness itself.",6,14],[48,"Name one clinical application of theories of consciousness.","Testing for hidden awareness in brain-injured patients who cannot respond. They also inform research on anaesthesia, sleep and dissociation.",6,15],[48,"Match ignition and phi to their theories.","Ignition belongs to Dehaene's global neuronal workspace. Phi belongs to Tononi's integrated information theory.",6,16],[51,"What did Chalmers (1995) distinguish as the easy and hard problems of consciousness?","Easy problems concern how the brain discriminates, attends and reports, explainable by mechanisms. The hard problem asks why any processing feels like something.",7,1],[51,"What are qualia?","The subjective felt qualities of experience, such as the redness of red.",7,2],[51,"What is Jackson's knowledge argument, also known as Mary's room?","Mary knows all physical facts about colour but lives in a black and white room. On seeing red she learns something new, so physical facts are not all the facts.",7,3],[51,"What point did Nagel make by asking what it is like to be a bat?","Subjective experience cannot be fully captured by an objective, outside description.",7,4],[51,"What is Descartes' substance dualism and where did he say mind and body meet?","The mind is a non-physical thinking substance and the body a physical extended substance. He said they meet at the pineal gland.",7,5],[51,"What is the main objection to Cartesian dualism?","The interaction problem: it cannot explain how a non-physical mind causes physical movement. Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia raised this.",7,6],[51,"Apart from the interaction problem, what evidence counts against dualism?","Brain injury and drugs clearly change the mind, suggesting mind depends on the physical brain.",7,7],[52,"What is mind-brain identity theory and who proposed it?","A sensation simply is a brain process, as lightning is an electrical discharge. Place (1956) and Smart (1959) proposed it.",7,8],[52,"What is multiple realisability and which theory does it challenge?","The same mental state, such as pain, could be realised by very different physical systems, such as an octopus brain. It challenges identity theory and supports functionalism.",7,9],[52,"What is functionalism and who developed it?","Putnam's view that a mental state is defined by its causal role, so it could in principle run on silicon. It underpins the computer analogy.",7,10],[52,"What is the main criticism of functionalism?","It leaves out qualia, the subjective feel of experience.",7,11],[52,"What is dual-aspect monism and whose view is it?","Spinoza's view that there is one reality with mental and physical aspects, neither reducible to the other, so there is no interaction problem.",7,12],[52,"What did Gilbert Ryle mean by the ghost in the machine and a category error?","He criticised dualism for treating the mind as a separate object beside the body, like asking where the university is after seeing Oxford's colleges.",7,13],[52,"Which philosophical position is Ryle's view often classed as?","Logical, or analytical, behaviourism.",7,14],[52,"What is the mereological fallacy described by Bennett and Hacker?","Ascribing to the brain or its parts what only a whole person can do, as in saying the brain decides. Such claims are senseless rather than false.",7,15],[52,"Whose name is linked to psychophysical parallelism, as distinct from dual-aspect monism?","Leibniz is linked to parallelism; dual-aspect monism is Spinoza's.",7,16],[52,"How is the mereological fallacy relevant when a UK psychiatrist explains illness to a 35-year-old patient?","Saying the brain is sick may help, but treating the whole person respects meaning and context and avoids attributing illness only to an organ.",7,17],[55,"Which EEG rhythm is seen in a relaxed adult awake with eyes closed?","Alpha rhythm, 8 to 13 Hz, over the occipital region.",8,1],[55,"What EEG features characterise stage N2 sleep, and how much of the night does it fill?","Sleep spindles of about 12 to 14 Hz and K-complexes. N2 makes up about half of the night's sleep.",8,2],[55,"What EEG pattern characterises N3 sleep?","High-amplitude delta waves below 2 Hz. N3 is slow-wave sleep and the sleeper is very hard to wake.",8,3],[55,"Who discovered REM sleep and why is it called paradoxical sleep?","Aserinsky and Kleitman in 1953. The EEG looks awake but skeletal muscles show atonia, sparing breathing and eye muscles.",8,4],[55,"How long is a sleep cycle and how many occur each night?","About 90 minutes, with 4 to 6 cycles per night.",8,5],[55,"How are N3 and REM distributed across the night?","N3 dominates early in the night, while REM periods lengthen towards morning.",8,6],[55,"What proportion of sleep is REM in adults and in newborns?","About 20 to 25% in adults and about 50% in newborns.",8,7],[55,"What changes in sleep occur with normal ageing?","Less slow-wave sleep, more awakenings and earlier sleep timing.",8,8],[55,"What are the typical sleep EEG changes in depression?","Shortened REM latency, increased REM, reduced N3 and early morning waking.",8,9],[55,"What is the effect of one night of total sleep deprivation in depression?","Mood lifts in about half of depressed patients, but it relapses after the next sleep.",8,10],[55,"Where is the master circadian clock and how does it control melatonin?","The suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus. It receives light input from the eye and signals the pineal gland to make melatonin, from serotonin, in the dark.",8,11],[56,"What is the most powerful zeitgeber for the human circadian clock?","Light. Morning light advances the clock and evening light delays it.",8,12],[56,"What is Borbely's two-process model of sleep regulation?","Sleep pressure, process S, builds during waking partly through adenosine, and interacts with the circadian clock, process C, to time sleep.",8,13],[56,"Define circadian, ultradian and infradian rhythms with an example of each.","Circadian is about 24 hours, such as sleep-wake. Ultradian is shorter, such as the 90-minute sleep cycle. Infradian is longer, such as the menstrual cycle.",8,14],[56,"How do Freud's theory and Hobson and McCarley's activation-synthesis theory explain dreams?","Freud saw dreams as disguised wishes, with manifest and latent content. Activation-synthesis says random pontine bursts in REM are woven into a story by the cortex.",8,15],[56,"How do sleep terrors differ from nightmares in a 6-year-old boy?","Sleep terrors arise from N3 early in the night with confusion and no recall. Nightmares arise from REM late in the night with full alertness and detailed recall.",8,16],[56,"What causes narcolepsy and what are its main features?","Loss of hypothalamic orexin neurons lets REM intrude, causing sleep attacks, cataplexy with laughter and rapid REM onset.",8,17],[56,"What did Van Dongen find with two weeks of six-hour nights?","Lapses in performance kept rising while sleepiness ratings levelled off, so people underestimated their impairment.",8,18],[56,"What hormonal changes did Spiegel find after sleep restriction?","Leptin fell and ghrelin rose, increasing hunger.",8,19],[57,"A 66-year-old man punches his wife while dreaming, with loss of REM atonia. What is his long-term risk?","He has REM sleep behaviour disorder and is at high risk of a synucleinopathy, such as Parkinson's disease or dementia with Lewy bodies, needing neurology follow-up.",8,20],[60,"What features of altered states of consciousness did Ludwig (1966) describe?","Altered thinking, changed sense of time, intensified emotion, altered body image and a sense of heightened meaning.",9,1],[60,"Are altered states of consciousness always pathological?","No. Many are chosen, brief and valued, such as prayer, meditation or ceremony.",9,2],[60,"Who introduced the term hypnosis, and when?","James Braid, a Scottish surgeon, in 1843. He rejected Mesmer's magnetic fluid.",9,3],[60,"What EEG pattern is seen during hypnosis?","A normal waking pattern, despite the name coming from the Greek for sleep.",9,4],[60,"What is hypnosis?","A state of focused attention with greatly increased openness to suggestion.",9,5],[60,"What did Hilgard's hidden observer experiment show?","A hypnotised woman denied pain with her arm in iced water, yet a hidden part of her reported pain, supporting neodissociation theory.",9,6],[61,"What is the non-state view of hypnosis and who holds it?","Spanos and Barber argue there is no special state; hypnotised people sincerely enact the role they expect.",9,7],[61,"Is hypnotic susceptibility stable, and how common is high hypnotisability?","It is a stable trait measured by the Stanford scales. Only about 10 to 15% of people are highly hypnotisable.",9,8],[61,"What EEG and physiological changes occur in meditation?","Increased alpha and theta activity, with lower oxygen use and slower heart and breathing rates, called the relaxation response by Benson.",9,9],[61,"What is the clinical use of mindfulness-based cognitive therapy?","It is recommended to help prevent relapse in people with recurrent depression.",9,10],[61,"By what receptor mechanism do classic psychedelics act, and what evidence supports this?","They are 5-HT2A receptor agonists. Ketanserin, a 5-HT2A blocker, largely abolishes psilocybin effects (Vollenweider, 1998).",9,11],[61,"How does ketamine's mechanism differ from that of classic psychedelics?","Ketamine is an NMDA receptor antagonist causing a dissociative state, not a 5-HT2A agonist.",9,12],[61,"What did Leary mean by set and setting?","The person's mindset and surroundings shape whether a drug experience is pleasant or frightening.",9,13],[61,"Why is DMT in ayahuasca orally active?","The vine contains a monoamine oxidase A inhibitor, so DMT is not broken down in the gut.",9,14],[61,"Through which receptor does Salvia divinorum act?","Kappa-opioid receptors, producing brief, intense dissociation.",9,15],[61,"When does a trance or possession state count as a disorder in ICD-11?","Only when it is involuntary, distressing or impairing. A voluntary, culturally accepted state limited to ceremony is not a disorder.",9,16],[61,"A 24-year-old woman in a UK clinic feels unreal, like a robot, but knows this is not true. What is this?","Depersonalisation. Retained insight is the key feature; loss of insight suggests psychosis.",9,17],[62,"What subjective effects typically follow psilocybin?","Altered perception and geometric imagery, synaesthesia, altered time sense and ego dissolution.",9,18],[64,"What did Kihlstrom (1987) mean by the cognitive unconscious?","Much perception, memory and learning happen outside awareness simply because they are automatic. Nothing is being kept out of awareness on purpose.",10,1],[64,"How does Kihlstrom's cognitive unconscious differ from Freud's dynamic unconscious?","Freud's unconscious holds upsetting material pushed away by repression. Kihlstrom's involves no repression; processing is non-conscious for structural reasons.",10,2],[64,"What did Kunst-Wilson and Zajonc (1980) show using shapes flashed too briefly to be recognised?","People could not tell old shapes from new ones but still liked the old ones more. The mere exposure effect can occur without conscious recognition.",10,3],[64,"What is masked priming and what does it show about unconscious processing?","A word is flashed briefly and then hidden by a mask. Although not consciously seen, it still speeds decisions about related words.",10,4],[64,"In Libet's 1983 experiment, what were participants asked to do and report?","They flexed a wrist whenever they chose and noted the clock position when they first felt the urge to move.",10,5],[64,"What is the slow negative EEG build-up that begins before a reported urge to move called?","The readiness potential. It starts several hundred milliseconds before the person reports the conscious urge.",10,6],[64,"According to Libet, what could consciousness still do even though the readiness potential came first?","It could veto the movement before it happened, an idea called 'free won't'.",10,7],[65,"How did Schurger (2012) reinterpret the readiness potential?","He argued it mostly reflects random neural fluctuations drifting towards a threshold, not a decision already made. Averaging trials aligned to the movement makes noise look like a plan.",10,8],[65,"Why is contingent negative variation not the same as the readiness potential?","Contingent negative variation arises between a warning stimulus and an imperative stimulus. The readiness potential comes before self-paced voluntary action.",10,9],[65,"What was Newell and Shanks' (2014) main criticism of research on unconscious influences on decisions?","Awareness tests are often insensitive, so conscious knowledge is wrongly ruled out. Several famous priming findings have also failed to replicate.",10,10],[65,"Which four qualities did Newell and Shanks say a good test of awareness must have?","It must be reliable, relevant, immediate and sensitive.",10,11],[65,"How do most researchers now view the boundary between conscious and unconscious processing?","As graded, with a continuous link between the two rather than a sharp dividing line.",10,12],[65,"What are the three main stages of Levelt's (1989) model of speech production?","A conceptualiser forms a wordless message, a formulator selects words and grammar and then sound form, and an articulator produces the speech.",10,13],[65,"In Levelt's model, what is the difference between a lemma and a lexeme?","The lemma carries a word's meaning and grammar. The lexeme is the word's sound form.",10,14],[65,"In Levelt's model, how do speakers catch and correct their own speech errors?","They monitor their speech through their own comprehension, or listening, system and repair slips.",10,15],[65,"Explain the tip of the tongue state in terms of lemma and lexeme.","The lemma has been retrieved but the lexeme is blocked. The person knows the meaning but cannot bring up the sound of the word.",10,16],[65,"What did Brown and McNeill (1966) find when people were in a tip of the tongue state?","After hearing definitions of rare words, people often knew the first letter and number of syllables before the whole word came.",10,17],[65,"A 69-year-old woman tells her GP that frequent word-finding pauses mean she has dementia. What should be considered?","Tip of the tongue states become more common with normal ageing. This complaint alone does not indicate dementia.",10,18],[68,"A 58-year-old man with alcohol-related Korsakoff syndrome, on a ward for weeks, says he went to work this morning. What is this?","Confabulation: honestly telling false memories to fill gaps. He believes his account and has no intent to deceive.",11,1],[68,"How did Kopelman divide confabulation into two types?","Provoked confabulation is brief errors when questioned and is common in amnesia. Spontaneous confabulation occurs unprompted and is often linked to frontal damage.",11,2],[68,"Apart from Korsakoff syndrome, name two conditions in which confabulation is seen.","Split-brain patients and anosognosia. Both show how readily the brain fills gaps with a plausible story.",11,3],[68,"What did Nisbett and Wilson's (1977) stockings study show?","Shoppers mostly chose the right-hand pair of identical stockings, praised its quality and denied position mattered. People have little introspective access to the causes of their choices.",11,4],[68,"In Nisbett and Wilson's work, what did the guesses of outside observers show?","Outsiders gave the same reasons as participants, so people seem to report plausible theories rather than true inner knowledge of causes.",11,5],[68,"What is choice blindness, described by Johansson and Hall (2005)?","Failing to notice that a chosen face has been secretly swapped for the rejected one, then giving reasons for it, sometimes naming features only the rejected face had.",11,6],[68,"What is Pronin's introspection illusion about judging bias in ourselves and others?","We judge ourselves by our inner thoughts but others by their actions. Because we feel no bias inside, we believe we are less biased than others.",11,7],[68,"What does Wegner's theory of apparent mental causation propose?","The feeling of willing an act is an inference made from thought and action. It is not a direct readout of the true cause of behaviour.",11,8],[68,"What are Wegner's three cues that make us feel we caused an action?","Priority (the thought comes first), consistency (the thought fits the act) and exclusivity (no rival cause is present).",11,9],[68,"What happened in Wegner's 'I spy' study?","Participants heard an object's name just before a partner forced the cursor to stop on it. They felt they had chosen that stop themselves.",11,10],[68,"How might Wegner's theory help explain a symptom of psychosis?","Breakdown of the priority, consistency and exclusivity cues may contribute to passivity experiences.",11,11],[69,"What did Dutton and Aron (1974) find when men on a high swaying bridge met an attractive interviewer?","They wrote more sexual stories and phoned her more than men on a low steady bridge. Fear arousal was misread as attraction.",11,12],[69,"Which theory of emotion is supported by misattribution of arousal?","Schachter and Singer's two-factor theory, in which emotion is physiological arousal plus a cognitive label.",11,13],[69,"How did Ellis and Young explain Capgras syndrome?","The face is consciously recognised but the emotional route fails, with no warm feeling or skin conductance response. The person concludes the familiar person is an impostor.",11,14],[69,"How does Capgras syndrome differ from prosopagnosia?","In Capgras, conscious face recognition is intact but the emotional response is missing. In prosopagnosia, conscious face recognition itself fails.",11,15],[69,"What is the central false belief in Fregoli syndrome?","The belief that strangers are really a known person, often a persecutor, in disguise.",11,16],[69,"What is Gazzaniga's split-brain interpreter and what does it do?","The speaking left hemisphere invents explanations for actions made by the right hemisphere, such as saying a shovel is for cleaning out a chicken shed.",11,17],[69,"How does confabulation differ from pseudologia fantastica and malingering?","Confabulation has no intent to deceive. In pseudologia fantastica the person knows the stories are untrue when challenged; malingering is deliberate falsification for external gain.",11,18],[69,"How does confabulation differ from deja vu?","Deja vu is a brief feeling of false familiarity. Confabulation is a false account of past events given with conviction.",11,19],[72,"What are the two components of consciousness?","Arousal, which is being awake, and awareness, which is knowing what is going on.",12,1],[72,"An 84-year-old woman is drowsy and withdrawn two days after hip surgery and cannot say the months backwards. Likely diagnosis?","Hypoactive delirium. It is often mistaken for depression and carries a worse prognosis.",12,2],[72,"Which brain system maintains arousal, and where is it?","The ascending reticular activating system, located in the brainstem.",12,3],[72,"What did Moruzzi and Magoun (1949) show by stimulating the reticular formation in anaesthetised cats?","The EEG changed from synchronised slow waves to a desynchronised waking pattern, showing the brainstem drives arousal.",12,4],[72,"Why can a small upper brainstem lesion cause coma when a large one-sided cortical stroke usually does not?","The brainstem lesion damages the ascending reticular activating system, which keeps the whole cortex aroused.",12,5],[72,"What are the maximum scores for each part of the Glasgow Coma Scale and the total range?","Eyes 4, verbal 5 and motor 6, giving a total from 3 to 15.",12,6],[72,"A 45-year-old man in A&E opens eyes to pain, makes incomprehensible sounds and withdraws from pain. What is his GCS?","8 (eyes 2, verbal 2, motor 4). A score of 8 or less indicates coma and a threatened airway.",12,7],[72,"What defines the vegetative state and how does the minimally conscious state differ?","In the vegetative state arousal and sleep-wake cycles remain but there is no awareness or purposeful response. The minimally conscious state shows small but definite signs of awareness.",12,8],[72,"What usually causes locked-in syndrome and how do patients communicate?","A ventral pons stroke. The person is fully aware but paralysed, and communicates by blinking and vertical eye movements.",12,9],[73,"What is blindsight, as shown by Weiskrantz's patient DB?","After damage to primary visual cortex he denied seeing anything on one side yet pointed to targets far above chance. Pathways via the superior colliculus bypass V1.",12,10],[73,"A split-brain patient cannot name a key flashed to his left visual field but his left hand finds it. Why?","The image reaches the right hemisphere, which cannot speak but controls the left hand. Language is usually in the left hemisphere.",12,11],[73,"What did Claparede's hidden pin handshake experiment show?","His amnesic patient did not remember him but refused to shake his hand. Implicit memory can guide behaviour without conscious recollection.",12,12],[73,"What is covert face recognition in prosopagnosia?","Skin conductance rises to family faces even though the person cannot consciously recognise them.",12,13],[73,"Which lesion typically causes hemispatial neglect and what is seen?","Right parietal damage. The left side of space is ignored even though vision is normal.",12,14],[73,"What did Bisiach and Luzzatti's imagined Milan cathedral square task show?","Neglect patients described only the right side from either imagined viewpoint. Neglect can affect mental images, called representational neglect.",12,15],[73,"What is anosognosia and who first described it?","Unawareness of one's own deficit, such as insisting a paralysed left arm is fine. It was described by Babinski in 1914.",12,16],[73,"What is the cardinal cognitive deficit in delirium and a quick bedside test for it?","Attention, not memory. Saying the months of the year backwards, part of the 4AT, tests it.",12,17],[73,"What are the motor subtypes of delirium and which is commonest in older people?","Hyperactive, hypoactive and mixed. Hypoactive is the commonest in older people and is often missed.",12,18],[73,"What EEG change did Engel and Romano describe in delirium?","Generalised slowing of background activity that tracks severity. Alcohol withdrawal delirium may instead show fast activity.",12,19],[74,"Which features best separate delirium from dementia?","Delirium has acute onset, fluctuating course and marked inattention, with clouding of consciousness.",12,20],[78,"What did Hebb (1949) propose about learning at the synapse?","When cell A repeatedly helps fire cell B, the link between them strengthens. This was later summed up as 'cells that fire together, wire together'.",13,1],[78,"What did Hebb mean by cell assemblies?","Groups of linked neurons that together hold a learned idea or association.",13,2],[78,"Which phenomenon later gave Hebb's rule a measurable mechanism?","Long-term potentiation.",13,3],[78,"Which simple reflex did Kandel study in the sea slug Aplysia?","The gill-withdrawal reflex: touching the siphon makes the slug pull in its gill.",13,4],[78,"What synaptic change causes habituation of the Aplysia gill-withdrawal reflex?","Less neurotransmitter is released from the sensory neuron onto the motor neuron. It is not a loss of receptors on the motor neuron.",13,5],[78,"What is the molecular pathway of short-term sensitisation in Aplysia?","A tail shock makes interneurons release serotonin onto the sensory neuron, raising cAMP and activating PKA, so more transmitter is released.",13,6],[78,"What features of imprinting did Lorenz describe in greylag goslings?","Goslings follow the first moving object seen after hatching. It is rapid, limited to a brief sensitive period, needs no reward and is hard to reverse.",13,7],[78,"How did imprinting influence Bowlby's ideas about human attachment?","Bowlby borrowed the idea of a sensitive period, although human attachment is far more flexible than imprinting.",13,8],[79,"What extra steps does long-term sensitisation in Aplysia require?","PKA moves to the nucleus and activates the transcription factor CREB, leading to new protein synthesis and synapse growth.",13,9],[79,"What happens to sensitisation in Aplysia if protein synthesis is blocked?","The long-term form is prevented but the short-term form still occurs.",13,10],[79,"How do midbrain dopamine neurons respond to an unexpected reward, according to Schultz?","They fire a burst of activity.",13,11],[79,"After a light reliably predicts juice, how does dopamine firing change?","The burst moves to the light, and the fully predicted juice causes no change in firing.",13,12],[79,"What happens to dopamine firing when a predicted reward fails to arrive?","Firing dips below baseline at the moment the reward was due.",13,13],[79,"What do midbrain dopamine neurons encode, and which learning model does this fit?","Reward prediction error rather than pleasure itself. It fits the error term of the Rescorla-Wagner model.",13,14],[79,"Which brain structure is essential for the conditioned eyeblink response in rabbits?","The interpositus nucleus of the cerebellum, not the hippocampus. A lesion abolishes the learned blink but leaves the reflex blink to the air puff.",13,15],[79,"Which view of memory storage did McCormick and Thompson's eyeblink findings challenge?","Lashley's mass action, the idea from rat studies that memory is spread widely across the cortex.",13,16],[79,"What did Marler show about song learning in young sparrows?","They copy only their own species' song, in the local dialect, if heard early. Birds raised alone sing poorly.",13,17],[79,"What is instinctive drift, as described by the Brelands?","Inborn habits pull learned behaviour back. Raccoons trained to drop coins in a box began rubbing and dipping them like food and lost reward.",13,18],[79,"What is Seligman's concept of preparedness?","Each species is biologically built to learn some links fast, some slowly and some never.",13,19],[83,"A 10-year-old boy's nails are painted with bitter varnish and his nail biting decreases. Which operant process is this?","Positive punishment: an unpleasant taste is added after biting, so biting becomes less likely.",14,1],[83,"In operant conditioning, what do reinforcement, punishment, positive and negative mean?","Reinforcement increases behaviour and punishment decreases it. Positive means something is added; negative means something is taken away.",14,2],[83,"A 14-month-old girl gets a chocolate button each time she waves goodbye and now waves at everyone. Which process?","Positive reinforcement: a pleasant consequence is added and the behaviour increases.",14,3],[83,"A 32-year-old man with panic disorder leaves a supermarket and his panic ends. Which process strengthens leaving?","Negative reinforcement. Relief from panic removes an unpleasant state, which keeps avoidance going in anxiety.",14,4],[83,"A 15-year-old loses his games console after missing curfew and is then home on time. Which process?","Negative punishment, also called response cost: something pleasant is removed to reduce a behaviour.",14,5],[83,"What is the difference between a primary and a secondary reinforcer?","A primary reinforcer works without learning, such as food or warmth. A secondary reinforcer, such as money, praise or tokens, gains value through links with primary ones.",14,6],[83,"What is the Crespi effect, also called negative contrast?","Negative contrast: rats switched from a large to a small reward ran slower than rats that always had the small reward.",14,7],[84,"What is meant by delay discounting of rewards?","The loss of a reward's value the longer one has to wait for it.",14,8],[84,"Why does someone choose £10 now over £20 next month but prefer £20 in 13 months over £10 in 12?","Delay discounting is hyperbolic, not exponential. Value falls very steeply at short delays, so preferences reverse.",14,9],[84,"How does delay discounting differ in people with addiction?","They discount delayed rewards more steeply, favouring small immediate rewards.",14,10],[84,"According to Azrin and Holz (1966), when is punishment most effective?","When it is immediate, consistent and not built up gradually.",14,11],[84,"What are the main drawbacks of punishment as a way to change behaviour?","It mainly suppresses behaviour and teaches no alternative. It can cause fear, avoidance of the punisher and aggression, and it models aggression.",14,12],[84,"On a children's inpatient unit, a 9-year-old boy stops hitting only when a strict nurse is on shift. Why?","The punisher has become a discriminative stimulus, a cue that punishment is around, so the behaviour is suppressed only in her presence.",14,13],[84,"How should punishment be used if it is used at all in a ward behaviour plan?","Pair it with reinforcement of a desired alternative behaviour, and rely mainly on rewarding wanted behaviour.",14,14],[84,"What are Paul Gilbert's three emotion regulation systems and their linked neurochemicals?","Threat (anxiety, anger), drive (wanting and achieving, linked to dopamine) and soothing (calm and connection, linked to oxytocin and endorphins).",14,15],[84,"In Gilbert's model, which system is weak in harsh self-critics and which therapy strengthens it?","The soothing, or affiliative and safeness, system. Compassion-focused therapy builds it.",14,16],[84,"In Gross's model, how does reappraisal differ from expressive suppression?","Reappraisal changes how a situation is thought about early on and lowers distress. Suppression hides the feeling outwardly after it has started.",14,17],[84,"What happened when people suppressed their emotions while watching upsetting material?","Their arousal rose and they later remembered fewer details, because holding the feeling in used up attention.",14,18],[84,"In operant terms, why does exposure with response prevention help anxiety?","Avoidance is maintained by negative reinforcement through relief. Preventing escape stops this reinforcement so fear can fall.",14,19],[87,"A 6-year-old girl bitten by a terrier now cries whenever it appears. Identify the UCS, UCR, CS and CR.","The bite is the UCS and pain and fear the UCR. The terrier has become the CS and fear of it is the CR.",15,1],[87,"What did Pavlov show about reflexes, and what was his Nobel Prize (1904) awarded for?","He was first to show systematically that a reflex such as salivation can be linked to a neutral signal. His Nobel Prize was for work on digestion.",15,2],[87,"In Pavlov's experiment, what are the unconditioned stimulus and unconditioned response?","Food (meat powder) is the UCS because it works without learning. Salivation to food is the UCR.",15,3],[87,"After training in Pavlov's experiment, what are the bell and the salivation to the bell called?","The bell is the conditioned stimulus and salivation to it is the conditioned response, usually a little weaker than the UCR.",15,4],[87,"What feature most clearly separates classical from operant conditioning?","In classical conditioning the responses are reflexive and involuntary and the learner is passive. Operant learning involves voluntary actions changed by their consequences.",15,5],[87,"What is delay (forward) conditioning and how effective is it?","The CS starts first and stays on until the UCS arrives. It gives the fastest and strongest learning.",15,6],[88,"What is trace conditioning and how does it compare with delay conditioning?","The CS ends and the UCS follows after a gap. Learning is weaker and relies more on the hippocampus.",15,7],[88,"What is backward conditioning and why is it the weakest arrangement?","The UCS comes before the CS. The CS predicts nothing, so there is often no learning.",15,8],[88,"What is the optimal interval from CS onset to UCS onset for most reflexes?","About half a second.",15,9],[88,"In Watson and Rayner's Little Albert study, what were the UCS and the CS?","A sudden loud noise was the UCS and the white rat became the CS. The fear then spread to other furry objects.",15,10],[88,"A 52-year-old woman feels sick as she enters the chemotherapy day unit. What is this?","Anticipatory nausea, a classically conditioned response to cues linked with chemotherapy.",15,11],[88,"What is second-order conditioning in classical conditioning?","An established CS, such as a light, is used to condition a new neutral stimulus, such as a tone, that was never paired with the UCS.",15,12],[88,"What is Kamin's blocking effect in classical conditioning?","If a tone already predicts shock, an added light learns almost nothing, because it adds no new predictive information.",15,13],[88,"What is Eysenck's incubation of fear?","Brief exposures that end at peak anxiety can make a fear grow rather than fade.",15,14],[88,"What did Garcia and Koelling (1966) show about conditioned taste aversion?","Rats linked a taste with illness after one trial despite a delay of hours, but did not link taste with shock.",15,15],[88,"Which concept is supported by conditioned taste aversion, and which timing rule does it break?","Seligman's preparedness, since some links are learned more easily. It breaks the half-second rule, forming over hours in a single trial.",15,16],[89,"Why must exposure therapy sessions last long enough for fear to fall?","If sessions stop at peak anxiety, incubation may make the fear worse.",15,17],[89,"How do second-order conditioned responses compare with first-order ones?","They are usually weaker and extinguish more easily, but show how fears and cravings attach to cues never paired with the original event.",15,18],[92,"How is operant conditioning defined in learning theory?","Learning in which a voluntary response becomes linked to its consequence. Reinforcing consequences increase it; punishing ones decrease it.",16,1],[92,"What type of learning did Thorndike's cats show in puzzle boxes (1898)?","Trial and error learning. Escape times fell gradually over many trials, with no sudden leap of insight.",16,2],[92,"What is Thorndike's law of effect (1911)?","Actions followed by a satisfying result are stamped in. Actions followed by discomfort are stamped out.",16,3],[92,"What apparatus did Skinner develop and which animals did he use?","The operant chamber (1938), where rats pressed levers; later pigeons pecked keys.",16,4],[92,"What is a discriminative stimulus in operant conditioning?","A cue signalling when a response will be rewarded, such as a red light during which lever pressing brings food. It signals but does not trigger a reflex.",16,5],[92,"A 12-year-old boy on a paediatric ward is praised by a nurse for taking tablets and cooperates more. Which process?","Positive reinforcement: something pleasant is added and the behaviour increases.",16,6],[93,"A 40-year-old man with tension headaches takes paracetamol sooner each time because the pain eases. What maintains this?","Negative reinforcement: removing an unpleasant state increases the behaviour. It helps explain analgesic overuse.",16,7],[93,"A rat receives a shock each time it presses a lever and stops pressing. Which process is this?","Positive punishment: an unpleasant stimulus is added and the behaviour decreases.",16,8],[93,"What type of operant process is time out, and what is a rough guide to its length?","Negative punishment, as it is time away from positive reinforcement. A rough guide is one minute per year of age.",16,9],[93,"What is a primary reinforcer and when does it work less well?","One that meets a bodily need, such as food, water or warmth. It works less well when the need is already met, for example when the animal is full.",16,10],[93,"What is a secondary reinforcer and how is it used in psychiatric settings?","A reinforcer such as money, tokens, praise or grades that gains value through links with primary reinforcers. Token economies on wards use this.",16,11],[93,"What is the Premack principle in operant conditioning?","A preferred activity can be used to reward a less preferred one, such as homework before playing outside. It is also called grandma's rule.",16,12],[93,"What is the difference between escape learning and avoidance learning?","In escape, a response ends an unpleasant stimulus already present. In avoidance, a response to a warning signal stops it happening at all.",16,13],[93,"Why is avoidance learning so resistant to extinction?","It is maintained by negative reinforcement, and the animal never tests whether the danger has gone.",16,14],[93,"Why do exposure-based treatments for anxiety stop patients escaping or using safety behaviours?","Escape and safety behaviours are negatively reinforced by relief, which keeps anxiety and avoidance going.",16,15],[94,"Which theory of phobia is underpinned by avoidance learning?","Two-factor theory: fear is acquired by classical conditioning and maintained by operant avoidance that is negatively reinforced.",16,16],[96,"A 27-year-old woman frightened by a spider soon fears beetles and moths too. What process is this?","Stimulus generalisation: a learned response is triggered by cues similar to the original one.",17,1],[96,"What is the generalisation gradient in conditioning?","The more similar a new cue is to the trained one, the stronger the response. Fear of a 1000 Hz tone is strong at 1100 Hz and weak at 3000 Hz.",17,2],[96,"What is stimulus discrimination in conditioning?","Responding only to the cue that was paired or rewarded and not to similar ones, such as a dog drooling to a high tone but not a low tone.",17,3],[96,"A 45-year-old woman feels sick with round tablets after chemotherapy but not with her capsules. What does the lack of nausea to capsules show?","Stimulus discrimination: the capsule differs enough from the tablet that it does not trigger the conditioned nausea.",17,4],[96,"Why do tantrums that parents give in to only sometimes become very persistent?","Intermittent reinforcement makes unrewarded responses normal, so the end of reward is hard to detect and the behaviour resists extinction.",17,5],[96,"What is extinction and what did Bouton show about it?","The response fades when the cue keeps occurring without the outcome. Bouton showed it is new learning laid on top, not erasure of the original memory.",17,6],[96,"What is spontaneous recovery after extinction?","After a rest, an extinguished response briefly returns, often weaker. It occurs because extinction does not erase the original learning.",17,7],[96,"Parents of a 4-year-old boy stop giving sweets for tantrums, and tantrums briefly worsen. What is this?","An extinction burst. It is common but not certain, so parents should be warned and urged to stay consistent.",17,8],[96,"A medical student stops feeling faint in the dissection room after repeated visits. What process is this?","Habituation: a harmless stimulus repeated many times stops causing a response.",17,9],[96,"After a car crash, a 35-year-old man startles at horns, doors and cutlery. Which non-associative process is this?","Sensitisation: after a frightening event, many stimuli cause an exaggerated response.",17,10],[96,"Why are habituation and sensitisation called non-associative learning?","Neither needs pairing of stimuli or any reward. Extinction, by contrast, is associative.",17,11],[97,"What is shaping and how did Isaacs and colleagues use it?","Shaping rewards successive approximations to a goal. They used gum to reward a mute man for eye contact, then lip movement, then a sound, then the word 'gum'.",17,12],[97,"What is chaining, and what is the advantage of backward chaining?","Chaining links simple steps into a sequence, each cueing the next. Backward chaining teaches the last step first so each attempt ends in success.",17,13],[97,"What is cueing, or prompting, in behavioural training?","Giving a hint that helps the right action happen, then gradually fading the hint.",17,14],[97,"How do continuous and partial reinforcement compare for learning and extinction?","Continuous reinforcement gives fastest learning but fastest extinction. Partial reinforcement is slower to learn but persists much longer, the partial reinforcement extinction effect.",17,15],[97,"What schedule pattern is best for establishing then maintaining a new behaviour?","Start with continuous reinforcement to establish it, then thin it out to partial reinforcement so it lasts.",17,16],[97,"What response pattern does a fixed ratio schedule produce, and what is an example?","Fast responding with a pause after each reward. Piecework pay is an example.",17,17],[97,"Which reinforcement schedule maintains gambling, and what are its effects?","Variable ratio, as in fruit machines. It gives the highest, steadiest response rate and the most resistance to extinction.",17,18],[97,"What response pattern does a fixed interval schedule produce?","A scallop: little effort just after a reward, then faster responding as the next reward becomes due, as with a monthly salary.",17,19],[97,"What response pattern does a variable interval schedule produce, and what is an example?","Moderate, steady responding. Checking for messages is an example.",17,20],[101,"A 5-year-old boy copies his father by shouting and kicking a cupboard when frustrated at nursery. Which process explains this?","Observational learning: he stored and reproduced behaviour he saw, with no reward needed to acquire it.",18,1],[101,"What did Bandura's Bobo doll study (1961) show?","Nursery children who watched an adult attack an inflatable doll later copied the same actions. Aggression can be learned by observation without reward.",18,2],[101,"What did Bandura's 1963 follow-up of the Bobo doll study add?","Children also copied aggression from filmed or cartoon models, not only live ones.",18,3],[101,"How does social learning theory relate to conditioning?","It adds thinking steps, such as attention and memory, to conditioning, and allows learning simply by watching others.",18,4],[101,"What are Bandura's four processes of observational learning?","Attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation. Judgement is not one of them.",18,5],[101,"Which kinds of models are children most likely to imitate?","Models who are similar to them, skilled, warm and of high status.",18,6],[101,"What is vicarious reinforcement in social learning theory?","Seeing a model rewarded or praised makes the observer more likely to copy the behaviour.",18,7],[101,"In Bandura's 1965 study, what did it show when children copied a punished model only once offered stickers?","Learning and performance are distinct. The behaviour had been learned but was only performed when there was an incentive.",18,8],[101,"A 62-year-old man in cardiac rehabilitation manages a short walk, then attempts a longer one. Which concept grows most through this?","Self-efficacy, the belief that one can carry out a particular task. It grows most through mastery experiences.",18,9],[101,"In Piaget's theory, what is the difference between assimilation and accommodation?","Assimilation fits new information into an existing schema, like calling a cow 'doggie'. Accommodation changes the schema or makes a new one.",18,10],[101,"What are schemas and equilibration in Piaget's theory?","A schema is an organised pattern of thought or action. Equilibration is the inner drive to balance assimilation and accommodation when new facts do not fit.",18,11],[102,"What are the key features of Piaget's sensorimotor stage?","From 0 to 2 years, infants learn through senses and action. Object permanence appears at about 8 to 9 months.",18,12],[102,"What are primary circular reactions and what does deferred imitation show?","Primary circular reactions, at 1 to 4 months, are repeated pleasing actions on the baby's own body. Deferred imitation near age 2 shows mental representation.",18,13],[102,"What are the main features of Piaget's preoperational stage?","From 2 to 7 years: symbolic function, egocentrism, centration with failure of conservation, animism and transductive reasoning.",18,14],[102,"Which task demonstrates egocentrism in the preoperational child?","Piaget's three mountains task, in which the child cannot describe the view from another person's position.",18,15],[102,"Which abilities emerge in the concrete operational stage, and at what age?","From 7 to about 11 or 12 years, children grasp conservation of liquid and number and can classify by a clear rule.",18,16],[102,"What marks the formal operational stage and which task demonstrates it?","From about 11 or 12, systematic hypothesis testing, changing one variable at a time. The pendulum problem demonstrates it.",18,17],[102,"What did McGarrigle and Donaldson's 'naughty teddy' study show?","Many 4 to 6-year-olds conserved when a teddy messed up the counters by accident. Piaget underestimated young children because his tasks were socially confusing.",18,18],[102,"What is Vygotsky's zone of proximal development, and what is scaffolding?","The gap between what a child can do alone and with guidance. Scaffolding, described by Bruner and colleagues, is temporary support withdrawn as skill grows.",18,19],[102,"What are Bruner's three modes of representing knowledge?","Enactive (through action), iconic (through images) and symbolic (through words and symbols).",18,20],[105,"A 42-year-old woman has not drunk alcohol since detoxification two weeks ago and attends a support group. Which stage of change?","Action. Maintenance only begins after about six months of sustained change.",19,1],[105,"What did Tolman and Honzik (1930) show with unrewarded rats in a maze?","Rats that explored without food quickly caught up with always-fed rats once food was added. They had learned the maze but only showed it when given an incentive.",19,2],[105,"What is latent learning, as described by Tolman?","Learning that occurs without reward and stays hidden until there is an incentive to show it.",19,3],[105,"What is Tolman's cognitive map and what evidence supported it?","A mental representation of a layout. When a familiar route was blocked, rats took a new shortcut straight to the goal.",19,4],[105,"A 30-year-old man drives a route perfectly the first time after years as a passenger. What does this show?","Latent learning: knowledge gained without reward that appears when it is needed.",19,5],[105,"What did Kohler (1925) observe in chimpanzees on Tenerife?","A chimp unable to reach bananas sat still, then suddenly fitted two sticks together to rake in the fruit. This is insight learning.",19,6],[105,"How does insight learning differ from trial and error learning?","Insight comes suddenly by seeing the problem in a new way, is well remembered and transfers to new problems. Trial and error is gradual.",19,7],[106,"Match the theorists Pavlov, Thorndike, Skinner, Tolman, Kohler and Bandura to their key ideas.","Classical conditioning, law of effect, operant conditioning, latent learning and cognitive maps, insight, and modelling or social learning respectively.",19,8],[106,"Who developed the transtheoretical model of change and from which population?","Prochaska and DiClemente (1983), from studying smokers who quit. Preparation was added in a 1992 update.",19,9],[106,"A 50-year-old man drinking 60 units a week tells his GP he has no problem. Which stage, and what helps?","Precontemplation, with no intention to change within six months. Work should focus on raising awareness rather than action plans.",19,10],[106,"A 35-year-old man knows smoking harms him and might stop this year but enjoys it. Which stage of change?","Contemplation: he sees the problem but is ambivalent, and may change within about six months.",19,11],[106,"A 38-year-old woman has set a quit date and bought nicotine patches. Which stage of change?","Preparation: planning to act within about one month and taking small steps.",19,12],[106,"When does the action stage become maintenance, and how is relapse viewed?","At about six months of sustained change. Relapse is common and seen as part of the cycle, not a failure.",19,13],[106,"What is termination in the stages of change model?","A later addition describing a state with no temptation to return to the old behaviour.",19,14],[106,"What is motivational interviewing and who developed it?","A warm, collaborative style of conversation that helps people find their own reasons to change, developed by Miller and Rollnick.",19,15],[106,"What is developing discrepancy in motivational interviewing?","Gently highlighting the gap between what a person does and what they value, such as wanting to be a good father while using cannabis.",19,16],[106,"What are the four classic principles of motivational interviewing?","Expressing empathy, developing discrepancy, rolling with resistance and supporting self-efficacy.",19,17],[106,"In motivational interviewing, why is change talk important?","Change is more likely when the patient voices reasons for change than when the clinician argues for it.",19,18],[106,"What four processes describe motivational interviewing in the 2013 edition?","Engaging, focusing, evoking and planning.",19,19],[109,"What are the two stages of Mowrer's two-factor theory of how fears persist?","Fear is first acquired by classical conditioning. It is then maintained by avoidance, because the relief from avoiding is negatively reinforcing.",20,1],[109,"Why does avoidance stop a phobic patient from losing her fear?","Because she always leaves, she never stays long enough to learn that the feared situation is safe, so extinction cannot happen.",20,2],[109,"What did Seligman mean by preparedness in relation to phobias?","We are biologically primed to fear ancient dangers, which is why phobias of snakes, heights and the dark are commoner than fears of sockets or cars.",20,3],[109,"A 6-year-old girl speaks only when one particular therapist is present. Which learning process does this show?","Discriminative learning: speech occurs only in the presence of a cue that signals safety.",20,4],[109,"What is Wolpe's principle of reciprocal inhibition?","Relaxation and anxiety cannot be felt at the same time, so a calm response can be used to block fear.",20,5],[109,"What are the three steps of systematic desensitisation?","Training in relaxation, building a graded hierarchy of feared scenes, then working up the hierarchy step by step, usually in imagination.",20,6],[109,"A 30-year-old woman stays in a hospital lift with her therapist for two hours until her anxiety falls. What technique is this?","Flooding: prolonged real-life exposure to the most feared situation without escape, so anxiety falls by habituation and extinction.",20,7],[109,"How does implosion differ from flooding?","Implosion uses exposure in imagination, often with exaggerated scenes, whereas flooding is in real life. Neither uses a graded hierarchy.",20,8],[109,"What is the essential active ingredient in behavioural treatments for phobias, and what is first-line for a specific phobia?","Exposure to the feared stimulus is the key ingredient. Graded exposure therapy is the treatment of choice, not medication.",20,9],[110,"How does exposure and response prevention work in OCD?","The patient faces the feared trigger but does not perform the ritual, which removes the negative reinforcement that maintains compulsions. NICE recommends CBT with ERP first line.",20,10],[110,"What does habit reversal training involve and for which conditions is the evidence best?","Awareness of the urge plus a competing response, such as tensing muscles against a tic. Evidence is best for tics and trichotillomania.",20,11],[110,"How does covert sensitisation differ from aversion therapy?","Aversion therapy pairs a real behaviour with an actual unpleasant stimulus, whereas covert sensitisation (Cautela) pairs an imagined behaviour with imagined disgust or consequences.",20,12],[110,"A 35-year-old man receives vouchers for drug-free urine samples. What technique is this and what learning principle?","Contingency management, which uses positive reinforcement. Disulfiram, by contrast, acts as an aversive deterrent.",20,13],[110,"On a rehabilitation ward, patients earn tokens for self-care and lose tokens for shouting. Name both techniques.","Earning tokens exchanged for rewards is a token economy (Ayllon and Azrin). Losing tokens for unwanted behaviour is response cost.",20,14],[110,"What is differential reinforcement of other behaviour (DRO)?","Reward is given for each period in which the unwanted behaviour, such as self-injury, does not occur.",20,15],[110,"How is the enuresis pad and bell alarm traditionally explained?","By classical conditioning: bladder fullness becomes a cue for waking. Avoidance learning may also contribute.",20,16],[110,"What is stimulus control therapy for insomnia?","Bootzin's method of keeping the bed for sleep only, so the bed becomes a strong cue for sleep rather than wakefulness.",20,17],[114,"What are the three basic stages of memory?","Encoding forms the first trace, storage keeps it (with consolidation making it stable), and retrieval brings it back to mind.",21,1],[114,"Does giving cues at the time of testing improve encoding?","No. Cues at testing aid retrieval. Chunking, imagery, mnemonics, spacing and self-testing act at encoding.",21,2],[114,"What did Craik and Lockhart propose in their levels of processing theory?","Deep processing of meaning produces better memory than shallow processing of how a word looks or sounds.",21,3],[114,"Which stage of sleep supports consolidation of declarative memory?","Slow-wave sleep, which is richest early in the night, as the hippocampus replays the day. Late REM-rich sleep was linked with skill learning.",21,4],[114,"What is reconsolidation, as shown by Nader, Schafe and LeDoux?","A retrieved memory becomes unstable and must be stored again; blocking amygdala protein synthesis after a reminder removed conditioned fear in rats.",21,5],[114,"How does reconsolidation differ from extinction?","Reconsolidation alters the original memory, whereas extinction lays down new learning on top of it.",21,6],[114,"A 68-year-old woman in a memory clinic must say 12 heard words in any order without prompts. Which retrieval task is this?","Free recall, the hardest retrieval task. It typically shows primacy and recency effects.",21,7],[115,"How do cued recall and recognition differ?","Cued recall supplies a prompt such as a category or first letter. Recognition asks the person to identify a previously met item from a set.",21,8],[115,"Why is recognition easier than recall, and what exam format relies on it?","The item itself provides a complete retrieval cue. Multiple-choice exams mainly draw on recognition.",21,9],[115,"What are the two processes in dual-process theory of recognition?","Familiarity, a sense of knowing without context, and recollection, which brings back detail and context.",21,10],[115,"What is redintegration?","Recall of a whole memory triggered by a small cue, such as an old song bringing back a whole episode.",21,11],[115,"What does Tulving's encoding specificity principle state?","A retrieval cue works best when it matches the information present when the memory was formed.",21,12],[115,"What did Godden and Baddeley show with divers?","Words learnt underwater or on the beach were recalled best in the same setting: context-dependent memory.",21,13],[115,"A 45-year-old man with depression recalls sad memories far more easily than happy ones. What is this strictly called?","Mood-congruent memory (Bower). Mood-dependent memory strictly means recall is best when mood matches mood at learning.",21,14],[115,"What did Bartlett's War of the Ghosts study show about memory?","Memory is reconstructive: retold stories became shorter and fitted the reteller's culture, as gaps were filled using schemas.",21,15],[115,"What did Loftus and Palmer show about leading questions?","Using the word smashed raised speed estimates and led people to falsely remember broken glass, so open questions should be used.",21,16],[119,"Which structure is needed to form new declarative memories, and what did patient H.M. show?","The hippocampus. After bilateral medial temporal removal in 1953, H.M. could not form new declarative memories but still learned motor skills.",22,1],[119,"Which brain structure underlies procedural memory?","The striatum of the basal ganglia, with the cerebellum handling simple conditioned reflexes.",22,2],[119,"A 40-year-old man with early Huntington's disease struggles to learn a rotary pursuit task but recalls instructions. Why?","Huntington's damages the striatum, impairing procedural skill learning while declarative memory is relatively spared early.",22,3],[119,"What role does the amygdala play in memory?","It adds emotional significance to memories, especially fear learning.",22,4],[119,"What is long-term potentiation?","A lasting, activity-dependent increase in the strength with which one neuron drives another; a working version of Hebb's rule.",22,5],[119,"Who first described LTP and how?","Bliss and Lomo in 1973, by brief high-frequency stimulation of the rabbit perforant path, which strengthened dentate gyrus responses.",22,6],[120,"Why is the NMDA receptor called a coincidence detector?","Magnesium blocks its channel at rest; it opens only when glutamate binds and the postsynaptic membrane is depolarised, so both cells must be active.",22,7],[120,"Which ion enters through the NMDA receptor to trigger LTP?","Calcium.",22,8],[120,"What is the mechanism of early LTP and how long does it last?","Calcium activates CaMKII, which inserts extra AMPA receptors into the postsynaptic membrane. It lasts 1 to 3 hours and needs no new protein.",22,9],[120,"What does late LTP require that early LTP does not?","CREB-driven gene expression, new protein synthesis and structural growth at the synapse.",22,10],[120,"LTP forms normally but fades within about 4 hours. What has most likely been blocked?","Protein synthesis, as shown by Frey and colleagues. An NMDA blocker would instead prevent LTP from forming at all.",22,11],[120,"What did Morris show using the water maze and AP5?","Rats given the NMDA blocker AP5 could not learn the hidden platform location but could learn visual discrimination, linking hippocampal LTP to spatial learning.",22,12],[120,"What is systems consolidation?","New memories depend first on the hippocampus and gradually become stored in the neocortex over years.",22,13],[120,"A 55-year-old man with bilateral hippocampal damage loses memories from recent years but recalls childhood. What is this pattern called?","Temporally graded retrograde amnesia, or Ribot's gradient, explained by systems consolidation.",22,14],[120,"How does multiple trace theory differ from the standard consolidation model?","Nadel and Moscovitch argue that vivid, detailed episodic memories always remain dependent on the hippocampus.",22,15],[120,"Which drugs affect memory through NMDA receptors?","Ketamine and alcohol, which helps explain their impairment of new learning.",22,16],[124,"What are the key features of sensory memory?","It is modality-specific and unprocessed, with large capacity but very brief duration. Unattended information is lost.",23,1],[124,"How long does iconic memory last?","Less than half a second.",23,2],[124,"What did Sperling's partial report technique show?","The iconic store briefly holds more than can be reported, but it fades within a fraction of a second.",23,3],[124,"How long does echoic memory last?","About 2 to 4 seconds, longer than iconic memory, which is why you can answer just after saying what.",23,4],[124,"Who proposed the working memory model and what did it replace?","Baddeley and Hitch (1974), replacing the idea of short-term memory as a single passive store with an active workspace.",23,5],[125,"What is the role of the central executive?","It allocates attention and coordinates the subsystems but stores nothing itself.",23,6],[125,"Which brain region is most linked to the central executive and working memory?","The frontal lobes, mainly the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex.",23,7],[125,"What does the phonological loop hold?","About 2 seconds of speech, rehearsed by an inner voice and heard by an inner ear.",23,8],[125,"What is the word-length effect?","Short words are recalled better than long ones because more fit into the phonological loop.",23,9],[125,"What is articulatory suppression and which component does it block?","Repeating a word such as the aloud occupies the inner voice, blocking the phonological loop and removing the word-length effect.",23,10],[125,"What evidence shows the visuospatial sketchpad is separate from the phonological loop?","Dual-task studies: tracking a light is hardly affected by repeating digits but is badly disrupted by a visual task.",23,11],[125,"What does the episodic buffer do and when was it added?","Added by Baddeley in 2000, it binds information from different sources into integrated episodes and links with long-term memory.",23,12],[125,"Which function is least affected by a selective working memory impairment?","Retrieval of long-established facts, such as where a person grew up.",23,13],[125,"Which psychiatric conditions commonly show working memory deficits?","Schizophrenia, ADHD, depression and frontal brain injury. Deficits predict coping with daily tasks.",23,14],[125,"A 62-year-old woman with hippocampal amnesia can hold a normal conversation. Why?","Hippocampal amnesia spares working memory, so short-term holding of information is intact.",23,15],[129,"Describe the Atkinson and Shiffrin multi-store model.","Information passes in sequence from a sensory register to a short-term store with attention, then to a long-term store with rehearsal.",24,1],[129,"What are the main criticisms of the multi-store model?","It is too simple: the type of rehearsal matters, and each store is really several systems.",24,2],[129,"What is the capacity of short-term memory according to Miller?","About 7 plus or minus 2 items, which remains the exam default.",24,3],[129,"A 20-year-old student remembers 199819452001 as three familiar years. What process is this?","Chunking, which groups items into meaningful units to stretch effective capacity.",24,4],[129,"What is the pure capacity of STM when chunking and rehearsal are blocked?","About 4 plus or minus 1 chunks (Cowan 2001).",24,5],[129,"What did the Brown-Peterson task show?","With rehearsal blocked by counting backwards, recall fell to about 10% after 18 seconds. Interference also explains much of the loss.",24,6],[129,"What did Conrad show about coding in short-term memory?","People confused similar-sounding letters such as B and V even when seen, so STM coding is mainly acoustic.",24,7],[130,"What did Baddeley (1966) show about coding in long-term memory?","After a delay people confused words of similar meaning, so LTM coding is mainly semantic.",24,8],[130,"What are the capacity and duration of long-term memory?","Capacity is almost unlimited and memories may last a lifetime.",24,9],[130,"What explains the primacy and recency effects in the serial position curve?","Primacy reflects long-term memory because early items were rehearsed most. Recency reflects items still held in short-term memory.",24,10],[130,"What did Glanzer and Cunitz find with a 30-second filled delay before recall?","The recency effect disappeared while primacy remained, supporting separate short- and long-term stores.",24,11],[130,"How does the serial position curve change in amnesia from hippocampal damage?","Primacy is lost while recency is preserved, because short-term span is normal but new long-term memories fail.",24,12],[130,"How does maintenance rehearsal differ from elaborative rehearsal?","Maintenance rehearsal is simple repetition that keeps items in STM. Elaborative rehearsal links material to meaning and builds lasting LTM traces.",24,13],[130,"What is the main criticism of levels of processing theory?","It is circular, because depth of processing is hard to measure independently of recall.",24,14],[130,"Which revision strategies work best for long-term retention?","Spaced practice and self-testing. Roediger and Karpicke showed testing yourself beats rereading.",24,15],[134,"How did Squire divide long-term memory?","Into declarative (explicit) memory, which is knowing that and can be stated, and non-declarative (implicit) memory, which is knowing how and shows only in performance.",25,1],[134,"A pianist plays perfectly but cannot describe her finger movements. What feature of implicit memory does this show?","Implicit memory cannot be consciously inspected and is expressed only in performance.",25,2],[134,"How did Tulving divide declarative memory?","Into episodic memory for personal events tied to time and place, and semantic memory for facts without the context of learning.",25,3],[134,"Which type of memory is most hippocampus-dependent and fails first in Alzheimer's disease?","Episodic memory, which Tulving called mental time travel.",25,4],[134,"Knowing that Canberra is the capital of Australia is which type of memory?","Declarative semantic memory, a context-free mental encyclopaedia.",25,5],[134,"Which condition erodes semantic memory and which brain region is affected?","Semantic dementia, affecting the anterior temporal lobes.",25,6],[134,"What is the reminiscence bump?","Adults over 50 recall most autobiographical memories from about age 10 to 30, probably because these years are full of firsts and shape identity.",25,7],[135,"What is childhood amnesia?","The inability of adults to recall events from before about age 3 to 4.",25,8],[135,"A 70-year-old man forgets to buy milk on the way home despite intending to. Which memory has failed?","Prospective memory, remembering to do something in future, which needs self-initiated retrieval and is often an early complaint in dementia.",25,9],[135,"Which brain structures support procedural memory and in which disorders is it impaired?","The basal ganglia and cerebellum. It is spared in amnesia but impaired in Parkinson's and Huntington's disease.",25,10],[135,"What did Milner show about H.M. and mirror drawing?","He improved at mirror drawing day by day yet denied ever having done the task, showing procedural memory is spared in amnesia.",25,11],[135,"What is priming and is it preserved in amnesia?","Earlier exposure speeds later processing without awareness. Warrington and Weiskrantz showed it is intact in amnesia.",25,12],[135,"Where is classical conditioning placed in Squire's taxonomy, and which structure supports eyeblink conditioning?","It is non-declarative memory. Eyeblink conditioning depends on the cerebellum.",25,13],[135,"How is emotional memory classified and which structure supports it?","It is non-declarative; the amygdala can learn fear even when the event itself is forgotten.",25,14],[135,"An 80-year-old man with Alzheimer's still drives skilfully. Does this mean driving is safe?","No. Preserved procedural skill does not mean safe judgement, and the DVLA must still be informed.",25,15],[136,"After herpes encephalitis, a 45-year-old man cannot recall his therapist but improves daily at mirror tracing. What is preserved?","Procedural memory, which relies on cortico-striatal and cerebellar circuits rather than the hippocampus.",25,16],[138,"What does the Ebbinghaus forgetting curve show?","A steep early loss followed by levelling off. Relearning was still faster, showing savings, and spaced review flattens the curve.",26,1],[138,"For which memory store does trace decay theory fit best?","Short-term memory. Most everyday long-term forgetting is retrieval failure instead.",26,2],[138,"What is the commonest cause of everyday long-term forgetting?","Retrieval failure, which Tulving called cue-dependent forgetting: the memory exists but the right cue is missing.",26,3],[138,"A doctor knows a drug begins with h but cannot say haloperidol. What is this?","Blocking, the tip-of-the-tongue state (Brown and McNeill).",26,4],[138,"How do proactive and retroactive interference differ?","In proactive interference old learning disrupts new, such as writing an old postcode. In retroactive interference new learning disrupts old.",26,5],[138,"A man can find his keys only when drunk again. What is this phenomenon?","State-dependent memory, where recall is best when internal state matches the state at learning.",26,6],[138,"What is repression in terms of forgetting?","Freud's motivated forgetting of painful material. Maslow's hierarchy is about motivation, not a theory of forgetting.",26,7],[138,"What is source misattribution?","Correctly remembering a fact or event but attributing it to the wrong source.",26,8],[139,"A 50-year-old man with depression describes his warm childhood as miserable. What is this?","Retrospective falsification: unconsciously rewriting the past to fit present mood.",26,9],[139,"How do deja vu and jamais vu differ, and in which condition do both occur?","Deja vu is false familiarity with something new; jamais vu is a familiar place feeling new. Both occur in temporal lobe epilepsy.",26,10],[139,"What is the misinformation effect?","Misleading information after an event alters the stored memory, as when the word smashed led people to recall broken glass.",26,11],[139,"What did the Deese-Roediger-McDermott paradigm show?","After hearing related words such as bed, rest and dream, people confidently recall an unpresented word such as sleep.",26,12],[139,"What did the lost in the mall study show?","Loftus and Pickrell showed that repeated suggestion can implant whole false childhood memories, relevant to the recovered memory debate.",26,13],[139,"What is a flashbulb memory and how accurate is it?","A vivid memory of where one heard shocking news (Brown and Kulik). It is confident but not reliably accurate, as Neisser and Harsch showed.",26,14],[139,"What are weapon focus and own-race bias in eyewitness testimony?","Weapon focus draws attention away from the face. Own-race bias means more false identifications of people from less familiar ethnic groups.",26,15],[139,"When does eyewitness confidence predict accuracy?","Confidence at the first fair, double-blind line-up is useful. Later courtroom confidence is not, as feedback inflates it.",26,16],[139,"What does Brewin's dual representation theory say about trauma memory?","Trauma creates strong sensory traces poorly linked to hippocampal time-and-place context, so flashbacks feel as if happening now while the verbal account is patchy.",26,17],[139,"What did the trauma film studies show about playing Tetris afterwards?","It reduced intrusive images without reducing deliberate recall, by competing for visuospatial resources needed to consolidate sensory images.",26,18],[143,"What does digit span test and what are normal values?","Attention and immediate memory, not long-term storage. Normal forward span is 7 plus or minus 2 and reverse span about 5 plus or minus 2.",27,1],[143,"An 82-year-old woman with delirium repeats only three digits forwards. What does this mean for further memory testing?","Later memory tests are hard to interpret because information is not registered. Low span suggests delirium or frontal dysfunction rather than pure amnesia.",27,2],[143,"In what order should memory be examined in the mental state examination?","Attention first, then recent memory checked with staff, then remote memory checked with family.",27,3],[143,"What mainly declines with normal ageing?","Delayed recall, while cues and recognition still help.",27,4],[143,"What is preserved and what is lost in the amnesic syndrome?","Episodic memory and new learning fail, while digit span, skills, intellect and old facts are preserved.",27,5],[143,"What causes Korsakoff syndrome and which structures are damaged?","Thiamine deficiency, usually with alcohol, damaging the mammillary bodies and medial thalamus.",27,6],[143,"A 58-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has dense new-learning failure, confabulation and normal digit span. What is the diagnosis?","Korsakoff syndrome. Confabulation is fluent false memory filling gaps with no intent to deceive.",27,7],[143,"How does semantic dementia differ from the amnesic syndrome?","Semantic dementia erodes word and object meaning with left anterior temporal atrophy, while recent events are still recalled.",27,8],[144,"What does Ribot's law state?","In retrograde amnesia recent memories are lost before remote ones, suggesting an organic cause.",27,9],[144,"Which measure best predicts outcome after head injury and how is it graded?","Post-traumatic amnesia: under 1 hour mild, 1 to 24 hours moderate, over 24 hours severe, over 7 days very severe.",27,10],[144,"What are the features of transient global amnesia?","Sudden loss of new learning, often after cold water or exertion, with repetitive questioning, preserved identity and recovery within 24 hours.",27,11],[144,"A 19-year-old student has no memory of several hours of heavy drinking though he talked normally. What is the mechanism?","An alcohol blackout: anterograde consolidation failure, so new traces are never stored.",27,12],[144,"What feature points to dissociative rather than organic amnesia?","Loss of personal identity with preserved new learning. Organic brain disease keeps identity until very late.",27,13],[144,"A 34-year-old woman loses her identity after stress and travels purposefully to another city. What is the ICD-11 diagnosis?","Dissociative amnesia with dissociative fugue, after ruling out epilepsy, intoxication and head injury.",27,14],[144,"What do the Rey-Osterrieth Complex Figure and the Rivermead Behavioural Memory Test assess?","The Rey figure tests visual memory and planning. The Rivermead uses everyday tasks to assess memory and plan rehabilitation.",27,15],[144,"What should be discussed about memory when consenting a patient for ECT?","The possibility of retrograde memory gaps, which usually shrink with recovery.",27,16],[145,"Who first reported patient H.M. and what did his case establish?","Scoville and Milner (1957). The hippocampus is needed for new declarative memories but not for skills, intellect or span.",27,17],[148,"What is meant by calling intelligence a hypothetical construct?","It is an idea inferred from behaviour rather than something directly observable or measurable.",28,1],[148,"What kind of definition is Boring's statement that intelligence is what the tests test?","An operational definition. It is circular and depends on which tests are chosen.",28,2],[148,"Why does construct validity matter when defining intelligence?","It shows that a test really measures the construct it claims to measure, not just test performance.",28,3],[148,"What is the positive manifold?","The finding that scores on all mental tests correlate positively with one another.",28,4],[148,"What is Spearman's two-factor theory?","Performance on any test reflects a general factor g plus a specific ability s for that test.",28,5],[148,"What statistical check did Spearman use to support g?","Tetrad differences.",28,6],[148,"Which modern score rests on the idea of g?","The full-scale IQ, used in diagnosing intellectual disability.",28,7],[148,"What is factor analysis?","A statistical method that reduces many correlated test scores to a smaller number of underlying latent factors.",28,8],[148,"A vocabulary test has a value of 0.8 on a verbal factor. What is this value called?","A factor loading: the correlation between one test and one factor. A high loading means a strong measure of that factor.",28,9],[149,"What is an eigenvalue in factor analysis?","The amount of variance across all tests explained by one factor, used to decide how many factors to keep.",28,10],[149,"What is a test's communality?","The proportion of its score explained by all shared factors together, the sum of its squared loadings.",28,11],[149,"What is the purpose of factor rotation?","It turns the factors so they are easier to interpret and name.",28,12],[149,"What did Thurstone propose and what method did he use?","About seven primary mental abilities, found using multiple factor analysis with rotation to simple structure in university students.",28,13],[149,"Name Thurstone's primary mental abilities.","Verbal comprehension, word fluency, number, space, memory, perceptual speed and reasoning.",28,14],[149,"What happened when Thurstone's factors were allowed to correlate?","They still correlated, giving back a second-order general factor, so most experts accept a hierarchy with g at the top.",28,15],[152,"Why was Binet's first intelligence test developed in 1905?","To identify children who needed extra help at school.",29,1],[152,"How is ratio IQ calculated and why does it fail in adults?","Mental age divided by chronological age, times 100. It fails in adults because mental age stops rising.",29,2],[152,"What is deviation IQ and what are its mean and standard deviation?","A score comparing a person with others of the same age, introduced by Wechsler. The mean is 100 and the SD is 15.",29,3],[152,"What proportions of people score 85 to 115 and 70 to 130?","About 68% score within 1 SD (85 to 115) and about 95% within 2 SD (70 to 130).",29,4],[152,"What does an IQ of 70 represent statistically?","Two standard deviations below the mean, roughly the lowest 2 to 3% of the population.",29,5],[152,"Which Wechsler test suits a 10-year-old boy, and what are the age ranges of the scales?","The WISC, for about 6 to 16. The WPPSI is for preschool children and the WAIS covers 16 to 90.",29,6],[152,"What are the four indices of the WAIS-IV?","Verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory and processing speed, combined into a full-scale IQ.",29,7],[152,"Which test suits a 30-year-old refugee with little English, and what does it measure?","Raven's Progressive Matrices, a non-verbal pattern test that is one of the purest measures of general fluid intelligence, though not culture-free.",29,8],[153,"What simple non-verbal task did Goodenough use to estimate a child's ability?","Scoring the child's drawing of a human figure.",29,9],[153,"A 72-year-old retired solicitor with suspected dementia needs premorbid IQ estimating. Which test is used and how does it work?","The National Adult Reading Test: reading aloud 50 irregular words such as yacht, which depends on prior knowledge preserved in early dementia.",29,10],[153,"When is the NART least valid?","When reading is damaged, such as surface dyslexia in semantic dementia, aphasia, poor schooling or English as a second language.",29,11],[153,"What is the practice effect and how can it be reduced?","Scores rise on retesting because the test is familiar. Parallel forms and longer gaps between tests help.",29,12],[153,"What is the Flynn effect and what is its practical consequence?","Average IQ scores rose about 3 points per decade last century, so test norms must be updated or old norms inflate scores.",29,13],[153,"What criteria are needed to diagnose intellectual disability?","IQ about 2 SD below the mean, significant deficits in adaptive behaviour, and onset in the developmental period. IQ alone is never enough.",29,14],[153,"What were the ICD-10 IQ bands for intellectual disability?","Mild 50 to 69, moderate 35 to 49, severe 20 to 34 and profound below 20.",29,15],[153,"How does ICD-11 grade intellectual disability and separate severe from profound?","By standard deviations below the mean: mild 2 to 3, moderate 3 to 4, severe and profound 4 or more. Severe and profound are separated by adaptive behaviour.",29,16],[154,"Which cognitive ability declines with age and is sensitive to brain injury?","Fluid ability, unlike crystallised knowledge, which is relatively preserved.",29,17],[157,"How did Spearman's view of intelligence differ from Thurstone's original view?","Spearman proposed a single general factor, g. Thurstone proposed about seven separate primary mental abilities.",30,1],[157,"When Thurstone's primary abilities were reanalysed, what was found and what did it lead to?","The primary abilities still correlated with one another. This led to acceptance of a second-order general factor above them, which Thurstone himself came to accept.",30,2],[157,"Describe the levels of Vernon's 1950 hierarchical model of intelligence.","g sits at the top, then two major group factors, v:ed and k:m. Below these come minor group factors and then specific factors.",30,3],[157,"What do Vernon's two major group factors, v:ed and k:m, stand for?","v:ed is verbal-educational ability. k:m is practical, mechanical and spatial ability.",30,4],[157,"Who split general intelligence into fluid and crystallised intelligence?","Raymond Cattell, working with John Horn, from 1963 onwards.",30,5],[157,"Define fluid intelligence (gf) and give an example.","Fluid intelligence is the ability to reason and solve new problems without relying on prior knowledge, such as solving an unfamiliar puzzle.",30,6],[157,"Define crystallised intelligence (gc) and give an example.","Crystallised intelligence is the store of knowledge and skills built up through learning and experience, such as vocabulary.",30,7],[157,"How do fluid and crystallised intelligence change across the adult lifespan?","Fluid intelligence peaks in early adulthood and then declines. Crystallised intelligence is maintained or may even grow into old age.",30,8],[157,"What does Cattell's 1971 investment theory propose about gf and gc?","People invest their fluid reasoning in learning opportunities from school and culture, which builds crystallised knowledge. So gf drives gc, not the reverse.",30,9],[157,"According to investment theory, when are fluid and crystallised intelligence most strongly correlated?","In childhood, when fluid ability is actively being invested to build crystallised knowledge.",30,10],[158,"Describe the three strata of Carroll's 1993 three-stratum theory of intelligence.","Stratum III is general intelligence, g. Stratum II holds about eight broad abilities. Stratum I holds many narrow, test-specific abilities such as memory span.",30,11],[158,"How was Carroll's three-stratum theory developed?","Carroll reanalysed hundreds of factor-analytic data sets from earlier studies of mental abilities.",30,12],[158,"What is the Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model, and why does it matter for IQ testing?","It merges Carroll's three-stratum theory with Cattell and Horn's gf-gc theory. Most modern IQ batteries, including Wechsler index scores, are interpreted using CHC broad abilities.",30,13],[158,"Name four broad (stratum II) abilities in the CHC model.","Examples include fluid reasoning, crystallised knowledge, short-term memory, visual processing, auditory processing, long-term retrieval and processing speed.",30,14],[158,"At which level of the CHC model does processing speed sit?","Stratum II, as one of the broad abilities, not stratum I or III.",30,15],[158,"Why can a test such as the NART estimate premorbid intelligence in early dementia?","Reading and vocabulary are crystallised skills that are relatively preserved early in dementia, so they reflect earlier ability.",30,16],[158,"A 72-year-old retired teacher struggles with new puzzles but keeps excellent vocabulary. Is this normal, and why?","This is consistent with normal ageing: fluid ability declines while crystallised knowledge is preserved. A broad fall in both would suggest illness.",30,17],[162,"What is inspection time and how does it relate to IQ?","Inspection time is the briefest masked exposure a person needs to judge which of two lines is longer. Shorter inspection times are moderately associated with higher IQ.",31,1],[162,"Why is inspection time considered a relatively pure measure of mental speed?","The stimulus is masked and no rapid motor response is needed, so it reflects speed of perceptual intake rather than movement speed.",31,2],[162,"What does Hick's law state about choice reaction time?","Reaction time increases as the number of possible choices increases. Jensen used this paradigm to study the link with IQ.",31,3],[162,"Which correlates more strongly with intelligence, simple or choice reaction time?","Choice reaction time correlates more strongly with intelligence than simple reaction time. Consistency of responding also matters, not just speed.",31,4],[162,"Describe a complex span task and what its scores predict.","The person does a processing task, such as sums, while remembering a word after each. Scores predict fluid reasoning much better than simple digit span.",31,5],[163,"How did Engle explain the strong link between working memory capacity and fluid intelligence?","Working memory capacity depends on executive attention, the ability to keep a goal in mind despite distraction, which is closely tied to fluid intelligence.",31,6],[163,"What did Kyllonen and Christal (1990) find about working memory and reasoning?","Working memory capacity and reasoning ability were very highly correlated, almost overlapping.",31,7],[163,"What are the three executive functions in Miyake's model, and which is most linked to IQ?","Inhibition, set shifting and updating of working memory. Friedman and colleagues found only updating was strongly linked to IQ.",31,8],[163,"What did the case of patient EVR (Eslinger and Damasio, 1985) show?","After frontal surgery his IQ stayed superior, yet his work and home life collapsed. A normal IQ can hide serious executive problems.",31,9],[163,"Which executive function do the Stroop test and the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test each assess?","The Stroop test assesses inhibition of an automatic response. Perseverative errors on Wisconsin card sorting indicate a set shifting deficit.",31,10],[163,"Name the four index scores of the WAIS-IV.","Verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory and processing speed.",31,11],[163,"Which WAIS-IV subtests make up the processing speed index?","Coding and Symbol Search, which are timed copying and scanning tasks.",31,12],[163,"A 54-year-old man after head injury is slow but accurate on Coding and Symbol Search. What is affected?","Processing speed. It is very sensitive to diffuse brain injury, ageing and psychiatric illness and can lower full-scale IQ even when reasoning is intact.",31,13],[163,"Why should clinicians look at the WAIS-IV index profile rather than only full-scale IQ?","A single index such as processing speed can drag down the total, and a normal total can hide specific deficits that affect daily life.",31,14],[163,"What do school leaving age natural experiments show about education and IQ?","Each extra year of schooling causally raises measured IQ by roughly 1 to 5 points (Ritchie and Tucker-Drob, 2018).",31,15],[163,"According to Eysenck's attentional control theory, how does anxiety affect test performance?","Anxiety reduces processing efficiency more than accuracy. Worry takes up attention, so anxious people may score the same but take longer and use more effort.",31,16],[163,"What aspect of cognition is mainly impaired by sleep loss?","Sustained attention is the main function harmed by sleep deprivation.",31,17],[167,"What are the three parts of Sternberg's 1985 triarchic theory of intelligence?","Analytical (componential), creative (experiential) and practical (contextual) intelligence.",32,1],[167,"What is analytical intelligence in Sternberg's theory, and which tests mainly measure it?","It is comparing, judging and evaluating, such as weighing up two treatments. Conventional IQ tests and exams mostly measure this part.",32,2],[167,"What is creative intelligence in Sternberg's theory? Give an example.","It is dealing with novelty by inventing and discovering, such as designing a new way to cool a server room or a new ward round tool.",32,3],[167,"What is practical intelligence in Sternberg's theory?","Using knowledge to adapt to, shape or select one's environment, often called street smarts. It is poorly captured by IQ tests.",32,4],[167,"What is tacit knowledge and how does it relate to practical intelligence?","Tacit knowledge is practical know-how that is rarely taught directly. Practical intelligence often rests on it, and Sternberg said it predicts job success beyond IQ.",32,5],[167,"A 35-year-old ward manager runs a busy ward well by applying experience. Which Sternberg component is this?","Practical (contextual) intelligence, applying knowledge to fit and shape the real-world setting.",32,6],[167,"List Gardner's original seven multiple intelligences.","Linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal and intrapersonal.",32,7],[167,"Which intelligence did Gardner add in 1999?","Naturalist intelligence was added as the eighth.",32,8],[167,"Is practical intelligence part of Gardner's list of multiple intelligences?","No. Practical intelligence belongs to Sternberg's triarchic theory, not Gardner's theory.",32,9],[167,"What kinds of evidence did Gardner use to identify separate intelligences?","Selective loss of one skill after brain damage, savants and prodigies, distinct developmental courses and evolutionary plausibility, rather than factor analysis.",32,10],[167,"What is the main criticism of Gardner's multiple intelligences theory?","When measured, the more cognitive intelligences correlate positively and load on g, and some, like musical and bodily skills, look more like talents.",32,11],[168,"Who first described emotional intelligence as an ability, and what are its four branches?","Salovey and Mayer (1990, revised 1997). The branches are perceiving, using, understanding and managing emotions.",32,12],[168,"How is emotional intelligence measured in the ability model?","By the MSCEIT, a performance test with answers scored right or wrong against expert or consensus norms, like an IQ test.",32,13],[168,"What is Goleman's model of emotional intelligence and how is it measured?","Goleman (1995) proposed a broad mixed model including self-awareness, self-control, motivation, empathy and social skills, measured by self-report.",32,14],[168,"What is the main weakness of Goleman's mixed model of emotional intelligence?","It overlaps heavily with personality traits such as emotional stability and extraversion, so adds little beyond existing measures.",32,15],[168,"Why do broader theories of intelligence matter when planning care for someone with a low IQ?","A person with a low IQ may still have real practical, social or creative strengths that should be used in care plans.",32,16],[171,"How do Western and many non-Western cultures differ in their ideas of intelligence?","Western tests stress fast, abstract reasoning. Many other cultures also value wisdom and social responsibility as part of intelligence.",33,1],[171,"What did Cole and colleagues find when Kpelle farmers in Liberia sorted objects?","They sorted by function and called this wise, but sorted into Western categories when asked how a fool would. Intelligent behaviour is defined by culture.",33,2],[171,"What did Sternberg and colleagues (2001) find in rural Kenyan children?","Children with the most knowledge of local herbal medicines scored lower on Western vocabulary tests, so culturally valued knowledge may not match academic scores.",33,3],[171,"Describe Serpell's 1979 study of Zambian and English children copying shapes.","English children did better with pencil and paper, Zambian children with wire, and both were equal with clay. Differences reflected familiarity with materials, not ability.",33,4],[172,"What did Carraher and colleagues (1985) show in Brazilian child street vendors?","The children calculated prices quickly and correctly at their stalls but made many errors on the same sums written on paper.",33,5],[172,"What is situated cognition, as shown by the coconut-selling children?","The idea that thinking skills are tied to the context in which they are learnt, so formal tests may underestimate real-world competence.",33,6],[172,"Name two tests designed to be culture fair.","Cattell's Culture Fair Intelligence Test, originally called Culture Free, and Raven's Progressive Matrices.",33,7],[172,"What is the main limitation of a non-verbal culture-fair matrices test?","It reduces but cannot remove cultural influence, because familiarity with abstract shapes, test formats and timing still varies. Such tests are culture-reduced, not culture-free.",33,8],[172,"What do Flynn effect gains on Raven's matrices show about culture-fair tests?","The largest Flynn gains were on these tests, showing they are highly sensitive to environment.",33,9],[172,"What is differential item functioning in an intelligence test?","When an item is harder for one group than another among people with the same overall ability. It indicates item bias, for example after translation.",33,10],[172,"What steps are needed when an intelligence test is used in a new language or culture?","The test should be adapted rather than simply translated, checked for item bias, and given local norms.",33,11],[172,"Describe Steele and Aronson's 1995 stereotype threat experiment.","Black students scored lower on a hard verbal test when it was called an ability test, but the gap disappeared when it was described as a puzzle exercise.",33,12],[172,"What is stereotype threat and how might it lower scores?","Fear of confirming a negative stereotype about one's group lowers performance, possibly through anxiety and reduced working memory. Larger studies suggest the effect is smaller than first thought.",33,13],[173,"A 30-year-old recent refugee with little schooling scores IQ 65 on UK norms. How should this be interpreted?","The score may underestimate her ability because test content and norms do not fit her background. Use interpreters, suitable tools, collateral history and adaptive functioning before diagnosing intellectual disability.",33,14],[173,"Why did Kpelle farmers' functional sorting not indicate a lack of abstract ability?","When asked to sort as a foolish person would, they produced taxonomic categories, showing ability was present but culturally valued differently.",33,15],[175,"What does the term heritability mean when applied to IQ?","The proportion of variation in a trait across a population that is linked to genetic differences. It is not the share of one person's IQ due to genes.",34,1],[175,"What is the usual estimate of adult IQ heritability from twin and adoption studies?","About 0.5, or 50%.",34,2],[175,"How does the heritability of IQ change with age, and what is this called?","It rises from about 20% in infancy to 60% or more in adulthood. This is the Wilson effect.",34,3],[175,"What happens to the influence of shared family environment on IQ as children grow up?","It falls to near zero by adulthood.",34,4],[175,"What best explains the rise in IQ heritability with age?","Active gene-environment correlation, or niche picking: people increasingly choose and shape environments that suit their genetic tendencies.",34,5],[175,"What did Turkheimer (2003) find about IQ heritability and family income?","Heritability was near zero in the poorest families and high in affluent families, because poverty limits everyone so genetic differences cannot show.",34,6],[175,"What did Duyme and colleagues (1999) find in French children adopted at 4 to 6 years?","Children with low IQ showed substantial IQ gains by adolescence, with bigger gains in higher socioeconomic adoptive homes.",34,7],[176,"What is the Flynn effect and how large is it?","The rise in average IQ test scores across generations in many countries, about 3 points per decade during the twentieth century.",34,8],[176,"On which tests were Flynn effect gains largest and smallest, and what does this imply?","Largest on fluid reasoning tests such as matrices and smallest on vocabulary. The rise was too fast to be genetic, so causes are environmental.",34,9],[176,"What are the likely causes of the Flynn effect?","Better nutrition and health, more schooling, smaller families and more abstract ways of thinking.",34,10],[176,"Why is the Flynn effect clinically important when assessing intellectual disability?","Tests must be re-normed, because old norms inflate scores and can hide intellectual disability. Always use current norms.",34,11],[176,"How does phenylketonuria illustrate that nature versus nurture is a false split?","PKU is strongly genetic, yet the intellectual disability is prevented by an environmental change, a low-phenylalanine diet. Genetic does not mean fixed.",34,12],[176,"What is missing heritability in relation to intelligence?","The gap between twin-based heritability of about 0.5 and the much smaller variance explained by measured common variants and polygenic scores.",34,13],[176,"Give possible explanations for missing heritability of IQ.","Rare variants not captured on genotyping arrays, gene-gene and gene-environment interactions, and assumptions built into twin models.",34,14],[176,"How useful are polygenic scores for predicting an individual's IQ?","They predict group trends but are weak predictors for a single person, as IQ involves thousands of variants of tiny effect.",34,15],[176,"What is genetic nurture, as shown by Kong (2018)?","Parental alleles not passed on to the child still predicted the child's educational attainment, showing parents' genes act through the home environment.",34,16],[176,"How does Lewontin's seed analogy show the limits of heritability?","Seeds in poor and rich soil differ within each tray due to genes, but the gap between trays is entirely due to soil. Within-group heritability says nothing about between-group differences.",34,17],[176,"What factors are associated with average IQ differences between groups?","Schooling, health, poverty and testing bias. The Flynn effect shows how fast environment can shift scores.",34,18],[180,"What does the classical view of concepts propose, and what is its main problem?","Each concept has a strict list of defining features shared by all members. It cannot explain why some members, like robins, seem more typical than others, like penguins.",35,1],[180,"What is prototype theory of concepts?","Rosch (1975) proposed that categories are organised around a best example, with members varying in how typical they are.",35,2],[180,"What is Rosch's basic level of categorisation? Give an example.","The preferred middle level, such as dog rather than animal or Labrador. It is named fastest, learnt first by children, and its members share shape and use.",35,3],[180,"What does the finding that 3 is rated a better odd number than 447 suggest about concepts?","Concepts may have a defining core plus a quick identification procedure based on typicality, even when membership is clearly defined.",35,4],[180,"What is the theory-based (essentialist) view of concepts, and what supports it?","Concepts rest on beliefs about hidden essences (Murphy and Medin). Keil found older children judged a raccoon altered to look like a skunk was still a raccoon.",35,5],[180,"What is the difference between a heuristic and an algorithm?","A heuristic is a quick rule of thumb that usually works but can fail. An algorithm is a step-by-step method that guarantees a correct solution.",35,6],[180,"What is the availability heuristic? Give an example.","Judging how common something is by how easily examples come to mind, such as feeling flying is more dangerous after news of a plane crash.",35,7],[181,"What is the representativeness heuristic and its key error?","Judging probability by resemblance to a stereotype. It typically ignores base rates, such as guessing librarian when most people in the group are engineers.",35,8],[181,"What is the anchoring and adjustment heuristic in judgement?","Numerical estimates are pulled towards an initial value, and people fail to adjust enough away from it.",35,9],[181,"Contrast System 1 and System 2 in Kahneman's dual process theory.","System 1 is fast, automatic and intuitive. System 2 is slow, effortful, deliberate and capacity limited, and often fails to check System 1.",35,10],[181,"What does the bat and ball problem show, and what test is it from?","The quick answer of 10p is a System 1 error not checked by System 2; the correct answer is 5p. It is from Frederick's Cognitive Reflection Test.",35,11],[181,"What is belief bias in syllogistic reasoning?","Judging an argument by how believable its conclusion is rather than its logic, so invalid but believable arguments are accepted and valid unbelievable ones rejected.",35,12],[181,"What is diagnostic momentum as a source of clinical error?","A diagnostic label that becomes more fixed as it is passed between clinicians, without being re-examined.",35,13],[181,"How can clinicians reduce heuristic-driven diagnostic errors?","Slow down, check base rates and ask what else it could be, which engages System 2 thinking.",35,14],[182,"Who described belief bias, and what makes it stronger or weaker?","Evans (1983). Time pressure increases it, while instructions to reason strictly logically reduce it, supporting dual process theory.",35,15],[184,"What three executive functions did Miyake (2000) identify by factor analysis?","Updating of working memory, shifting between mental sets (cognitive flexibility), and inhibition of a strong habitual response.",36,1],[184,"What is meant by unity and diversity in Miyake's model?","The three core executive functions correlate with each other (unity) but are also separable (diversity).",36,2],[184,"Distinguish core (low-level) from higher-level executive functions, as described by Diamond.","Core functions are inhibition, shifting and updating. Higher-level functions built on them include initiation, planning, multitasking, reasoning and problem solving.",36,3],[184,"What is the supervisory attentional system in Norman and Shallice's model?","A system that takes over when novel action is needed. Routine actions are handled by contention scheduling, which selects habits in response to cues.",36,4],[184,"What happens when the supervisory attentional system fails? Give an example.","Habit takes over, so you might drive to work on a Saturday when you meant to go to the shop.",36,5],[184,"What is utilisation behaviour and when is it seen?","Seen with frontal damage, the patient uses objects in reach automatically, such as putting on a second pair of glasses unasked (Lhermitte, 1983).",36,6],[185,"What does the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test assess and what is a perseverative error?","It assesses set shifting. A perseverative error is continuing to sort by the old rule after it has changed without warning.",36,7],[185,"Which brain region did Milner (1963) link with perseverative errors on the Wisconsin test?","The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex.",36,8],[185,"Which executive function does the Tower of London test assess?","Planning, by moving beads on pegs to a target arrangement in the fewest moves, planned in advance (Shallice, 1982).",36,9],[185,"What does the Hayling test assess and what indicates failure?","Response inhibition. Failure is giving the obvious word to complete a sentence instead of an unrelated one.",36,10],[185,"What does the Six Elements Test assess, and why is it useful?","Multitasking and organisation. It detects frontal patients who pass standard tests but cannot organise daily life.",36,11],[185,"What were the Goldstein-Scheerer sorting tests used to assess?","Abstract thinking, by asking people to sort objects by colour and then by form.",36,12],[185,"Describe the three frontal syndromes linked to circuits by Cummings (1993).","Dorsolateral damage gives slow thinking, poor planning and poor set shifting with intact personality. Orbitofrontal damage gives disinhibition. Medial frontal damage gives apathy.",36,13],[185,"Who named the dysexecutive syndrome seen after frontal damage?","Baddeley and Wilson.",36,14],[185,"Distinguish deductive from inductive reasoning with a clinical example.","Deduction goes from general rules to a certain conclusion, as with sertraline being an SSRI. Induction goes from cases to a probable rule, such as clozapine causing drooling.",36,15],[185,"In Wason's selection task with cards A, K, 4 and 7, which cards must be turned and why?","A and 7, because only these could falsify the rule. Most people wrongly choose A and 4, seeking confirmation.",36,16],[185,"What improves performance on the Wason selection task?","Framing the same logic as a familiar social rule, such as a drinking age rule.",36,17],[185,"Why do executive deficits matter clinically even when IQ is normal?","They are common after head injury, stroke and in schizophrenia, and predict daily functioning better than IQ. Normal office tests do not exclude them.",36,18],[188,"Ward managers asked first about 90 days give higher length-of-stay estimates than those asked about 10 days. Which bias?","Anchoring. People start from the initial number and adjust too little; even a random number can shift estimates.",37,1],[188,"Who proposed cognitive dissonance theory, and when?","Leon Festinger, in 1957.",37,2],[188,"What produces cognitive dissonance, and what is its main effect?","Holding two inconsistent cognitions at the same time. It creates an unpleasant tension that motivates people to reduce it.",37,3],[188,"What is the commonest way to reduce dissonance? Give an example.","Adding a justifying cognition, such as a smoker with emphysema saying his uncle smoked and lived to ninety-two.",37,4],[188,"Other than adding a cognition, how else can people reduce dissonance?","By changing the attitude, changing the behaviour (hardest), playing down the importance of the clash, or avoiding information that threatens it.",37,5],[188,"A 40-year-old woman shouts at her mother then thinks she loves her mother more than anyone. Why does guilt increase?","This thought highlights the clash with her behaviour, so it increases dissonance rather than reducing it.",37,6],[189,"In Festinger and Carlsmith's 1959 experiment, which group rated the dull task as more interesting, and why?","The one-dollar group. Their payment was insufficient justification for lying, so they changed their attitude to the task.",37,7],[189,"What did Aronson and Mills (1959) show?","Effort justification: students who went through an embarrassing initiation to join a group rated its dull discussion more highly.",37,8],[189,"What is spreading of alternatives, as shown by Brehm (1956)?","After choosing between two similar items, people rate the chosen one more highly and the rejected one lower.",37,9],[189,"How did cult members in When Prophecy Fails respond when the predicted flood did not come?","They recruited more actively, because gaining new believers supported their belief. This is belief disconfirmation.",37,10],[189,"According to Cooper and Fazio, when is dissonance greatest?","With free choice, little external reward, public commitment and foreseeable negative consequences.",37,11],[189,"What is the gambler's fallacy and which heuristic underlies it?","Believing a result is due after a run of the opposite, though each event is independent. It stems from the representativeness heuristic.",37,12],[189,"What is confirmation bias and which task demonstrated it?","Seeking or favouring only evidence that supports an existing belief. Wason's 2-4-6 task demonstrated it.",37,13],[189,"Distinguish recency bias from the primacy effect.","Recency bias overweights the last item heard. In primacy the first item dominates.",37,14],[189,"What is the sunk cost fallacy?","Continuing with something because of past investment rather than expected future benefit (Arkes and Blumer).",37,15],[189,"Define arbitrary inference and selective abstraction in Beck's cognitive model of depression.","Arbitrary inference is reaching a conclusion without evidence or despite contrary evidence. Selective abstraction focuses on one detail while ignoring the wider context.",37,16],[189,"Define personalisation and dichotomous thinking as Beck's cognitive errors.","Personalisation is blaming oneself for external events. Dichotomous thinking is seeing things in all-or-nothing terms.",37,17],[189,"Is the domino effect one of Beck's formal cognitive distortions?","No. It is an informal phrase for gloom spreading from one area of life to others.",37,18],[189,"How does motivational interviewing use cognitive dissonance?","The clinician helps the person see a gap between their values and behaviour, such as drinking, so they resolve the dissonance by changing behaviour.",37,19],[190,"A 28-year-old depressed woman fails her driving test once and says she fails at everything. Which distortion?","Overgeneralisation, drawing a global rule from a single event. Words like always, never and everything are clues.",37,20],[192,"What is prospect theory and who proposed it?","Kahneman and Tversky (1979) proposed that people value outcomes as gains or losses from a reference point rather than in terms of final wealth.",38,1],[192,"What is loss aversion and roughly how large is it?","Losses feel more painful than equal gains feel pleasant, roughly twice as strongly.",38,2],[192,"A 45-year-old man refuses a coin toss to win £110 or lose £100. What explains this?","Loss aversion: the possible loss looms larger than the slightly bigger gain, despite the positive expected value.",38,3],[192,"Describe the shape of the prospect theory value function.","Steeper for losses than for gains. It is concave for gains, making people risk averse, and convex for losses, making them risk seeking.",38,4],[192,"How do gain and loss frames affect risk taking?","Gain frames make people risk averse, choosing the sure option. Loss frames make them risk seeking.",38,5],[192,"What is the framing effect, as shown in the Asian disease problem?","The same information leads to different choices depending on wording; people chose the safe option when framed as lives saved and the risky one when framed as lives lost.",38,6],[192,"What did McNeil and colleagues (1982) show about framing in doctors?","Doctors chose surgery for lung cancer more often when outcomes were described as 90% survival than as 10% mortality.",38,7],[192,"What is attribute framing? Give an example.","A single attribute is described positively or negatively, such as beef labelled 75% lean being rated better than 25% fat (Levin and Gaeth).",38,8],[193,"Who described groupthink, and how is it defined?","Irving Janis (1972). A cohesive group's desire for agreement overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives.",38,9],[193,"What are the antecedent conditions of groupthink?","A highly cohesive group, isolation from outsiders, a directive leader and high stress.",38,10],[193,"List the main symptoms of groupthink.","Self-censorship, an illusion of unanimity, direct pressure on dissenters and self-appointed mindguards who block outside views.",38,11],[193,"How can groupthink be prevented in a clinical team?","The leader withholds their view at first, a devil's advocate is appointed, outside experts are invited and a second-chance meeting is held before acting.",38,12],[193,"Describe James Reason's Swiss cheese model of error.","Each safety defence is a slice with holes. Harm happens when holes in several layers line up.",38,13],[193,"Distinguish active failures from latent conditions in Reason's model.","Active failures are unsafe acts by front-line staff, such as a wrong lithium dose. Latent conditions are hidden system weaknesses, such as understaffing or look-alike packaging.",38,14],[193,"What did Markus and Kitayama (1991) and Hofstede (1980) contribute to understanding culture and decisions?","Markus and Kitayama contrasted independent and interdependent selves. Hofstede identified individualism versus collectivism as a key cultural dimension.",38,15],[193,"How should risks be presented when obtaining consent to reduce framing effects?","Give risks in both frames, such as the chance of survival and the chance of death, so wording does not nudge the patient.",38,16],[194,"A 34-year-old woman with capacity wants her elders to share her treatment decision. How should this be viewed?","As an interdependent, collectivist model of the self. It is an autonomous choice, not a lack of capacity; check her own wishes.",38,17],[197,"What is a problem space, according to Newell and Simon (1972)?","A representation of a problem made up of an initial state, a goal state and operators (moves) that change one state into another.",39,1],[197,"What is means-end analysis in Newell and Simon's account of problem solving?","A heuristic of identifying the biggest difference between current and goal states and setting a subgoal to reduce it, building a hierarchy of subgoals.",39,2],[197,"In the Tower of Hanoi, what is the first subgoal using means-end analysis?","To free the largest disc so it can be moved to its goal peg.",39,3],[197,"What computer program did Newell and Simon build using means-end analysis?","The General Problem Solver.",39,4],[197,"What did Köhler's 1925 studies of chimpanzees on Tenerife demonstrate?","Insight learning: after a pause a chimp suddenly stacked boxes to reach bananas, solving the problem by seeing it in a new way.",39,5],[197,"How does insight learning differ from Thorndike's trial and error learning?","Insight is a sudden solution after restructuring the problem. Thorndike's cats improved gradually through trial and error.",39,6],[198,"What is functional fixedness, and which experiment demonstrated it?","Seeing an object only in its usual function. In Duncker's 1945 candle problem, people failed to use the pin box as a shelf.",39,7],[198,"What change made Duncker's candle problem easier to solve?","Presenting the pins outside the box, so the box was no longer seen only as a container.",39,8],[198,"What is mental set (the Einstellung effect), and who demonstrated it?","Persisting with a previously successful method when a simpler one exists. Luchins (1942) showed it with water jar problems.",39,9],[198,"How does mental set differ from functional fixedness?","Mental set concerns problem-solving methods or procedures. Functional fixedness concerns the uses of objects.",39,10],[198,"What is analogical transfer, and what did Gick and Holyoak (1980) find?","Solving a new problem using the structure of a known one. Few people used the fortress story to solve Duncker's tumour problem until given a hint.",39,11],[198,"Why do people often fail to transfer analogies spontaneously?","They attend to surface features rather than deep structure, unless the link is pointed out.",39,12],[198,"What are Fitts and Posner's (1967) three stages of skill learning?","Cognitive, where each step is thought about; associative, which is smoother with fewer errors; and autonomous, where the skill runs largely by itself.",39,13],[198,"How did Shiffrin and Schneider contrast controlled and automatic processing?","Controlled processing is slow, serial and effortful. Automatic processing is fast, parallel, effortless and hard to change once learned.",39,14],[198,"What is hierarchical organisation in skill learning?","Small sub-skills become grouped into larger units that run as a whole.",39,15],[198,"A 19-year-old who once stalled when talked to now drives chatting and cannot recall the journey. What has developed?","Automaticity: the skill has reached the autonomous stage and no longer needs conscious control.",39,16],[198,"What is a capture error in Reason's error classification?","A slip where a strong habit takes over, such as driving the usual route to work when intending to go elsewhere.",39,17],[198,"Distinguish slips and lapses, mistakes and violations in Reason's classification.","Slips and lapses are failures of execution. Mistakes are failures of planning. Violations are deliberate departures from rules.",39,18],[198,"How can capture slips and mental set be reduced in clinical practice?","Checklists and forcing functions reduce prescribing slips, and a fresh look or second opinion helps when a patient is not improving.",39,19],[201,"What did Guilford mean by convergent thinking, and which type of test mostly rewards it?","Convergent thinking homes in on the single correct answer to a problem. Standard IQ tests mostly reward this kind of thinking.",40,1],[201,"What is divergent thinking and which classic task is used to measure it?","Divergent thinking generates many different answers to an open-ended problem and is linked to creativity. A classic task asks people to list all the possible uses of a brick.",40,2],[201,"Name the four ways in which answers on divergent thinking tests are scored.","Fluency (number of ideas), flexibility (number of different categories), originality (how unusual the ideas are) and elaboration (amount of detail).",40,3],[201,"What is the difference between fluency and flexibility in scoring divergent thinking?","Fluency is the total count of ideas produced. Flexibility is the number of distinct categories those ideas fall into, such as building, weapons and art.",40,4],[201,"Whose creativity tests were built on divergent thinking scores, and how well do they predict real creative achievement?","Torrance based his tests on fluency, flexibility, originality and elaboration. Scores predict real-world creative success only modestly.",40,5],[201,"List Wallas's four stages of the creative process in order.","Preparation, incubation, illumination and verification. Wallas (1926) based them on accounts from creative thinkers such as Poincaré.",40,6],[201,"A 35-year-old novelist abandons a stuck plot, goes gardening, and suddenly sees the answer. Which Wallas stage was the gardening?","Incubation, the period when the problem is set aside. The sudden answer is illumination, and testing it in a draft is verification.",40,7],[201,"In Wallas's model, what is intimation and how strong is the evidence for incubation?","Intimation is the feeling that a solution is about to arrive, part of illumination. Later experiments support an incubation effect, though its mechanism is still debated.",40,8],[201,"How did Mednick explain creativity in his associative theory?","Mednick (1962) saw creativity as forming new, useful combinations of remote ideas. Creative people have a flat associative hierarchy that lets them reach distant associations.",40,9],[201,"What is a steep associative hierarchy and who is said to have one?","A steep hierarchy has a few strong, obvious associations to a stimulus. Mednick said less creative people have this pattern.",40,10],[201,"What does the Remote Associates Test involve, and what is the answer to 'cottage, Swiss, cake'?","It asks for one word that links three apparently unrelated words, testing associative reach. The answer here is cheese.",40,11],[202,"What did Koestler mean by bisociation?","Bisociation is the sudden linking of two frames of reference that had seemed unconnected. Koestler said it produces laughter in jokes, discovery in science and insight in art.",40,12],[202,"What did Amabile find when students made collages expecting evaluation or a reward?","They produced less creative work than students who did it for enjoyment. Expected reward and evaluation reduce creativity by undermining intrinsic motivation.",40,13],[202,"What three components does Amabile's componential model of creativity require?","Domain-relevant skills, creativity-relevant thinking habits and intrinsic motivation, meaning doing the task for its own sake.",40,14],[202,"Describe Csikszentmihalyi's systems model of creativity.","Creativity arises from interaction between the individual, the domain (the rules of an art or science) and the field (the experts who judge what counts as creative).",40,15],[202,"What is Sternberg and Lubart's investment theory of creativity?","Creators 'buy low and sell high': they adopt unfashionable ideas, persuade others of their value, then move on. It needs six resources working together, and strength in one can partly compensate for weakness in another.",40,16],[202,"How did Winnicott link play with creativity?","Winnicott (1971) said only in play is a person free to be creative. Play occurs in a potential or transitional space between inner and outer reality, and therapy should restore the capacity to play.",40,17],[202,"In Winnicott's theory, what is a transitional object and why does it matter for creativity?","An object such as a baby's teddy, standing between inner and outer reality. It is the infant's first creative use of the outside world.",40,18],[206,"What is a phoneme? Give an English example.","The smallest unit of sound that can change a word's meaning. In English /r/ and /l/ are separate phonemes because 'rice' and 'lice' differ in meaning.",41,1],[206,"Why do adult Japanese speakers often struggle to hear the difference between 'rice' and 'lice'?","In Japanese /r/ and /l/ are not separate phonemes. Adults find it hard to hear distinctions their own language does not use to change meaning.",41,2],[206,"What are allophones?","Slightly different sounds that a language treats as one phoneme, so swapping them never changes meaning.",41,3],[206,"The aspirated 'p' in 'pin' and unaspirated 'p' in 'spin' are allophones in English. What are they in Hindi, and why?","They are two separate phonemes in Hindi, because there the difference changes word meaning. Whether sounds are phonemes or allophones depends on the language.",41,4],[206,"What is categorical perception of speech, as shown by Liberman and colleagues?","Listeners hear a smooth acoustic continuum, such as ba to da to ga, as distinct categories. They barely tell apart steps within a category but easily detect steps that cross the boundary.",41,5],[206,"Which acoustic cue distinguishes 'ba' from 'pa' in categorical perception studies?","Voice onset time, the delay between releasing the consonant and the voice starting.",41,6],[206,"What did Eimas show about categorical perception in infants?","Eimas (1971) found that babies aged 1 and 4 months already perceive speech sounds categorically, suggesting an early or innate basis.",41,7],[206,"What did Werker and Tees show about infants' ability to hear foreign speech contrasts?","Young babies can discriminate contrasts from all languages, but by about the end of the first year they tune in to their own language and lose sensitivity to others.",41,8],[207,"What is a morpheme, and how many does 'unkindness' contain?","The smallest unit of meaning in a language. 'Unkindness' has three: un-, kind and -ness.",41,9],[207,"What is the difference between a free and a bound morpheme?","A free morpheme, such as 'kind', can stand alone as a word. A bound morpheme, such as 'un-' or '-ness', must attach to another morpheme.",41,10],[207,"How do derivational morphemes differ from inflectional morphemes?","Derivational morphemes change meaning or word class, as in kind to kindness. Inflectional morphemes mark grammar such as tense or number, as in '-ed' or '-s'.",41,11],[207,"What do syntax, semantics and pragmatics each refer to?","Syntax is the rules for ordering words, semantics is meaning, and pragmatics is how context shapes meaning in use.",41,12],[207,"What did Chomsky show with 'Colourless green ideas sleep furiously'?","That syntax and semantics are separate: the sentence is grammatically correct but has no sensible meaning.",41,13],[207,"What is the difference between deep structure and surface structure in Chomsky's theory?","Deep structure is a sentence's underlying meaning; surface structure is the actual words spoken. Transformational rules link the two.",41,14],[207,"How do active and passive sentences, and ambiguous sentences, illustrate deep and surface structure?","Active and passive forms of a sentence share one deep structure but differ in surface structure. An ambiguous sentence like 'Visiting relatives can be boring' has one surface structure but two deep structures.",41,15],[207,"A 3-year-old girl says 'The purple giraffe ate my spaceship', never heard before. Which design feature of language is this?","Productivity, also called generativity: using a finite set of words and rules to create an infinite number of new sentences. Animal communication mostly lacks it.",41,16],[207,"Apart from productivity, name three other design features of human language listed by Hockett.","Displacement (referring to things not present), arbitrariness and duality of patterning.",41,17],[207,"How do the building blocks of language map onto clinical signs in aphasia and schizophrenia?","Phonemic paraphasias swap sounds, semantic paraphasias swap meanings and agrammatism is a syntax problem. In schizophrenia speech can be grammatical yet empty of meaning.",41,18],[210,"What did Seyfarth, Cheney and Marler find when they played vervet monkey alarm calls?","Different calls produced distinct responses even without a predator: 'leopard' sent monkeys up trees, 'eagle' made them look up and 'snake' made them look down.",42,1],[210,"Which of Hockett's design features do vervet alarm calls show, and which do they lack?","They show semanticity because the calls refer to specific things. They lack displacement and productivity.",42,2],[210,"Which design feature of language do honeybee dances show, and what is their limitation?","They show displacement, signalling food that is not present. However, they use a fixed code and lack productivity.",42,3],[210,"Which chimpanzee did the Gardners teach, and what did the project show?","Washoe, who learned a substantial vocabulary of American Sign Language signs. It showed apes can learn many symbols.",42,4],[210,"What did Terrace conclude after analysing films of Nim Chimpsky signing?","Nim's sign combinations lacked grammatical structure, were often imitations of his trainers and did not grow longer or more complex.",42,5],[210,"How did the bonobo Kanzi learn to use lexigrams?","Kanzi learned the lexigram keyboard simply by watching his mother being trained, not through direct teaching.",42,6],[210,"How well did Kanzi understand spoken English, and what was the limit of his own output?","He understood many novel spoken sentences about as well as a two-year-old child. His own messages remained short.",42,7],[210,"What is the current consensus on whether apes can acquire language?","Most experts agree apes can learn symbols and vocabulary but not human grammar or productive syntax.",42,8],[211,"What did Bruner propose in response to Chomsky's language acquisition device?","Bruner (1983) argued that innate ability needs a language acquisition support system from adults to develop.",42,9],[211,"How do shared routines support language learning according to Bruner?","Familiar routines such as peek-a-boo and joint picture-book reading give a predictable, safe structure in which children learn words.",42,10],[211,"What is joint attention?","Two people attending to the same object while each knows the other is attending to it.",42,11],[211,"An 18-month-old boy links a new word to the toy an adult is looking at, not his own. Whose study and what skill?","Baldwin (1991). It shows joint attention: following the speaker's gaze to work out what a word refers to.",42,12],[211,"What is the clinical significance of poor joint attention in a toddler?","Reduced joint attention is an early sign of autism and is assessed in tools such as the ADOS.",42,13],[211,"What are the features of infant-directed speech (motherese)?","Higher pitch, exaggerated intonation, slower rate, short simple sentences and repetition.",42,14],[211,"What did Fernald find about infants' response to infant-directed speech?","Fernald (1985) found 4-month-olds prefer infant-directed speech to adult-directed speech. It may help them detect word boundaries and emotion.",42,15],[211,"Describe Kuhl's study of American 9-month-olds exposed to Mandarin.","Infants who had 12 play sessions with a live Mandarin speaker kept the ability to discriminate a Mandarin sound contrast. Those given the same input by video or audio recording did not.",42,16],[211,"What is Kuhl's social gating hypothesis and its practical implication?","Live social interaction focuses infant attention and enables early speech sound learning. It supports advice that young children learn language from real people rather than screens.",42,17],[214,"What does the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis propose?","That the structure of a person's language shapes how they think. Sapir and his student Whorf put it forward; Whorf's writings were collected in 1956.",43,1],[214,"What is linguistic determinism, and what is its current status?","The strong form of Sapir-Whorf, saying language sets the limits of thought, so no word means no concept. It is now largely rejected.",43,2],[214,"What is linguistic relativity, and how well supported is it?","The weak form of Sapir-Whorf, saying language influences perception, memory and habits of thought. It has good support from studies of colour, number, space and time.",43,3],[214,"Why is the 'many words for snow' claim poor evidence for Sapir-Whorf?","The story that some languages have many snow words is itself exaggerated, so it supports neither form.",43,4],[214,"What did Berlin and Kay find about basic colour terms across languages?","Basic colour words appear in a fixed order across languages: black and white first, then red, then green or yellow, then blue. This suggests universal roots for colour perception.",43,5],[214,"What did Rosch Heider find in the Dani of New Guinea, and what did it argue against?","Though the Dani have only two basic colour terms, they remembered focal colours better than non-focal ones, like English speakers. This argues against strong linguistic determinism.",43,6],[214,"What did Gordon find about number skills in the Pirahã of the Amazon?","With no words for exact numbers, they matched very small sets accurately but became approximate with larger ones. This supports weak relativity, as number words help represent exact large quantities.",43,7],[215,"What did Winawer and colleagues find in Russian speakers distinguishing shades of blue?","Russian has separate basic words for light and dark blue, and speakers were faster at telling blues apart across that boundary.",43,8],[215,"What happened to the Russian blues advantage under verbal interference, and what does that show?","The advantage disappeared during a simultaneous verbal task. This shows language can influence perceptual discrimination in real time.",43,9],[215,"What did Levinson show with Guugu Yimithirr speakers?","They use compass directions rather than left and right. After being turned round they rebuilt a row of toys by compass direction, while Dutch speakers kept left-right order.",43,10],[215,"Does Levinson's Guugu Yimithirr study support linguistic determinism or relativity?","Relativity. Language shaped habits of spatial thinking, not the limits of what can be thought.",43,11],[215,"What is Bernstein's restricted code?","Short, simple, context-dependent speech that relies on shared assumptions and focuses on the here and now.",43,12],[215,"What is Bernstein's elaborated code, and what criticism did his theory face?","Explicit speech that spells out meaning and does not depend on context; Bernstein said schools reward it. Critics said his work wrongly implied a deficit in restricted-code speakers.",43,13],[215,"What did Harris and colleagues find in Turkish-English bilinguals raised in Turkey?","Skin conductance rose more to childhood reprimands in Turkish than in English. Emotion is felt most strongly in the language in which it was first learned.",43,14],[215,"A 40-year-old Polish woman in a UK clinic finds trauma easier to discuss in English. Why might this be?","A second language can feel more emotionally distant, while emotion is often expressed more fully in the first language. Use a professional interpreter where needed.",43,15],[216,"What did Frank and colleagues add to the Pirahã number findings?","The Pirahã could match one to one when the array stayed visible but struggled when memory was needed, suggesting number words act as a cognitive tool.",43,16],[218,"At what age does cooing begin, and what is it?","At about 6 to 8 weeks, around the time of the social smile. Cooing is soft vowel sounds such as 'ooh'.",44,1],[218,"When does babbling start and how does it change by 9 months?","Babbling ('ba-ba-ba') starts at about 6 months. By about 9 months it becomes long, tuneful strings that rise and fall like conversation.",44,2],[218,"At what age does a child typically say a first meaningful word?","At about 12 months, when words like 'mama' or 'dada' are used for the right person.",44,3],[218,"What is a holophrase?","A single word used to express a whole idea, such as 'milk!' meaning 'I want milk'.",44,4],[218,"What is the vocabulary (naming) explosion and when does it occur?","A sharp rise in the rate of learning new words. It follows once a toddler has about 50 words, usually from around 18 months.",44,5],[218,"What is telegraphic speech and at what age is it typical?","Short utterances of content words without grammatical words, such as 'Daddy gone' or 'more juice'. It runs from about 18 to 30 months.",44,6],[218,"What are the expected language milestones at 24 and 36 months?","By 24 months a child combines two or three words and knows over 200 words. By 36 months sentences include small grammatical words such as 'the', 'can' and 'will'.",44,7],[218,"A 4-year-old boy says 'I goed to the park'. What is this error and what does it show?","Overregularisation, a normal error. It shows the child has learned a grammatical rule and is applying it too widely.",44,8],[218,"What is overextension in language development?","Using a word for a wider category than adults do, such as calling every man 'Daddy'. It is a normal error.",44,9],[219,"A 7-month-old girl says 'dada' while babbling. Does this count as her first word?","No. Babbled 'dada' at 6 to 9 months is not a word; it counts only when used specifically for the right person, at about 12 months.",44,10],[219,"Which factors are associated with slower language development, and what must always be checked?","Male sex, twins, later birth order in large families, deafness and neglect. Hearing must always be checked when speech is delayed.",44,11],[219,"What did Hart and Risley find about home environment and vocabulary?","The amount of talk children hear at home strongly predicts their vocabulary.",44,12],[219,"What is Chomsky's poverty of the stimulus argument?","The speech children hear is too limited to explain how quickly they acquire grammar, so there must be an innate language acquisition device and universal grammar.",44,13],[219,"What did Lenneberg propose, and what does the case of Genie suggest?","Lenneberg (1967) proposed a critical period for language ending near puberty. Genie, found at 13 after severe isolation, learned many words but never normal grammar, though her abuse complicates interpretation.",44,14],[219,"What did Johnson and Newport find about age of arrival and grammar in immigrants?","Grammar was native-like if they arrived before about 7 years, then fell steadily up to puberty, with lower and more varied scores in adult arrivals. This fits a gradual sensitive period.",44,15],[219,"What do studies of sign language and left hemisphere stroke show?","Sign languages are full natural languages using left hemisphere language areas. Poizner, Klima and Bellugi showed left-sided strokes cause sign aphasia while gesture and drawing survive.",44,16],[219,"What does the emergence of Nicaraguan Sign Language show?","Younger cohorts made the new sign language more systematic and grammatical than the older signers who began it, supporting the role of early exposure in building grammar.",44,17],[219,"A mother of a 2-year-old bilingual boy fears code-mixing means delay. What should you advise?","Bilingualism does not cause language delay and code-mixing is normal and rule-governed. Total vocabulary matches monolingual peers, so she should continue both languages.",44,18],[220,"At about what age is speech fully intelligible to strangers and adult-like?","By about 60 months (5 years), when children tell stories and explain ideas much like adults.",44,19],[222,"What was Piaget's view of the relationship between thought and language?","Thought develops first and language simply reflects it. Cognitive development precedes and drives language.",45,1],[222,"How did Piaget interpret young children talking aloud to themselves?","As egocentric speech, reflecting the child's egocentrism, with no social or useful function and fading as the child grows.",45,2],[222,"What did Sinclair-de-Zwart do and find, and whose view does it support?","She taught non-conserving children the words conservers use, such as 'more' and 'longer'. Most still failed conservation tasks, supporting Piaget's view that cognition drives language.",45,3],[222,"What is conservation in Piaget's terms?","Understanding that an amount stays the same when its shape or appearance changes.",45,4],[222,"How did Vygotsky describe the origins of thought and speech?","Thought and speech have separate roots and merge in early childhood. Speech begins as social talk, becomes private speech and finally becomes silent inner speech.",45,5],[222,"What is private speech according to Vygotsky?","Talking aloud to oneself to guide and regulate action, such as 'big one at the bottom'. It is later internalised as inner speech.",45,6],[222,"A 4-year-old girl talks aloud while doing a jigsaw. How would Vygotsky interpret this?","As private speech that helps her plan and solve the problem, which will later become inner speech.",45,7],[222,"Which finding about task difficulty supports Vygotsky over Piaget?","Children use more private speech when tasks become harder, showing it has a regulating, problem-solving role.",45,8],[222,"Describe Luria's rubber bulb experiment.","Young children were asked to squeeze a bulb when a light came on and not otherwise. The youngest squeezed at every light, while older preschoolers succeeded, especially when saying 'press' or 'don't press' aloud.",45,9],[223,"A mother of a 4-year-old boy worries his self-talk is abnormal. What should you advise?","Self-talk is a normal developmental phase that helps young children plan. It is not a sign of psychosis or thought disorder.",45,10],[223,"What is verbal regulation of behaviour, and which part develops later?","The use of words to control one's own actions (Luria). Words can start an action before they can stop one, so inhibition comes under verbal control later.",45,11],[223,"What is Fodor's language of thought hypothesis?","Fodor (1975) proposed that thinking runs on an innate symbolic code called mentalese, which exists before spoken language and is used by babies and animals.",45,12],[223,"How does Fodor's view of language and thought differ from Whorf's?","Fodor says an inner code of thought comes before language. Whorf says spoken language shapes thought.",45,13],[223,"What did Varley and Siegal report in a man with severe agrammatic aphasia?","After a left hemisphere stroke he could not understand sentences, yet solved cause-and-effect problems and passed non-verbal false belief tasks.",45,14],[223,"What does preserved reasoning in severe agrammatic aphasia show?","Complex thought can proceed without intact grammar. It argues against strong linguistic determinism and against equating thought with inner speech.",45,15],[223,"What did Wynn show about number sense in preverbal infants?","Five-month-olds reacted to incorrect simple sums, showing basic numerical reasoning before language.",45,16],[223,"A 70-year-old man with expressive aphasia after a stroke needs a capacity assessment. What does the evidence imply?","Reasoning may be intact despite aphasia, so decision-making should be assessed using non-verbal aids before concluding he lacks capacity.",45,17],[226,"How does Chomsky's linguistic competence differ from Hymes's communicative competence?","Linguistic competence is an ideal speaker's knowledge of grammar. Hymes (1972) said competence also includes knowing when, where, to whom and how to speak appropriately.",46,1],[226,"Into which four components did later models divide communicative competence?","Grammatical, sociolinguistic, discourse and strategic competence.",46,2],[226,"What is pragmatics?","The study of how language is used in context to convey meaning beyond the literal words.",46,3],[226,"What is an indirect speech act? Give an example.","An utterance whose form differs from its function, described by Searle. 'Can you pass the salt?' looks like a question but is a request.",46,4],[226,"A 20-year-old man with autism answers 'Yes' to 'Can you pass the salt?' and does nothing. What is impaired?","Pragmatics. He takes the indirect request literally, even though his vocabulary and grammar are intact.",46,5],[226,"What is Grice's cooperative principle?","The assumption that speakers in conversation are cooperating, following shared maxims so that meaning can be inferred.",46,6],[226,"Name and define Grice's four maxims of conversation.","Quantity: give enough but not too much. Quality: say what you believe is true. Relation: be relevant. Manner: be clear.",46,7],[226,"What is an implicature?","A hidden meaning a listener infers when a speaker deliberately breaks, or flouts, one of Grice's maxims.",46,8],[226,"Saying 'Lovely weather' during a thunderstorm flouts which maxim, and who may miss the sarcasm?","It flouts the maxim of quality. People with autism or right hemisphere damage often take such remarks literally.",46,9],[226,"Which of Grice's maxims does circumstantial speech break?","The maxim of quantity, because too much unnecessary detail is given.",46,10],[227,"What are paraverbal cues and what is prosody?","Paraverbal cues are how something is said: tone, pitch, loudness, speed and pauses. Prosody is the rhythm, stress and intonation of speech.",46,11],[227,"A 65-year-old woman after a right frontal stroke speaks fluently but sounds flat. What is this and where are lesions that cause it?","Motor aprosodia, from right anterior lesions (Ross). Right posterior lesions instead impair recognising emotion in others' voices.",46,12],[227,"In which psychiatric and neurological conditions besides stroke does flat prosody occur?","Depression, Parkinson's disease and the negative symptoms of schizophrenia.",46,13],[227,"What do kinesics, haptics and proxemics refer to?","Kinesics is body movement, haptics is touch and proxemics is the use of personal space.",46,14],[227,"What are Hall's four proxemic zones and their distances?","Intimate (under 45 cm), personal (45 cm to 1.2 m), social (1.2 to 3.6 m) and public (beyond 3.6 m). Culture affects which distances feel comfortable.",46,15],[227,"What did Kendon find about gaze during conversation?","Listeners look at speakers more than speakers look at listeners. Speakers look away when starting and look up as they finish to hand over the turn.",46,16],[227,"What is the common misuse of Mehrabian's 7%, 38%, 55% figures?","They are wrongly applied to all communication. They came from single words about feelings or attitudes where words and tone conflicted, and Mehrabian warned against generalising them.",46,17],[227,"What is non-verbal leakage, according to Ekman and Friesen?","Hidden feelings leak out through the body, especially the legs and feet, which we control less than our faces.",46,18],[227,"Besides using a professional, what should be requested of interpreters in a psychiatric assessment?","Word-for-word translation, flagging of cultural meanings, and checks on dialect and the patient's gender preference.",46,19],[228,"A 45-year-old Tamil-speaking woman with possible psychosis has a 14-year-old son offering to interpret. What is best practice?","Use a trained professional interpreter, brief them beforehand and speak directly to the patient. Never use children, and avoid family, who may soften or omit symptoms.",46,20],[231,"What was McDougall's instinct theory, and why was it criticised?","McDougall (1908) said behaviour comes from inborn instincts such as flight, curiosity and pugnacity. The lists became endless and only named behaviour rather than explaining it.",47,1],[231,"What is homeostasis and who coined the term?","The body's maintenance of a stable internal state around a set point using corrective feedback. Walter Cannon coined the term.",47,2],[231,"What does Hull's drive reduction theory propose?","A biological need creates a drive, an unpleasant tension. Any behaviour that reduces the drive is reinforced and strengthened.",47,3],[231,"State Hull's formula for reaction potential and its implication when drive is zero.","Reaction potential equals habit strength multiplied by drive (sEr = sHr x D). With zero drive, even a strong habit produces no response.",47,4],[231,"What is the difference between primary and secondary drives?","Primary drives such as hunger, thirst and sex are innate. Secondary drives are learned through association with primary ones.",47,5],[231,"What is the classic example of a secondary drive and why?","Money. It has no biological value itself but is linked with obtaining food and shelter.",47,6],[231,"What did Harlow's puzzle-solving monkeys show about drive reduction theory?","Monkeys solved puzzles repeatedly without food or hunger, showing curiosity that drive reduction cannot explain.",47,7],[231,"What does arousal theory of motivation propose?","People seek an optimal level of arousal rather than reducing tension to zero.",47,8],[231,"State the Yerkes-Dodson law.","Performance rises with arousal up to an optimum and then falls, forming an inverted U.",47,9],[231,"A 23-year-old medical student performs worst in an OSCE when drowsy or panicking. Which principle applies?","The Yerkes-Dodson law: moderate arousal gives best performance, while too little or too much impairs it.",47,10],[231,"What is Zuckerman's concept of sensation seeking?","A personality trait involving a need for new, varied and intense experiences, such as skydiving.",47,11],[232,"What did Yerkes and Dodson's original 1908 study actually involve?","Mice learning a discrimination task with mild electric shocks. The general 'law' is a later summary of their findings.",47,12],[232,"Explain Solomon and Corbit's opponent process theory.","Every strong emotional state triggers an opposite after-state. With repetition the initial a-process stays the same while the opposing b-process grows, so the high shrinks and the low lasts longer.",47,13],[232,"How does opponent process theory explain why a 50-year-old man with alcohol dependence keeps drinking?","The growing b-process causes a lasting low, so he drinks to escape withdrawal rather than for pleasure. This explains tolerance and withdrawal.",47,14],[232,"Define intrinsic and extrinsic motivation.","Intrinsic motivation is doing something for its own satisfaction. Extrinsic motivation is doing it for a separate outcome such as pay, grades or praise.",47,15],[232,"Describe Lepper, Greene and Nisbett's study and the effect it showed.","Nursery children who liked drawing were promised a certificate and later drew less in free play than unrewarded children. This is the overjustification effect: expected rewards undermine existing interest.",47,16],[232,"How does self-perception explain the overjustification effect, and which rewards do least harm?","Children came to believe they drew for the prize rather than for fun. Unexpected praise does far less harm than expected rewards.",47,17],[232,"What are the three basic needs in Deci and Ryan's self-determination theory?","Autonomy, competence and relatedness.",47,18],[232,"How are theories of motivation divided into biological and socio-psychological groups?","Homeostatic, drive and arousal theories are biological. Needs and goal theories, such as Maslow's hierarchy of needs, are socio-psychological.",47,19],[233,"How does task difficulty affect the optimal level of arousal in the Yerkes-Dodson law?","Complex or unfamiliar tasks have a lower optimum, while simple, well-practised tasks tolerate higher arousal. Anxiety therefore disrupts complex performance sooner.",47,20],[236,"Who first described the stable internal environment, and who coined homeostasis?","Claude Bernard described the milieu intérieur. Walter Cannon coined homeostasis in 1926 and explained it in The Wisdom of the Body (1932).",48,1],[236,"What triggers osmotic thirst and where is it sensed?","Raised blood osmolality (blood too salty). It is detected by osmoreceptors in the OVLT.",48,2],[236,"What drives hypovolaemic thirst, for example after haemorrhage?","Loss of blood volume causes the kidneys to release renin, producing angiotensin II, which acts at the subfornical organ.",48,3],[236,"Which motivated behaviours does the hypothalamus control, and by what means?","Hunger, thirst, temperature and sex drive, acting through both nerves and hormones.",48,4],[236,"Which hypothalamic region is the body's thermostat?","The preoptic area of the anterior hypothalamus. Its warm-sensitive neurons control shivering, sweating and changes in blood vessel tone.",48,5],[236,"How does fever raise body temperature?","Prostaglandin E2 acts on preoptic neurons to raise the temperature set point.",48,6],[237,"What did Satinoff show with preoptic lesions in rats?","Rats could no longer shiver or constrict blood vessels well in the cold but still pressed a bar to turn on a heat lamp. Behavioural and autonomic thermoregulation are partly separate.",48,7],[237,"What do lateral hypothalamic orexin neurons do?","They fire during fasting, promote wakefulness and project to the VTA to boost food and drug seeking.",48,8],[237,"What disorder results from loss of orexin neurons, and how is orexin targeted in treatment?","Narcolepsy with cataplexy. Orexin antagonists are now used to treat insomnia.",48,9],[237,"What did Olds and Milner discover in 1954?","Rats with electrodes in the septal area pressed a lever at very high rates to stimulate their own brains, leading to the idea of a dedicated reward system.",48,10],[237,"Along which tract do the best self-stimulation sites lie, and what does it carry?","The medial forebrain bundle, which carries ascending dopamine fibres from the ventral tegmental area.",48,11],[237,"Describe the mesolimbic dopamine pathway.","It runs from dopamine cell bodies in the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens, where dopamine is released.",48,12],[237,"What did Di Chiara and Imperato find about drugs of abuse?","Amphetamine, cocaine, morphine, nicotine and alcohol all raise dopamine most in the nucleus accumbens.",48,13],[237,"What did Schultz find when recording dopamine neurons in monkeys learning that a light predicts juice?","Neurons first fired to the juice, then after training fired to the light, and dipped below baseline when expected juice did not arrive.",48,14],[237,"What is reward prediction error?","The difference between expected and actual reward. Better than expected increases dopamine firing and worse decreases it, acting as a teaching signal.",48,15],[237,"What did Berridge and Robinson show in dopamine-depleted rats?","They still showed pleasure reactions to sugar but would not work for it. Dopamine mediates wanting (incentive salience), not liking.",48,16],[237,"What underlies 'liking', and how do wanting and liking change in addiction?","Liking depends on opioid hotspots. In addiction wanting grows while liking stays the same or falls, explaining craving without pleasure.",48,17],[237,"How can dopamine-blocking antipsychotics affect motivation?","By blocking dopamine in reward pathways they can blunt motivation.",48,18],[241,"What did Anand and Brobeck find after lateral hypothalamic lesions in rats?","Rats stopped eating and drinking (aphagia and adipsia) and starved unless tube fed, so the area was called the feeding centre.",49,1],[241,"What did Hetherington and Ranson find after ventromedial hypothalamic lesions?","Rats overate (hyperphagia) and doubled their weight, so the VMH was called the satiety centre.",49,2],[241,"What did Teitelbaum find about the eating of VMH-lesioned rats?","They were finicky about taste, eating more palatable food but less bitter food than normal rats.",49,3],[241,"Who set out the dual-centre model of hunger, and why was it too simple?","Stellar (1954). Ungerstedt (1971) showed lateral lesions also cut passing nigrostriatal dopamine fibres, which partly explains the aphagia.",49,4],[241,"Why is the arcuate nucleus well placed to read hormonal signals of energy balance?","It sits near the median eminence, where circulating hormones can reach it.",49,5],[241,"Which arcuate nucleus neurons drive eating and which suppress it?","NPY/AgRP neurons drive eating. POMC/CART neurons suppress it, acting through melanocortin-4 receptors (MC4R) via alpha-MSH.",49,6],[241,"Where is leptin made and how does it act on the arcuate nucleus?","Leptin is made by fat cells in proportion to fat mass. It inhibits NPY/AgRP neurons and activates POMC neurons, so appetite falls.",49,7],[241,"Where is ghrelin produced and how do its levels change around meals?","In the stomach. It rises sharply in the hour before habitual meals and falls after eating, activating NPY/AgRP neurons to drive hunger.",49,8],[242,"What happens to ghrelin in anorexia nervosa, and what does this imply?","Ghrelin is high, so the drive to eat is present but overridden.",49,9],[242,"What is set-point theory of body weight and what evidence supports it?","The body defends a set weight. Leibel (1995) found energy expenditure shifted in over-fed or under-fed volunteers to push weight back.",49,10],[242,"How does the settling-point model differ from set-point theory?","It says weight drifts with the environment rather than being actively defended, which better explains rising population obesity.",49,11],[242,"What is the genetic basis of common obesity, and which gene was first identified?","Common obesity is polygenic, with many genes each of small effect. FTO was the first common variant found by genome-wide study in 2007.",49,12],[242,"How does congenital leptin deficiency present and how is it treated?","It is rare, with undetectable leptin causing relentless hunger from infancy (Montague 1997). Farooqi (1999) showed recombinant human leptin reduced appetite and weight.",49,13],[242,"Why is leptin not an effective treatment for most people with obesity?","Most obese people already have high leptin levels, reflecting leptin resistance.",49,14],[242,"What is Schachter's externality hypothesis and what did the speeded clock study show?","Obese people eat in response to external cues more than internal hunger. With a clock secretly speeded, obese people ate more when it showed dinner time had passed, though later work found cue sensitivity at all weights.",49,15],[242,"What is Herman and Polivy's restraint theory?","Dieters replace physiological hunger and satiety signals with a cognitive diet limit, making their eating vulnerable to disinhibition.",49,16],[242,"Describe Herman and Mack's milkshake preload study and the effect it showed.","After milkshake preloads, non-dieters ate less ice cream but dieters ate more. This is counter-regulation, the 'what the hell' effect of disinhibited restrained eating.",49,17],[242,"Why does treatment for bulimia and binge eating disorder aim to reduce dietary restriction?","Restraint theory links rigid dieting to disinhibited binge eating, so reducing restriction lowers binge risk.",49,18],[242,"A 30-year-old man on an antipsychotic is gaining weight. What should be done routinely?","Many antipsychotics cause weight gain, so weight and metabolic health should be monitored regularly.",49,19],[245,"Which theory proposes separate behavioural approach and behavioural inhibition systems underlying motivation?","Jeffrey Gray's reinforcement sensitivity theory. The approach system (BAS) responds to reward cues and the inhibition system (BIS) to punishment cues.",50,1],[245,"Who developed the BIS/BAS scales and in what year?","Carver and White in 1994. Items include statements such as worrying about making mistakes and feeling energised by getting what you want.",50,2],[245,"Which psychiatric features are linked with high BAS and with high BIS sensitivity respectively?","High BAS is linked with impulsivity and mania. High BIS is linked with anxiety.",50,3],[245,"List the structures of the Papez circuit in order, starting from the hippocampus.","Hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus, cingulate gyrus and parahippocampal gyrus, then back to the hippocampus.",50,4],[245,"Was the amygdala part of Papez's original 1937 emotion circuit, and who later included it?","No. MacLean added the amygdala in 1952 when he described the broader limbic system.",50,5],[245,"Which function is the Papez circuit now more closely associated with than emotion?","Memory. The loop is now thought to be more important for memory than for emotion.",50,6],[245,"Which brain areas are particularly involved in recognising facial expressions of disgust?","The insula and the basal ganglia.",50,7],[245,"A 38-year-old man with a family history of Huntington's disease is poor at recognising one facial emotion. Which emotion is most likely?","Disgust. Sprengelmeyer showed selective impairment of disgust recognition in Huntington's disease, sometimes even in carriers before symptoms appear.",50,8],[245,"Damage to which structure bilaterally causes difficulty recognising fearful facial expressions?","Both amygdalae. Bilateral amygdala damage makes fear hard to recognise.",50,9],[245,"In Gray and McNaughton's revised reinforcement sensitivity theory (2000), what does the fight-flight-freeze system handle?","The FFFS handles fear in response to a clear, definite threat.",50,10],[246,"In revised reinforcement sensitivity theory, what is the role of the behavioural inhibition system?","It resolves conflict between approach and avoidance, producing risk assessment, rumination and anxiety. It centres on the septohippocampal system, where anxiolytics are thought to act.",50,11],[246,"What does Davidson's approach-withdrawal model of frontal asymmetry propose?","Greater left prefrontal EEG activity goes with approach and positive mood, while greater right prefrontal activity goes with withdrawal and negative mood.",50,12],[246,"What frontal EEG pattern has been described in people with depression?","Reduced left prefrontal activity, in keeping with blunted approach motivation.",50,13],[246,"Anger is a negative emotion. With which side of the prefrontal cortex is it linked in Davidson's model, and why?","The left side, because anger is an approach emotion rather than a withdrawal emotion.",50,14],[246,"What is affective startle modulation as shown by Lang?","A sudden noise produces a larger blink during unpleasant pictures and a smaller blink during pleasant ones. Emotions prime the matching defensive or appetitive system.",50,15],[246,"What does Fredrickson's broaden-and-build theory state about positive and negative emotions?","Positive emotions widen the range of thoughts and actions and build lasting social, intellectual and physical resources. Negative emotions narrow action towards specific responses such as fight or flight.",50,16],[246,"What is the difference between anticipatory and consummatory pleasure?","Anticipatory pleasure is looking forward to a reward and drives effort. Consummatory pleasure is enjoying the reward in the moment.",50,17],[246,"A 24-year-old man with schizophrenia enjoys activities while doing them but shows little drive to plan any. Which type of anhedonia is this?","Anticipatory anhedonia. In schizophrenia, consummatory pleasure is relatively spared and the deficit in anticipation shows as amotivation.",50,18],[246,"What practical approach may help a person whose low drive comes from inability to anticipate pleasure?","Planning small, achievable and rewarding activities can help restore motivation.",50,19],[249,"Which structure acts as the brain's threat detector and where is it located?","The amygdala, a small almond-shaped structure deep in each temporal lobe.",51,1],[249,"Describe LeDoux's low road for fear.","A direct pathway from the thalamus to the amygdala that bypasses the cortex. It is fast but crude.",51,2],[249,"Describe LeDoux's high road for fear.","A pathway from the thalamus to the sensory cortex and then to the amygdala. It is slower but accurate and allows a mistaken fear response to be corrected.",51,3],[249,"A hiker jumps back from a curved stick before realising it is not a snake. Which pathway caused the initial reaction?","LeDoux's low road, from the thalamus directly to the amygdala, which triggers fear before conscious recognition.",51,4],[249,"Which brain region helps to dampen the fear response?","The prefrontal cortex helps calm and regulate fear.",51,5],[249,"What did Kluver and Bucy observe after removing both temporal lobes in monkeys?","The monkeys became tame, approached snakes without fear, mouthed objects, were sexually disinhibited and showed psychic blindness, seeing objects without recognising them.",51,6],[249,"A 55-year-old woman recovering from encephalitis is placid, puts objects in her mouth and is hypersexual. What syndrome and lesion are likely?","Kluver-Bucy syndrome, from bilateral damage to the amygdalae and temporal lobes. Herpes simplex encephalitis is a classic cause.",51,7],[249,"What is Urbach-Wiethe disease and what did the study of patient SM show?","A rare genetic condition that calcifies both amygdalae. Patient SM could recognise happy, sad and angry faces but not fear, and hardly experienced fear herself.",51,8],[249,"In cat studies by Flynn and Siegel, what does stimulation of the medial hypothalamus produce?","Affective defence: hissing, raised fur, dilated pupils, arched back and attack, a noisy and highly aroused response.",51,9],[250,"What does stimulation of the lateral hypothalamus in cats produce?","Quiet predatory attack, such as silent biting of a rat with little arousal.",51,10],[250,"How do the two types of hypothalamic attack in cats map onto human aggression?","Affective defence maps onto hot, reactive aggression and predatory attack maps onto cold, planned aggression.",51,11],[250,"What biochemical finding do impulsive violent offenders and violent suicide attempters share?","Low cerebrospinal fluid 5-HIAA, the main breakdown product of serotonin, as found by Asberg and Linnoila.",51,12],[250,"Is low CSF 5-HIAA linked with premeditated or impulsive violence?","Impulsive violence. It was not found in planned violence, reflecting serotonin's role in impulse control.",51,13],[250,"What best summarises the evidence on testosterone and human aggression?","A weak positive association that runs in both directions and depends on social context. It is seen in both sexes.",51,14],[250,"How do winning and losing affect testosterone levels?","Winning raises testosterone and losing lowers it, showing behaviour can change hormone levels.",51,15],[250,"What is Archer's challenge hypothesis?","Testosterone rises in competitive situations, so context shapes both the hormone level and the behaviour.",51,16],[250,"According to Mazur and Booth, what does testosterone mainly relate to?","Striving for dominance and status, more than aggression itself.",51,17],[250,"What is the best-replicated biological marker of antisocial behaviour in children and adolescents?","Low resting heart rate, as shown by Ortiz and Raine. Low cortisol and low skin conductance point the same way.",51,18],[250,"What gene-environment interaction did Caspi (2002) show for antisocial behaviour?","Boys with the low-activity MAOA variant who suffered childhood maltreatment had a higher risk of antisocial behaviour.",51,19],[251,"What are the two main explanations for the link between low resting heart rate and antisocial behaviour?","Fearlessness, with less anxiety about punishment, and stimulation seeking to raise a low level of arousal.",51,20],[253,"What is hostile attribution bias?","The tendency to interpret ambiguous actions by others as deliberately hostile. Dodge showed aggressive boys more often judged an accidental act as intentional and chose to retaliate.",52,1],[253,"At what stage of social information processing does hostile attribution bias operate, and how can it be treated?","At the interpretation stage, when deciding what an act means. Training more benign readings of intent reduces reactive aggression.",52,2],[253,"What did Cohen and Nisbett find when men were bumped and insulted in a corridor?","Men raised in the southern United States showed larger rises in cortisol and testosterone and behaved more aggressively.",52,3],[253,"What is a culture of honour, and why is it thought to develop?","A culture where a tough reputation must be defended. It arises where property such as herds can be stolen and law enforcement is weak.",52,4],[253,"What are the four phases of Masters and Johnson's sexual response cycle in order?","Excitement, plateau, orgasm and resolution.",52,5],[253,"In which phase of Masters and Johnson's model does the male refractory period occur?","In the resolution phase.",52,6],[253,"Who added desire as the first phase of the sexual response cycle?","Helen Kaplan in 1979, giving a desire, excitement and orgasm model.",52,7],[253,"What is distinctive about Basson's model of sexual response?","It is circular rather than linear, and desire may follow arousal (responsive desire), especially in long-term relationships.",52,8],[253,"What are the three levels of Gagnon and Simon's sexual script theory?","Cultural scenarios (shared guidelines), interpersonal scripts (negotiated with a partner) and intrapsychic scripts (in one's own fantasies).",52,9],[253,"What is the central claim of sexual script theory about sexual behaviour?","Sex is not a simple biological drive but is learned through social and cultural guides.",52,10],[253,"What does Bancroft and Janssen's dual control model of sexual response propose?","Separate systems for sexual excitation and sexual inhibition. Inhibition arises from fear of performance failure or of consequences.",52,11],[253,"In the dual control model, what are the effects of too much and too little sexual inhibition?","Too much inhibition is linked to sexual dysfunction. Too little is linked to risky sexual behaviour.",52,12],[254,"What did Baumeister and Leary propose in their need to belong theory?","A basic, universal drive for frequent pleasant interactions within lasting, caring relationships. Both elements are required.",52,13],[254,"What is the Cyberball paradigm and who developed it?","An online ball-tossing game in which the player is soon left out, developed by Kipling Williams to study ostracism.",52,14],[254,"Which four needs does ostracism threaten according to Williams?","Belonging, self-esteem, control and meaningful existence.",52,15],[254,"Does Cyberball exclusion still lower mood when players are told the others are computers?","Yes. Mood still drops, suggesting ostracism is detected quickly and automatically.",52,16],[254,"Which brain region did Eisenberger find was activated by social exclusion?","The dorsal anterior cingulate cortex, an area also involved in the distress of physical pain.",52,17],[254,"What did Heine find when Japanese and North American students received success or failure feedback?","Japanese students persisted longer after failure, while North American students persisted longer after success.",52,18],[254,"How do Markus and Kitayama's self-construals explain cultural differences in persistence?","Interdependent selves focus on self-improvement, so failure motivates effort. Independent selves focus on self-enhancement, so they persist where they already succeed.",52,19],[254,"Why is it clinically important to ask about loneliness and social exclusion?","Being lonely or excluded raises the risk of depression and suicide and harms physical health.",52,20],[257,"Who proposed the hierarchy of needs and in what year?","Abraham Maslow, an American psychologist, in 1943.",53,1],[257,"List the five levels of Maslow's original hierarchy from bottom to top.","Physiological, safety, love and belonging, esteem, and self-actualisation.",53,2],[257,"Give examples of physiological and safety needs in Maslow's hierarchy.","Physiological needs include food, water, sleep and warmth. Safety needs include shelter, order and freedom from danger.",53,3],[257,"Into which two parts did Maslow divide esteem needs?","Self-esteem, meaning competence, mastery and confidence, and esteem from others, meaning status, recognition and respect.",53,4],[257,"A 27-year-old female junior doctor most values feeling skilled and being recognised by senior colleagues. Which Maslow level is this?","Esteem needs, which cover competence and recognition from others.",53,5],[257,"How did Maslow define self-actualisation?","Becoming everything one is capable of being, fulfilling one's full potential.",53,6],[257,"What is meant by prepotency in Maslow's theory?","A badly unmet lower need dominates attention and behaviour. Higher needs only drive behaviour once lower needs are largely met.",53,7],[257,"What did later research find about the strictness of Maslow's ordering?","The order is looser than Maslow claimed, and people can pursue several needs at once.",53,8],[258,"List the seven levels of the extended version of Maslow's hierarchy in order.","Physiological, safety, belonging, esteem, cognitive, aesthetic, and self-actualisation.",53,9],[258,"In the seven-level model, which need lies immediately above esteem?","Cognitive needs, the need to know and understand. Aesthetic needs for beauty, order and balance come next.",53,10],[258,"Which needs are deficiency needs (D-needs) and how does motivation for them behave?","The bottom four: physiological, safety, belonging and esteem. They arise from lack and motivation falls once they are met.",53,11],[258,"Which needs are growth or being needs (B-needs), and how do they differ from deficiency needs?","Cognitive, aesthetic and self-actualisation needs. They grow stronger the more they are satisfied and are never fully met.",53,12],[258,"What is the highest deficiency need in Maslow's hierarchy?","Esteem. It is a deficiency need, not a growth need.",53,13],[258,"What characteristics did Maslow describe in self-actualised people such as Lincoln and Einstein?","They were realistic, independent and spontaneous, focused on problems outside themselves, and often had peak experiences.",53,14],[258,"What is a peak experience, and how does it differ from flow?","A brief, intense moment of wonder, unity and deep fulfilment described by Maslow. Flow is Csikszentmihalyi's later idea of total absorption in a task.",53,15],[258,"What are the three needs in Alderfer's ERG theory?","Existence, relatedness and growth, proposed by Clayton Alderfer in 1969.",53,16],[258,"What is frustration-regression in Alderfer's ERG theory?","If a higher need is blocked, a person turns back to a lower need, such as investing in work friendships after missing promotion. It belongs to Alderfer, not Maslow.",53,17],[258,"How does Alderfer's ERG theory differ from Maslow regarding the order of needs?","Alderfer held that several needs can operate at the same time rather than in a strict sequence.",53,18],[259,"A 45-year-old man sleeping rough is referred for therapy for low self-worth. Which Maslow principle supports first arranging a hostel and meals?","Prepotency: basic physiological and safety needs must be largely met before higher-level psychological work can succeed.",53,19],[262,"Who first named the need for achievement, and how is nAch defined?","Henry Murray in 1938. It is a lasting wish to do something well and to meet or beat a high standard.",54,1],[262,"What are McClelland's three learned needs?","Need for achievement, need for affiliation (to be liked and close to others) and need for power (to influence others).",54,2],[262,"What are the two forms of need for power described by McClelland, and which marks an effective manager?","Personal power, aimed at dominating people, and institutional power, influence used for the group's goals. Institutional power marks the effective manager.",54,3],[262,"A 40-year-old female manager is energised by influencing decisions across her organisation. Which of McClelland's needs dominates?","The need for power, since influence over others is power rather than achievement.",54,4],[262,"Which test did McClelland use to measure need for achievement?","The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), in which people tell stories about ambiguous pictures that are scored for achievement themes.",54,5],[262,"Is need for achievement measured with the Rorschach inkblot test?","No. It is measured by scoring stories told to TAT pictures, not with the Rorschach.",54,6],[262,"What societal link did McClelland propose in 1961 regarding achievement imagery?","Higher levels of achievement themes in a nation's stories were linked with its later economic growth.",54,7],[262,"Why do story-based and questionnaire measures of need for achievement correlate poorly?","They tap distinct motive systems: implicit motives shown in stories and self-attributed motives shown in questionnaires, as McClelland, Koestner and Weinberger explained.",54,8],[262,"What do implicit motives predict compared with self-attributed motives?","Implicit motives, learned early, predict spontaneous long-term behaviour such as starting businesses. Self-attributed motives are conscious values that predict deliberate choices when directly asked.",54,9],[263,"What type of task do people high in need for achievement prefer?","Moderately difficult tasks with clear feedback and personal responsibility. Easy tasks feel hollow and impossible ones offer no real chance.",54,10],[263,"State Atkinson's expectancy-value formula for the tendency to approach success.","Approach tendency equals motive times probability of success times incentive, where incentive is one minus the probability of success.",54,11],[263,"Why is the incentive value of success defined as one minus the probability of success in Atkinson's theory?","Because easy successes bring little pride, so the less likely success is, the greater its value.",54,12],[263,"At what probability of success is Atkinson's approach tendency greatest?","At a probability of 0.5, which is why high achievers prefer moderately difficult tasks.",54,13],[263,"According to Atkinson, which tasks do people high in fear of failure choose, and why?","Very easy or very hard tasks. They will not fail at easy tasks, and failing very hard tasks brings no shame.",54,14],[263,"What did Winterbottom (1958) find about the origins of high need for achievement in boys?","Their mothers expected early independence and rewarded success warmly. This is called independence training.",54,15],[263,"What type of parenting tends to foster fear of failure rather than achievement motivation?","Harsh, critical and demanding parenting.",54,16],[263,"What did McClelland and Winter's work with business owners in Kakinada, India show?","Short achievement motivation training led men to start more businesses and invest more over two years, showing nAch is learned and can be raised in adults.",54,17],[263,"How can achievement motivation research be applied to rehabilitation in psychiatry?","By setting graded, moderately hard goals with clear feedback, which suits recovery and rehabilitation work.",54,18],[263,"How do implicit motives help explain mismatches in patients' behaviour?","They explain why a patient's stated goals may differ from what they actually do, since implicit and conscious motives are separate.",54,19],[267,"What are the main facets or components of an emotion?","A subjective feeling, bodily arousal, a cognitive appraisal of the event, a facial expression and an urge to act.",55,1],[267,"How does an emotion differ from a mood?","An emotion is brief, intense and directed at a specific object. A mood is weaker, longer lasting and has no clear target.",55,2],[267,"In the mental state examination, how is mood distinguished from affect?","Mood is what the patient reports over time. Affect is what the clinician observes.",55,3],[267,"What does the Schachter-Singer two-factor theory of emotion propose?","Emotion is physiological arousal plus a cognitive label drawn from the situation. Arousal itself is largely nonspecific.",55,4],[267,"A 26-year-old woman's heart pounds after strong coffee and, seeing others laughing at a party, she feels excited. Which theory explains this?","The Schachter-Singer two-factor theory, in which she labels nonspecific arousal using cues from her surroundings.",55,5],[267,"In Schachter and Singer's 1962 adrenaline study, which participants caught the actor's mood most?","Those with no accurate explanation for their arousal. Informed participants blamed the injection instead.",55,6],[267,"What did Dutton and Aron's 1974 swaying bridge study demonstrate?","Misattribution of arousal. Men on a high swaying bridge relabelled fear arousal as attraction and were more likely to call a female interviewer.",55,7],[267,"What did Valins (1966) show using false heartbeat feedback?","Men rated photos as more attractive when they heard a faster fake heartbeat, showing that simply believing you are aroused shapes emotion.",55,8],[267,"What is the central claim of Lazarus's cognitive appraisal theory?","The way a person judges an event comes before and determines the emotion, so two people can feel differently about the same event.",55,9],[268,"What questions are asked in primary and secondary appraisal?","Primary appraisal asks whether the event matters and whether it is a threat or a challenge. Secondary appraisal asks whether I can cope and what support I have.",55,10],[268,"What was Zajonc's position in the debate with Lazarus about cognition and emotion?","Zajonc argued that feeling can come before thinking, that preferences need no inferences, citing liking for shapes shown too fast to recognise.",55,11],[268,"How did Lazarus respond to Zajonc's claim of affective primacy?","He argued appraisal can be extremely fast and unconscious, so it still precedes emotion.",55,12],[268,"What does the facial feedback hypothesis propose, and what is the status of the evidence?","Facial expressions feed back and change how we feel. Strack's pen-in-teeth study supported it, but a large 2016 replication did not, so evidence is mixed.",55,13],[268,"Name Ekman's six basic emotions.","Anger, disgust, fear, happiness, sadness and surprise. Contempt was later proposed as a seventh.",55,14],[268,"What is the Facial Action Coding System?","A system developed by Ekman and Friesen that codes each visible facial movement as a numbered action unit.",55,15],[268,"What are the four pairs of opposite primary emotions in Plutchik's wheel?","Joy and sadness, trust and disgust, fear and anger, and surprise and anticipation.",55,16],[268,"Which two of Plutchik's primary emotions are not among Ekman's six basic emotions?","Trust and anticipation.",55,17],[268,"In Plutchik's model, which two emotions blend to form love?","Joy and trust, which are neighbours on the wheel.",55,18],[268,"What is alexithymia and who named it?","Difficulty identifying and describing one's own feelings, with concrete, externally focused thinking. Sifneos named it in 1973.",55,19],[268,"A 50-year-old man with medically unexplained symptoms struggles to describe feelings. Which trait is likely and how is it measured?","Alexithymia, measured with the Toronto Alexithymia Scale. It can make talking therapy harder.",55,20],[271,"What are the three main functions of emotion?","Survival, by preparing the body for danger; social communication, by signalling feelings and intentions; and motivation, by providing urges to act.",56,1],[271,"What term did Frijda use for the urges to act that accompany each emotion?","Action tendencies, such as fleeing in fear or approaching in joy.",56,2],[271,"What did Darwin argue in 1872 about facial expressions?","That facial expressions evolved and are shared by all humans, and that they serve communication.",56,3],[271,"What did Ekman and Friesen's study of the Fore people of Papua New Guinea show?","People with almost no Western media contact matched faces to emotional stories above chance, supporting universal facial expressions.",56,4],[271,"Which two emotions did the Fore people most often confuse, and why?","Fear and surprise, because both involve wide eyes and raised eyebrows.",56,5],[271,"What are display rules?","Learned cultural norms about when and how emotions may be shown in company. The basic expressions are inborn, but their management is learned.",56,6],[271,"Japanese and American students watched an unpleasant film alone and then with an experimenter present. What happened?","Both showed the same disgust alone, but Japanese students masked it with polite smiles when the experimenter was present, illustrating display rules.",56,7],[272,"What did Kraut and Johnston's bowling alley study show?","Bowlers rarely smiled facing the pins after a strike but smiled when turning to friends, showing expressions act largely as social signals.",56,8],[272,"Which muscles produce a Duchenne smile, and how does it differ from a polite smile?","Zygomaticus major plus orbicularis oculi, which creates crow's feet. A polite smile uses zygomaticus major alone.",56,9],[272,"What are the components of the embarrassment display described by Keltner?","Looking down, a controlled smile, turning the head away and touching the face.",56,10],[272,"A 33-year-old man knocks over a supermarket display, looks down, smiles awkwardly and touches his face. What function does this serve?","The appeasement function of embarrassment, signalling regret and reducing others' anger so that observers like and forgive him more.",56,11],[272,"What did Susskind and colleagues find about fear and disgust facial expressions?","A fear face widens the eyes, enlarges the visual field and increases nasal airflow, while a disgust face does the opposite, so expressions alter sensory intake.",56,12],[272,"What did Darwin mean by serviceable associated habits?","That emotional expressions began as useful physical actions, such as altering sensory intake, before becoming signals.",56,13],[272,"What is the behavioural immune system?","Schaller and Park's idea that disgust makes us avoid possible sources of infection before germs enter the body.",56,14],[272,"Why does the behavioural immune system produce many false alarms?","Because missing a real infection threat is costly, so the system errs on the side of caution.",56,15],[272,"How does the behavioural immune system relate to stigma?","People who feel prone to infection avoid strangers and people with harmless skin marks, which can fuel stigma towards visible skin conditions.",56,16],[272,"Why should clinicians not assume a calm facial expression indicates calm inner feelings?","Display rules mean people may mask emotions, particularly across cultures, so a calm face does not prove a calm mind.",56,17],[272,"Why is facial emotion recognition clinically relevant in psychiatry?","Reading faces is central to rapport, and patients with some conditions misread facial expressions.",56,18],[275,"What does the James-Lange theory of emotion propose?","An event triggers bodily changes, and the feeling of emotion is our awareness of those changes. We feel afraid because we tremble.",57,1],[275,"Who proposed the James-Lange theory and when?","William James in 1884 and Carl Lange in 1885, working independently.",57,2],[275,"A 30-year-old man meets a snarling dog, notices his heart racing and legs shaking, and then feels afraid. Which theory fits?","The James-Lange theory, in which the feeling follows awareness of bodily changes.",57,3],[275,"What prediction does the James-Lange theory make about bodily patterns across emotions?","Each emotion must have its own distinct pattern of bodily change, otherwise the brain could not tell emotions apart.",57,4],[275,"What does the Cannon-Bard theory of emotion propose?","The thalamus processes the threat and signals the cortex and the body at the same time, so feeling and arousal occur simultaneously and independently.",57,5],[275,"In Cannon-Bard theory, which structure was originally central, and where did later work shift the focus?","The thalamus was central. Later work moved the focus to the hypothalamus and limbic system.",57,6],[275,"In Cannon-Bard theory, does bodily arousal cause the feeling of emotion?","No. Arousal and feeling are simultaneous and independent, and neither causes the other.",57,7],[275,"What was Cannon's argument about the speed of visceral responses?","Visceral changes are too slow to cause feelings, because the organs respond more slowly than emotions are felt.",57,8],[275,"What did Cannon argue about the sensory supply of the viscera and arousal across emotions?","The viscera have few sensory nerves so are poorly sensed, and bodily arousal looks much the same across different emotions.",57,9],[276,"What did Cannon argue about adrenaline injections and cutting viscera off from the brain?","Adrenaline produces arousal but not a true emotion, and animals with viscera disconnected from the brain still showed emotional behaviour.",57,10],[276,"What did Hohmann (1966) find in men with spinal cord injuries?","Men with higher injuries, with less bodily sensation, reported weaker fear and anger, supporting James-Lange.",57,11],[276,"Which theory do Hohmann's findings support, and how robust are they?","They support James-Lange theory, but later studies did not always replicate them, so support is mixed.",57,12],[276,"What did Ekman, Levenson and Friesen (1983) find when people posed emotional faces?","Different emotions showed different physiological patterns, contradicting Cannon's claim that arousal is the same for all emotions.",57,13],[276,"In the 1983 posed emotion study, which emotion raised finger temperature most?","Anger, which fits the idea of being hot-headed.",57,14],[276,"How did fear affect heart rate and finger temperature in the 1983 study?","Fear raised heart rate but lowered finger temperature, as blood moved to large muscles.",57,15],[276,"What is the current view of the James-Lange versus Cannon-Bard debate?","Neither is fully right. Brain and body interact in loops, bodily feedback can shape feelings, and appraisal adds meaning.",57,16],[276,"How does panic disorder illustrate James-Lange ideas?","Noticing a racing heart can intensify fear, so bodily signs and fear feed each other.",57,17],[276,"How does cognitive therapy for panic disorder relate to bodily sensations?","It targets the catastrophic misinterpretation of bodily sensations, such as reading palpitations as a heart attack.",57,18],[276,"How does the Schachter-Singer theory differ from James-Lange theory?","Schachter-Singer requires a cognitive label from the situation as well as arousal, whereas James-Lange does not need a label.",57,19],[280,"How did Lazarus and Folkman (1984) define stress?","As a transaction that arises when a person judges that demands exceed their resources.",58,1],[280,"What is the difference between primary and secondary appraisal in Lazarus and Folkman's stress model?","Primary appraisal judges whether the event matters and whether it is harmless or a threat. Secondary appraisal judges what can be done and what resources are available.",58,2],[280,"A 52-year-old man facing redundancy lists his savings, supportive partner and job contacts. Which Lazarus process is this?","Secondary appraisal, which assesses coping options and resources. Good resources can turn a threat into a challenge.",58,3],[280,"What is the difference between eustress and distress according to Selye?","Eustress is energising and helps performance. Distress is harmful.",58,4],[280,"What does the Yerkes-Dodson law state?","Performance rises with arousal up to an optimum and then falls, forming an inverted U. The optimal arousal is lower for difficult or new tasks.",58,5],[280,"A 45-year-old female surgeon sutures well when moderately alert but errs on a complex new operation when very anxious. Which principle applies?","The Yerkes-Dodson law, since complex tasks are impaired at lower levels of arousal than simple tasks.",58,6],[280,"Which axis mediates the fast fight or flight response, and who described it?","The sympathetic-adrenal-medullary (SAM) axis, described by Walter Cannon in 1915. The adrenal medulla releases adrenaline and noradrenaline.",58,7],[280,"What is the role of the parasympathetic nervous system in the stress response?","It acts as the brake, mainly through the vagus nerve, slowing the heart and restoring rest after stress.",58,8],[280,"Where is CRH released from in the hypothalamus and where does it act?","From the paraventricular nucleus into the portal circulation, acting on the anterior pituitary.",58,9],[281,"Why do people with blood-injection-injury phobia faint, and what treatment is used?","A brief rise in heart rate and blood pressure is followed by a vagal surge that drops them. Ost's applied tension, tensing large muscles to keep blood pressure up, is used rather than relaxation.",58,10],[281,"Describe the sequence of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis.","The hypothalamus releases CRH, which acts on the anterior pituitary to release ACTH, which makes the adrenal cortex release cortisol.",58,11],[281,"How is the HPA axis switched off, and which structures are involved?","By cortisol negative feedback on the hippocampus, hypothalamus and pituitary.",58,12],[281,"What does the dexamethasone suppression test show in melancholic depression?","In about half of patients, dexamethasone fails to suppress next-day cortisol, showing impaired HPA negative feedback. It is not specific enough for diagnosis.",58,13],[281,"What triad did Selye observe in rats exposed to different stressors?","Adrenal enlargement, thymic shrinkage and gastric ulcers, leading him to call the stress response non-specific.",58,14],[281,"What are the three stages of Selye's general adaptation syndrome?","Alarm, resistance and exhaustion.",58,15],[281,"What is allostatic load?","McEwen's term for the cumulative wear and tear from repeated stress responses, contributing to hypertension, obesity and brain changes.",58,16],[281,"What did Kiecolt-Glaser find about wound healing in dementia carers?","Small wounds took about nine days longer to heal than in controls, showing stress impairs immune function.",58,17],[281,"Why is skin conductance a pure measure of sympathetic activity?","Sweat glands have no parasympathetic supply. Their sympathetic fibres unusually release acetylcholine.",58,18],[281,"Which physiological measures reflect vagal tone and HPA axis activity respectively?","Heart rate variability reflects vagal tone. Salivary cortisol reflects HPA axis activity.",58,19],[283,"A 48-year-old manager seems calm weeks after a crisis but has high cortisol and frequent colds. Which stage of the general adaptation syndrome is this?","The resistance stage, in which the person appears to cope while arousal stays high and reserves drain.",58,20],[285,"Two sisters both lose their jobs. One with a family history of depression and negative thinking becomes depressed. Which model explains this?","The diathesis-stress model, in which illness appears when stress meets a pre-existing vulnerability.",59,1],[285,"What is the Social Readjustment Rating Scale and who developed it?","A list of 43 life events, each scored in life change units by how much readjustment it needs, developed by Holmes and Rahe in 1967.",59,2],[285,"Which event was the anchor on the Social Readjustment Rating Scale and what value did it have?","Marriage, fixed at 50 life change units. Using a positive event shows the scale measures change, not just unpleasantness.",59,3],[285,"Which event scores highest on the Social Readjustment Rating Scale?","Death of a spouse, at 100 life change units.",59,4],[285,"How do divorce and marital separation compare on the Social Readjustment Rating Scale?","Divorce scores 73 and separation scores 65, so divorce is higher.",59,5],[285,"What annual life change unit score was linked with illness on the SRRS?","More than 300 life change units in a year.",59,6],[285,"What are the main weaknesses of the life events checklist approach?","Prospective studies showed only weak correlations of about 0.1 to 0.3, and retrospective reports suffer recall bias as ill people search their past for causes.",59,7],[285,"What type of life event did Paykel (1969) link with depression?","Exit events, in which someone leaves the person's life.",59,8],[285,"According to Kendler (2003), which event types predict depression and which predict anxiety?","Humiliation and loss predict depression. Danger, a threat of future loss, predicts anxiety.",59,9],[285,"What did Brown, Harris and Hepworth (1995) find about entrapment?","Being trapped in a situation with no way out is strongly linked with depression.",59,10],[285,"What did Brown and Birley (1968) find about life events and schizophrenia?","There was an excess of life events in the three weeks before relapse, acting as triggers.",59,11],[285,"What is the kindling hypothesis of recurrent depression?","Post proposed that each episode makes the next easier to trigger, so later episodes need smaller or no stressors.",59,12],[286,"What four vulnerability factors for depression did Brown and Harris find in Camberwell women?","Loss of mother before age 11, three or more children under 14 at home, no confiding relationship, and no paid work outside the home.",59,13],[286,"Do Brown and Harris's vulnerability factors cause depression on their own?","No. They raise risk only when a severe life event also occurs.",59,14],[286,"Who proposed the diathesis-stress model and what does it state?","Zubin and Spring in 1977. Illness appears when stress meets an existing vulnerability such as genes or negative thinking.",59,15],[286,"What did Kanner and colleagues (1981) find about daily hassles?","Daily hassles predicted symptoms better than major life events. Uplifts are small positive moments that may offset them.",59,16],[286,"Describe the Type A behaviour pattern and who described it.","Friedman and Rosenman, cardiologists, described it in 1959 as competitive, impatient, time-urgent and hostile.",59,17],[286,"Which component of Type A behaviour is most linked with coronary heart disease?","Hostility, rather than time urgency or competitiveness.",59,18],[286,"What is Type D personality and what did it predict?","Denollet's distressed type combines negative affectivity with social inhibition. It predicted poorer outcomes after heart attack, though later effects were smaller.",59,19],[287,"Which instrument reduces recall bias in life events research, and how?","The Life Events and Difficulties Schedule, which uses contextual rating, careful dating and judging whether events are independent of the illness.",59,20],[289,"A 52-year-old man with diabetes says his health depends on luck and his doctors, so he rarely checks his glucose. Which concept is this?","This is an external locus of control, described by Rotter (1966) and measured with the I-E scale. It is linked with poorer self-care, and Wallston adapted the idea for health.",60,1],[289,"What did Seligman and Maier (1967) show in their triadic design with dogs?","Only dogs that had earlier received shocks they could not stop later failed to escape over a low barrier. Lack of control, not the pain itself, taught them to give up.",60,2],[289,"What disorder was learned helplessness proposed as a model for?","Seligman proposed it as a model of depression.",60,3],[289,"What did the reformulated learned helplessness model of Abramson, Seligman and Teasdale (1978) add?","It added attributional style. Depression is more likely when bad events are explained as internal, stable and global.",60,4],[289,"How did Maier and Seligman (2016) revise the learned helplessness theory?","They argued that passivity is the brain's default response to prolonged uncontrollable stress. What animals actually learn is that they have control.",60,5],[289,"How did Lazarus and Folkman divide coping, and which style is best?","Problem-focused coping changes the problem, while emotion-focused coping changes how it feels. Neither is always best; problem focus suits changeable stressors and emotion focus suits unchangeable ones.",60,6],[289,"A 40-year-old woman copes with bereavement by seeing friends, journalling and reframing. Which coping type is this?","This is emotion-focused coping. It fits well because the loss itself cannot be changed.",60,7],[289,"What are the five points at which emotion can be regulated in Gross's process model?","Situation selection, situation modification, attentional deployment, cognitive change (reappraisal) and response modulation. The first four act before the emotion is fully formed.",60,8],[290,"Why can declining a party to avoid anxiety keep the anxiety going?","It is situation selection used as avoidance. No new learning happens, so the anxiety is maintained.",60,9],[290,"How do reappraisal and expressive suppression differ in their effects?","Reappraisal changes the meaning of a situation and lowers felt emotion. Suppression hides the expression but leaves felt emotion unchanged and raises sympathetic arousal (Gross and Levenson, 1993).",60,10],[290,"Which is antecedent-focused and which is response-focused: reappraisal or suppression?","Reappraisal is antecedent-focused, acting before the emotion is fully formed. Suppression is response-focused.",60,11],[290,"What are the three components of hardiness described by Kobasa (1979)?","Commitment, control and challenge. Challenge means seeing change as a chance to grow.",60,12],[290,"What is learned resourcefulness and how is it measured?","Rosenbaum (1980) described it as learned self-control skills such as self-instruction and problem-solving. It is measured with the Self-Control Schedule.",60,13],[290,"What is the buffering hypothesis of social support?","Cohen and Wills (1985) found that social support protects mainly when stress is high. It cushions the effect of stress rather than helping equally at all times.",60,14],[290,"What is Antonovsky's sense of coherence?","In his salutogenic approach, people stay healthy despite hardship when life feels comprehensible, manageable and meaningful.",60,15],[290,"What is the steeling effect described in Rutter's work on resilience?","Rutter called coping well despite adversity resilience. A manageable, supported stress early in life can strengthen later coping, which is the steeling effect.",60,16],[290,"How does ICD-11 classify burnout?","It is an occupational phenomenon in the chapter on factors influencing health status, not a mental disorder. It applies only to the work context.",60,17],[291,"A 45-year-old ward nurse calls patients by their overdose and feels indifferent to them. Which Maslach burnout dimension is this?","This is depersonalisation (cynicism). The other two dimensions are emotional exhaustion and reduced personal accomplishment.",60,18],[295,"What is an attitude and what are its three components in the ABC model?","An attitude is a learned, fairly stable judgement of a person, thing or idea. Rosenberg and Hovland (1960) described affective, behavioural and cognitive parts, which can conflict.",61,1],[295,"How is a Likert scale built and scored, and what are its weaknesses?","Respondents rate agreement with statements, usually on 5 points, and ratings are summed. It is easy to build and gives ordinal data but is prone to central tendency and acquiescence bias.",61,2],[295,"How is a Thurstone scale constructed and scored?","Judges first place many statements on an 11-point scale to give equal-appearing intervals. Respondents tick statements they agree with and the score is the median value; building it is slow.",61,3],[295,"What is the key feature of a Guttman scale?","It is cumulative: items are ordered so that agreeing with a strong item implies agreeing with all milder ones.",61,4],[295,"What is Osgood's semantic differential and what are its three factors?","A concept is rated on 7-point lines between opposite adjectives such as warm and cold. Its factors are evaluation (the largest), potency and activity.",61,5],[295,"A respondent marks the far-left box on every semantic differential line regardless of meaning. What is this and how is acquiescence handled?","This is positional response bias, a weakness of the semantic differential. Acquiescence (yes-saying) is reduced by balancing positive and negative item wording.",61,6],[296,"What did LaPiere (1934) show about attitudes and behaviour?","Touring the USA with a Chinese couple, they were refused service only once, yet most places later wrote that they would not serve Chinese guests. Stated attitudes did not predict behaviour.",61,7],[296,"When do attitudes best predict behaviour?","When the attitude is strong, formed through direct experience and specific to the act. High self-monitors have attitudes that predict their behaviour less well (Snyder, 1974).",61,8],[296,"What three factors determine intention in Ajzen's theory of planned behaviour?","Attitude, subjective norm (what others think) and perceived behavioural control. It helps explain medication adherence.",61,9],[296,"What is the sleeper effect?","A message from a low-credibility source becomes more persuasive weeks later because the source is forgotten while the message is remembered.",61,10],[296,"Two speeches are given back to back and the vote is delayed. Which speech is likely to be more influential?","The first speech, because of a primacy effect.",61,11],[296,"What are the two routes in Petty and Cacioppo's elaboration likelihood model?","Motivated, able listeners use the central route and change lastingly. Distracted listeners use the peripheral route, relying on cues like celebrity, so change is temporary.",61,12],[296,"What happens when a health message uses high fear without giving a clear plan of action?","It tends to cause defensive avoidance of the threat rather than behaviour change. Campaigns need clear action plans.",61,13],[296,"What is McGuire's attitude inoculation?","Building resistance to persuasion by exposing people to a weak counter-argument together with its rebuttal.",61,14],[296,"How do the foot-in-the-door, door-in-the-face and low-ball techniques differ?","Foot-in-the-door uses a small request before a large one. Door-in-the-face starts with a huge request that is refused, then a smaller one. Low-ball gains agreement first, then worsens the terms.",61,15],[296,"In Festinger's dissonance study, why did students paid one dollar like a dull task more than those paid twenty?","With insufficient justification for lying, they reduced cognitive dissonance by changing their attitude to match what they had said.",61,16],[297,"According to the Yale approach, which communicator feature most increases persuasion?","Source credibility, meaning perceived expertise and trustworthiness (Hovland and colleagues, 1953). Trust rises when the speaker seems to gain nothing from persuading.",61,17],[297,"A trust must persuade sceptical, well-informed consultants to adopt new software. Which message structure works best?","A two-sided message that refutes objections. It works best with educated or initially opposed audiences and builds resistance to later counter-arguments.",61,18],[300,"What is the self-serving bias?","Crediting successes to oneself and failures to circumstances. It concerns success versus failure, not self versus others.",62,1],[300,"What did Heider (1958) mean by naive psychology?","Ordinary people act like amateur scientists seeking causes for behaviour. They make internal (dispositional) or external (situational) attributions.",62,2],[300,"What are the three sources of information in Kelley's covariation model?","Consensus (do others act the same way), consistency (does this person always act this way) and distinctiveness (do they act this way only here).",62,3],[300,"Which pattern in Kelley's model leads to an internal attribution?","Low consensus, high consistency and low distinctiveness. High levels of all three point to an external cause, the thing being reacted to.",62,4],[300,"A colleague is late and judged lazy, though there was a bus strike. Which bias is this?","The fundamental attribution error (Ross, 1977): overestimating personal disposition and ignoring the situation. It is weaker in collectivist cultures.",62,5],[300,"Which classic study demonstrated the fundamental attribution error using essays?","Jones and Harris (1967). Readers believed writers held the views in their essays even when told the position was assigned.",62,6],[300,"How does the actor-observer bias differ from the fundamental attribution error?","The actor-observer bias contrasts self and others: my lateness is due to traffic, yours to laziness. The fundamental attribution error concerns explaining others' behaviour only.",62,7],[300,"What are the false consensus effect and the just world hypothesis?","False consensus is believing most people share our choices. Lerner's just world hypothesis is the need to see the world as fair, which leads to blaming victims.",62,8],[301,"What attributional style is typical of depression?","Bad events are explained as internal, stable and global, and the usual self-serving bias is weak or absent.",62,9],[301,"What attributional pattern did Bentall link with persecutory delusions?","An external personal attribution for negative events, blaming other people's deliberate actions rather than circumstances. This may protect fragile self-esteem.",62,10],[301,"What did Asch (1946) show by swapping warm for cold in a trait list?","Impressions of the stranger changed greatly, so warm and cold are central traits. Swapping polite and blunt had little effect.",62,11],[301,"What is the Barnum effect?","People accept vague, general personality descriptions as uniquely true of themselves (Forer, 1949).",62,12],[301,"What is the Hawthorne effect and what caveat applies?","Workers improved whether lighting went up or down, apparently because they were being watched. Later reanalysis found the effect much smaller than the legend suggests.",62,13],[301,"What did Schachter (1959) find about affiliation?","Frightened people preferred to wait with others facing the same situation. Affiliation is the wish to be with others.",62,14],[301,"Which factors most predict who becomes friends?","Proximity (living or working nearby), similarity and reciprocal liking.",62,15],[302,"A friendly, well-groomed nurse is rated highly competent at drug calculations that were never observed. What is this?","The halo effect (Thorndike, 1920): one positive trait colours ratings of unrelated qualities. Raters should score each item independently.",62,16],[302,"Teachers told that random pupils would bloom saw them gain more IQ. What is this effect and how robust is it?","The Pygmalion effect (Rosenthal and Jacobson, 1968), a self-fulfilling prophecy. Gains were mainly in the youngest pupils and later studies found the effect real but small.",62,17],[304,"What is theory of mind and who coined the term?","It is the ability to infer what others believe, want and intend. Premack and Woodruff (1978) coined it when asking whether chimpanzees have it.",63,1],[304,"At what ages are first-order and second-order false belief tasks usually passed?","First-order false belief is passed at around 4 years. Second-order false belief ('she thinks that he thinks') comes at about 6 to 7 years.",63,2],[304,"Describe Wimmer and Perner's (1983) Maxi task and typical results by age.","Maxi's chocolate is moved while he is out and children are asked where he will look. Most 3-year-olds point to the real location; from about 4 more children answer correctly, and by 6 nearly all do.",63,3],[304,"A 3-year-old girl finds pencils in a Smarties tube and says she always thought it held pencils. What does this show?","She does not yet understand false belief, even about her own past mind (Perner, Leekam and Wimmer, 1987).",63,4],[305,"Describe the Sally-Anne task and the main finding of Baron-Cohen, Leslie and Frith (1985).","Sally leaves a marble in a basket and Anne moves it to a box. About 80% of autistic children said Sally would look in the box, failing the task.",63,5],[305,"Why was the Down syndrome comparison group important in the Sally-Anne study?","Most children with Down syndrome passed despite lower mental age. This showed the autistic deficit was specific, not general developmental delay.",63,6],[305,"What did Baron-Cohen (1995) call the theory of mind deficit in autism?","Mindblindness: difficulty attributing mental states to others.",63,7],[305,"What did Leslie and Thaiss (1992) show with the false-photograph task?","Autistic children passed the matched false-photograph task but failed false belief. This supports a specific mentalising deficit.",63,8],[305,"What is protodeclarative pointing and when does it appear?","Pointing to share interest and checking the other person is looking, a form of joint attention. It appears at about 9 to 14 months.",63,9],[305,"How does protodeclarative pointing differ from protoimperative pointing?","Protodeclarative pointing shares interest, while protoimperative pointing only requests an object.",63,10],[305,"A health visitor finds an 18-month-old boy does not point to share interest. Why does this matter?","Absent joint attention at 18 months is an early red flag for autism. It should prompt further assessment.",63,11],[305,"What are the three classic cognitive accounts of autism?","Theory of mind deficit, executive dysfunction and weak central coherence.",63,12],[305,"Which aspect of autism does executive dysfunction best explain?","Rigidity and inflexible behaviour.",63,13],[305,"What is weak central coherence and what strengths does it explain?","A processing style favouring detail over the whole (Frith; Happé). It explains strengths such as fast hidden-figure detection, and reading homographs like 'tear' the same way regardless of context.",63,14],[305,"What is the double empathy problem?","The idea that misunderstanding between autistic and non-autistic people runs both ways, not only from the autistic side.",63,15],[305,"Which terms do ICD-11 and DSM-5-TR use for autism?","Both use autism spectrum disorder.",63,16],[306,"An 8-year-old autistic boy does not notice when his mother is upset. Which cognitive account best explains this?","Impaired theory of mind (mindblindness). It explains apparent lack of empathy and missed teasing, which is not true uncaring.",63,17],[308,"A toddler with rouge on her nose touches her own nose in a mirror. At what age is this first typically seen?","At about 18 months, with most children passing by 24 months. Amsterdam (1972) adapted this test from Gallup's chimpanzee study.",64,1],[308,"What did Gallup (1970) show in chimpanzees?","Anaesthetised chimpanzees marked with red dye touched the mark on waking when shown a mirror, demonstrating self-recognition.",64,2],[308,"How do self-concept, self-image and self-esteem differ?","Self-concept is everything you believe about yourself. Self-image is the descriptive part, while self-esteem is the evaluative part, how much you value yourself.",64,3],[308,"What does the Rosenberg Self-Esteem Scale measure and how is it structured?","It measures global self-worth using 10 items rated on a 4-point agreement scale (1965).",64,4],[308,"What is self-efficacy and what is its strongest source?","Bandura's (1977) task-specific belief in one's ability to do something. Its strongest source is personal mastery experience.",64,5],[308,"Besides mastery experiences, what are the other sources of self-efficacy?","Vicarious experience of others succeeding, verbal encouragement and physiological state.",64,6],[308,"According to Allport, which aspect of the self develops first?","The bodily self, in the first year of life.",64,7],[308,"What is Cooley's looking-glass self?","We know ourselves by imagining how others see and judge us (Cooley, 1902).",64,8],[309,"What do Festinger's social comparison theory and Bem's self-perception theory propose?","Festinger (1954): we judge our abilities by comparing with similar people. Bem (1967): we infer our attitudes by observing our own behaviour.",64,9],[309,"What did Rogers mean by incongruence and what causes it?","Incongruence is the gap between self-concept and ideal self. It is created by conditions of worth, where love depends on success.",64,10],[309,"In Higgins's self-discrepancy theory, which emotions follow actual-ideal and actual-ought gaps?","Actual-ideal gaps lead to sadness and dejection. Actual-ought gaps lead to guilt and anxiety.",64,11],[309,"What did Kohut's self psychology say children need to build a solid self?","Selfobjects who provide mirroring and someone to idealise.",64,12],[309,"How did Gallagher (2000) divide the self?","The minimal self is the immediate sense that an experience is mine and I am doing it. The narrative self is the life story built from memory.",64,13],[309,"On what did John Locke base personal identity?","Continuity of consciousness and memory, not the soul or the body.",64,14],[309,"What is ipseity disturbance and in which condition is it described?","Sass and Parnas describe it in early schizophrenia as a breakdown of the basic self. It involves hyperreflexivity and experiences feeling not quite one's own.",64,15],[310,"What is a self-schema according to Markus (1977)?","An organised body of self-knowledge. It speeds matching judgements, supplies supporting memories and resists contrary feedback.",64,16],[310,"A 23-year-old woman feels she watches herself from outside but says 'I know it is me'. What is this and how does ICD-11 classify it?","Depersonalisation, with reality testing intact. ICD-11 places persistent cases as depersonalisation-derealisation disorder within the dissociative disorders.",64,17],[312,"In Lewin, Lippitt and White's (1939) boys' clubs, which leadership style produced the least and poorest work?","Laissez-faire leadership, where the leader stood back and left the boys to it.",65,1],[312,"How did autocratic groups behave in Lewin's boys' club study?","They worked hard only while the leader was present and stopped when he left. They were aggressive or apathetic and picked on a scapegoat.",65,2],[312,"Which of Lewin's styles gave the best morale and quality, and kept working without the leader?","Democratic leadership, where choices were discussed with the boys. These groups also had the most original ideas.",65,3],[312,"What is the great man (trait) theory and what did Stogdill (1948) find?","It holds that leaders are born with special qualities. Stogdill's review found traits poorly predicted effective leadership across settings.",65,4],[312,"What does Fiedler's contingency model (1967) propose and how is style measured?","Leadership success depends on matching style to the situation. Style is measured with the least preferred co-worker (LPC) scale: low LPC is task-oriented, high LPC is relationship-oriented.",65,5],[312,"What makes a situation favourable in Fiedler's model?","Good leader-member relations, a clearly structured task and strong positional authority.",65,6],[313,"What is the persuasive (selling) leadership style?","The leader still makes the decision but explains it and seeks agreement before acting. Hersey and Blanchard place selling between telling and participating.",65,7],[313,"What are French and Raven's (1959) five original bases of social power?","Reward, coercive, legitimate, referent and expert power.",65,8],[313,"A consultant was elected clinical director by colleagues. Which type of power does the post give?","Legitimate power, because it comes from holding a recognised position, even one you were voted into. It is not referent power.",65,9],[313,"How do coercive, referent and expert power differ?","Coercive power rests on punishment and gives compliance only while watched. Referent power comes from being admired and copied, and expert power from specialist knowledge within its field.",65,10],[313,"What sixth power base did Raven (1965) add?","Informational power, where the content of the message itself persuades regardless of who delivers it.",65,11],[313,"What did Zimbardo's Stanford prison experiment (1971) claim to show, and what caveat applies?","Students randomly made guards became abusive and prisoners passive, and it was stopped after six days, showing the power of social roles. Its methods are now heavily criticised.",65,12],[313,"What did Moscovici (1969) show about minority influence?","A consistent minority calling blue slides green shifted about 8% of majority answers, versus about 1% when inconsistent. Minority influence produces private change, called conversion.",65,13],[313,"How does a filter bubble differ from an echo chamber?","A filter bubble (Pariser) is created by algorithms hiding opposing views. An echo chamber is a like-minded network the user has chosen.",65,14],[313,"What did Bakshy (2015) find about exposure to cross-cutting news on social media?","In over ten million users, people's own choices limited exposure to opposing news more than algorithmic ranking did.",65,15],[313,"What did Orben and Przybylski (2019) find about adolescent technology use and wellbeing?","The association was negative but very small, at most 0.4% of the variation, and the direction of cause is uncertain.",65,16],[313,"What are the Werther and Papageno effects?","The Werther effect (Phillips, 1974) is a rise in suicides after vivid reports of a celebrity suicide describing the method. The Papageno effect is the protective effect of stories of coping.",65,17],[313,"A journalist asks a psychiatrist how to report a local suicide. What advice fits the evidence?","Avoid method detail and sensational headlines, and include information on where to get help, to reduce imitation and promote the Papageno effect.",65,18],[314,"A new medical director inspires staff with a vision and attends to each person's growth. Which leadership approach is this?","Transformational leadership (Burns, 1978; Bass, 1985). It contrasts with transactional leadership, which exchanges rewards for effort.",65,19],[315,"In Fiedler's model, which leader suits a crisis with poor relations, no structure and weak authority?","A task-oriented (low LPC) leader, who does best at both extremes of favourability. Relationship-oriented leaders do best in moderate situations.",65,20],[317,"What is groupthink and what are its main signs?","Janis (1972) described a cohesive, insulated group seeking agreement so much that decisions worsen, as in the Bay of Pigs. Signs include illusion of invulnerability, self-censorship, illusion of unanimity and mindguards.",66,1],[317,"What did Sherif's autokinetic study (1935) show?","In a dark room a still light seems to move. Groups converged on a shared norm that individuals kept when alone, showing we use others as a guide on unclear tasks.",66,2],[317,"What were the main findings of Asch's (1951) line-judgement study?","About one third of critical answers conformed to the wrong majority, and 75% of people conformed at least once. Alone, people almost never erred.",66,3],[317,"How does majority size and the presence of an ally affect conformity in Asch's paradigm?","Conformity rises with group size up to about three, then plateaus. One dissenting ally breaks unanimity and conformity falls sharply.",66,4],[317,"Which individual is most likely to conform in a line-judgement study?","A low-status newcomer who is unsure of the task and must answer in public.",66,5],[317,"How do informational and normative social influence differ (Deutsch and Gerard, 1955)?","Informational influence reflects the wish to be right and leads to private acceptance. Normative influence reflects the wish to be liked, giving public agreement with private disagreement.",66,6],[317,"What three levels of conformity did Kelman (1958) describe?","Compliance (public only), identification (adopting views to belong to an admired person or group) and internalisation (truly held, even alone).",66,7],[317,"What is Hollander's idiosyncrasy credit?","Past competence and loyalty earn a respected long-serving member credit that allows them to dissent from the group without rejection.",66,8],[318,"What proportion of Milgram's (1963) participants gave the maximum 450 V shock?","About 65% obeyed to the maximum when ordered by an experimenter in a grey coat.",66,9],[318,"How did Milgram's 1974 variations change obedience levels?","Same-room learner cut it to about 40% and telephone orders to about 20%. It rose to about 92% when the participant only read words while another gave shocks.",66,10],[318,"What is Milgram's agentic state?","People see themselves as instruments of authority and disown personal responsibility for their actions.",66,11],[318,"What did Hofling (1966) find with nurses, and why does it matter?","21 of 22 nurses were about to give twice the maximum dose on a phone order from an unknown doctor. Staff must feel safe to challenge unsafe orders.",66,12],[318,"What is the relationship between risky shift and group polarisation?","Stoner (1961) found groups choose riskier options than members' average. Cautious groups become more cautious, so it was renamed group polarisation (Moscovici and Zavalloni, 1969).",66,13],[318,"What remedies did Janis suggest to prevent groupthink?","The leader withholds her own view, outside critics are invited, and a devil's advocate is appointed.",66,14],[318,"What is deindividuation and what causes it?","Loss of the sense of self in a crowd. Anonymity, arousal and immersion reduce self-awareness and accountability.",66,15],[318,"What did Zimbardo (1969) and Diener (1976) show about anonymity?","Hooded, unnamed women gave shocks about twice as long as named women. Anonymous trick-or-treaters in groups took the most sweets; Diener stressed reduced self-awareness.",66,16],[319,"How does obedience differ from conformity?","Obedience follows an explicit order from an authority. Conformity follows peers with no direct order.",66,17],[319,"A ward team's modest smoking views become a total site ban after discussion. Which process is this and what drives it?","Group polarisation: discussion pushes a group further in the direction it already leaned. It is driven by persuasive new arguments and social comparison.",66,18],[320,"A 24-year-old female medical student copies peers' answers believing they know more, then genuinely believes them. What influence is this?","Informational social influence, which produces private acceptance and internalisation.",66,19],[322,"A mother asks her son for a hug, stiffens when he does, then calls him cold. What is this and its status?","A double bind (Bateson and colleagues, 1956). It was proposed as a cause of schizophrenia but research did not support this, so it is a historical theory.",67,1],[322,"What are the components of a double bind?","A primary message, a contradictory second message often given non-verbally, and an inability to comment on the clash or leave.",67,2],[322,"What is Watzlawick's first axiom of communication?","One cannot not communicate: silence or turning away still sends a message (Watzlawick, Beavin and Jackson, 1967).",67,3],[322,"What are Watzlawick's other four axioms of communication?","Messages have content and relationship levels; each side punctuates the sequence differently; communication is digital (words) and analogic (body language); exchanges are symmetrical or complementary.",67,4],[322,"In which therapy are Watzlawick's axioms central?","Systemic family therapy.",67,5],[323,"Which family factor does have good evidence in schizophrenia, and for which outcome?","High expressed emotion, which raises the risk of relapse rather than first onset.",67,6],[323,"What are Berne's three ego states in transactional analysis?","Parent, Adult and Child (Berne, 1964).",67,7],[323,"How do complementary and ulterior transactions differ from crossed transactions?","In a complementary transaction the reply comes from the addressed ego state, so talk flows. Ulterior transactions carry hidden messages and drive psychological games.",67,8],[323,"What does social penetration theory (Altman and Taylor, 1973) propose?","People are like onions with safe outer layers and private inner ones. Closeness grows as self-disclosure increases in breadth and depth, usually reciprocally.",67,9],[323,"What is depenetration?","The shrinking of self-disclosure as a relationship fades.",67,10],[323,"What is active listening as described by Rogers and Farson (1957)?","Hearing both facts and feelings and reflecting them back without advice or judgement. It includes reflection of feeling, paraphrasing, summarising and open questions.",67,11],[323,"What are Gottman's four horsemen?","Criticism, contempt, defensiveness and stonewalling, which predicted divorce.",67,12],[323,"Which of Gottman's four horsemen is the strongest single predictor of divorce?","Contempt, such as eye-rolling, sneering and mockery from superiority. Anger alone did not predict divorce.",67,13],[323,"What is stonewalling and what usually precedes it?","Shutting down by going silent, looking away and not responding. It often follows flooding, an overwhelming state of arousal, and is more common in men.",67,14],[323,"What ratio of positive to negative moments do stable couples maintain during conflict?","About five positive moments to every negative one.",67,15],[323,"What are Gottman's antidotes to the four horsemen?","A gentle start-up for criticism, appreciation for contempt, taking responsibility for defensiveness, and self-soothing with a break for stonewalling.",67,16],[324,"A 15-year-old boy sits in silence during family therapy. Which axiom of communication does this illustrate?","One cannot not communicate. His silence still tells his parents something.",67,17],[324,"A husband asks factually where his keys are and his wife snaps back like a scolding parent. What has occurred?","A crossed transaction, where the reply comes from an unexpected ego state and conversation breaks down.",67,18],[327,"A trainee believes all elderly patients are hard of hearing and slow. What is this belief called?","A stereotype: a mental shortcut attributing shared traits to a whole group. It is the cognitive part of prejudice and leads to discrimination when acted upon.",68,1],[327,"What is social loafing and who first showed it?","Reduced individual effort on a shared task when one's own input cannot be seen. Ringelmann found men pulled less hard on a rope as teams grew; Latane (1979) replicated it with clapping.",68,2],[327,"What best reduces social loafing?","Making each person's contribution identifiable. Small, close groups and meaningful tasks also help (Karau and Williams, 1993).",68,3],[327,"Who ran the first social psychology experiment and what did it show?","Triplett (1898), after noticing cyclists rode faster against others, found children wound fishing reels faster in company. This is social facilitation.",68,4],[327,"How does Zajonc's drive theory explain social facilitation?","An audience raises arousal, which improves performance on well-learned tasks but impairs new or difficult ones.",68,5],[327,"What is the Hawthorne effect and why does it matter in clinical audit?","People change their behaviour because they know they are being watched. Audits should use control groups because observation alone can change practice.",68,6],[327,"What are the five stages of Allport's (1954) scale of prejudice?","Antilocution, avoidance, discrimination, physical attack and extermination.",68,7],[327,"What are illusory correlation and the outgroup homogeneity effect?","Illusory correlation (Hamilton and Gifford, 1976) is wrongly linking a rare act with a small group because both stand out. Outgroup homogeneity makes 'them' seem all alike while 'we' seem varied.",68,8],[328,"What is Adorno's authoritarian personality and its main criticism?","Harsh, rigid parenting produces deference to strong leaders and contempt for minorities, measured by the F-scale. Critics say it ignores social norms.",68,9],[328,"What is scapegoating?","Displacing frustration onto a weaker group. Hovland and Sears linked falling cotton prices with more lynchings.",68,10],[328,"What does realistic conflict theory propose?","Sherif argued that competition for scarce resources, such as jobs, creates intergroup hostility.",68,11],[328,"In the Robbers Cave study, what reduced hostility between the groups?","Simple contact failed. Hostility fell only with superordinate goals needing both groups to cooperate, such as fixing the water supply.",68,12],[328,"What is stereotype threat?","People perform worse on a test when it is framed as measuring a trait their group is negatively stereotyped on (Steele and Aronson, 1995).",68,13],[328,"What did Jane Elliott's blue eyes, brown eyes exercise (1968) show?","Children told their eye colour was inferior quickly became withdrawn and worked worse, showing the harm of labelling.",68,14],[328,"What four conditions does Allport's contact hypothesis require to reduce prejudice?","Equal status within the activity, common goals, cooperation and support from authorities. Pettigrew and Tropp (2006) confirmed it.",68,15],[328,"What is Aronson's jigsaw classroom and how does it link to anti-stigma work?","Each pupil holds one essential piece of the lesson, so all must cooperate. Anti-stigma campaigns like Time to Change in England similarly used equal-status contact.",68,16],[329,"A 50-year-old man feels disgust towards migrants but has never acted on it. Which component of prejudice is this?","The affective component. In the tripartite model, stereotypes are cognitive, feelings affective and discrimination behavioural.",68,17],[329,"Colleagues regularly share jokes mocking a religious minority but take no action. Which stage of Allport's scale is this?","Antilocution, hostile talk among like-minded people. It is the first stage and makes later stages easier.",68,18],[331,"Boys split by preference for Klee or Kandinsky favour their own group in sharing points. What does this show?","The minimal group paradigm (Tajfel, 1971): mere categorisation is enough to cause ingroup bias, even without meeting members.",69,1],[331,"What is sociometry and who founded it?","Moreno (1934) founded sociometry, the measurement of who likes whom. He also created psychodrama.",69,2],[331,"How is a sociogram produced?","Each member names the people they would most like to spend time with, and arrows between names map these choices.",69,3],[331,"In a sociogram, how do a star and an isolate differ?","A star receives many choices. An isolate gives and receives none.",69,4],[331,"Apart from stars and isolates, what else does a sociogram reveal, and how is it useful on a ward?","It shows mutual pairs and cliques. Staff can use it to spot patients who are left out and at risk.",69,5],[331,"How did Bion describe the two levels at which groups work?","The work group pursues the real task, while an unconscious basic assumption group gets in the way (Bion, 1961).",69,6],[331,"What are Bion's three basic assumptions?","Dependency, fight-flight and pairing.",69,7],[331,"A therapy group sits helplessly waiting for the therapist to rescue them. Which basic assumption is this?","Basic assumption dependency, where members wait for an all-knowing leader.",69,8],[331,"What characterises the fight-flight and pairing basic assumptions?","In fight-flight the group finds an enemy to attack or flee from. In pairing it hopes two members will produce a future saviour.",69,9],[331,"How did Bion address the group in therapy?","He interpreted to the group as a whole, treating the group itself as the patient.",69,10],[332,"In Tajfel's minimal group studies, how did boys allocate points?","They favoured their own group even at a cost, often maximising the gap between groups rather than total points gained.",69,11],[332,"What are the three steps in social identity theory (Tajfel and Turner, 1979)?","Social categorisation, social identification and social comparison that favours our own group. This comparison boosts self-esteem.",69,12],[332,"What did Foulkes mean by the matrix and the conductor?","The matrix is the web of communication and relationships, with each member a nodal point. The therapist is a conductor who guides rather than directs.",69,13],[332,"What is mirroring in Foulkesian group analysis?","Members learn by seeing parts of themselves reflected in others.",69,14],[332,"What are Foulkes's four levels of group communication?","Current, transference, projective and primordial, the deepest level of shared universal symbols.",69,15],[332,"Which group concepts belong to Moreno, Bion and Foulkes respectively?","Moreno: sociogram and psychodrama. Bion: basic assumptions and valency. Foulkes: matrix, conductor and mirroring.",69,16],[333,"What is Bion's concept of valency?","A person's instinctive readiness to combine with a particular basic assumption mood, borrowed from chemistry.",69,17],[333,"A 30-year-old woman in a group says 'I thought I was the only one who felt like this'. Which Yalom factor is this?","Universality, the relief of finding others share one's problems.",69,18],[333,"Name several of Yalom's eleven therapeutic factors.","They include instillation of hope, universality, imparting information, altruism, corrective recapitulation of the primary family group, socialising techniques, imitative behaviour, interpersonal learning, cohesiveness, catharsis and existential factors.",69,19],[335,"How is aggression defined in social psychology?","Aggression is behaviour intended to harm another person. It arises from a mix of instinct, frustration, learning from others and the situation.",70,1],[335,"How does instrumental aggression differ from hostile aggression?","Instrumental aggression uses harm as a means to gain something such as money or status and is usually cold and planned. Hostile aggression is hot and angry, and hurting the victim is the goal itself.",70,2],[335,"A 28-year-old man mocked in a pub punches the stranger and gains nothing but release of anger. What type of aggression is this?","Hostile, or reactive, aggression. It is the type most linked to frustration and to impulsivity associated with low serotonin.",70,3],[335,"What other name is used for instrumental aggression, and what is a typical example?","It is also called proactive aggression. A robber who strikes a shop assistant to get the till money is a classic example.",70,4],[335,"How did Freud explain aggression in his 1920 instinct theory?","He proposed a death instinct, Thanatos, which is turned outwards onto other people as aggression.",70,5],[335,"What is Lorenz's hydraulic model of aggression?","Lorenz saw aggression as an innate drive whose energy builds up like water in a reservoir until a trigger releases it. It has little support in humans.",70,6],[335,"What is an appeasement display, as described by Lorenz in wolves?","The losing wolf rolls over and exposes its throat, and the winner stops attacking. Such rituals prevent killing within a species.",70,7],[335,"What did the frustration-aggression hypothesis of Dollard and colleagues (1939) propose, and how did Miller modify it?","It claimed frustration, the blocking of a goal, always leads to aggression. Miller (1941) softened this to say frustration tends to lead to aggression.",70,8],[336,"What is displaced aggression and how robust is the evidence for it?","Anger is redirected from the frustrating source onto a safer target, such as a son instead of the boss. A meta-analysis of 49 studies showed it is a reliable, replicated effect.",70,9],[336,"How did Berkowitz revise the frustration-aggression hypothesis?","He said frustration produces anger, but cues in the environment trigger the aggressive act itself.",70,10],[336,"What is the weapons effect described by Berkowitz and LePage (1967)?","Angered students gave more electric shocks when a gun lay nearby than when a badminton racket did. Aggressive cues increase aggression.",70,11],[336,"What did Bandura's Bobo doll studies show about social learning of aggression?","Nursery children who watched an adult hit an inflatable doll copied the same acts. Aggression can be learned by observing models.",70,12],[336,"In Bandura's 1965 study, what happened when children saw the model punished and were then offered a reward?","They copied less after seeing punishment, but copied just as well when rewarded. Punishment reduced performance, not learning.",70,13],[336,"What did Eron and Huesmann (1972) find about television violence?","Boys who preferred violent television at age eight were more aggressive at nineteen.",70,14],[336,"Does venting anger, for example hitting a punchbag, reduce aggression?","No. Bushman (2002) showed venting makes aggression more likely, so the catharsis idea fails and patients should not be advised to vent on objects.",70,15],[336,"What is excitation transfer theory?","Zillmann (1971) proposed that leftover arousal from an unrelated source, such as exercise, adds to anger at a later provocation.",70,16],[336,"How do the heat hypothesis and alcohol myopia explain aggression?","Violence rises on hot days (Anderson). Alcohol narrows attention onto the provocation and away from reasons to hold back (Steele and Josephs).",70,17],[336,"What did Sapolsky's studies of wild baboons show about aggression?","Unstable hierarchies raised stress hormones and fighting. After the most aggressive males died, the troop stayed peaceful for years, so low aggression can spread culturally.",70,18],[336,"What is the strongest single predictor of violence on a psychiatric ward?","A history of previous violence. Past violence weighs most in tools such as the HCR-20, and hot, crowded, noisy wards raise risk.",70,19],[339,"What is the bystander effect?","The more people who witness an emergency, the less likely any one person is to help. It was studied by Latane and Darley after the 1964 Kitty Genovese case.",71,1],[339,"Why is bystander apathy considered a poor term?","Bystanders are often anxious and uncertain rather than uncaring, so apathy misdescribes them.",71,2],[339,"What were the helping rates in Darley and Latane's 1968 intercom seizure study?","About 85% of those who thought they were alone helped before the seizure ended, compared with about 31% who believed four others could hear.",71,3],[339,"What is diffusion of responsibility?","When more potential helpers are present, each feels less personal duty to act. It operates at the assume responsibility step of the decision model.",71,4],[339,"What is pluralistic ignorance and which study demonstrated it?","People read others' calm as a sign that there is no emergency. In the smoke-filled room study about 75% alone reported smoke, but only about 10% with two calm strangers.",71,5],[339,"What are the five steps of Latane and Darley's model of bystander intervention?","Notice the event, interpret it as an emergency, assume personal responsibility, decide how to help, then act. Failure at any step prevents help.",71,6],[339,"A patient collapses in a busy hospital corridor. How should an F2 doctor overcome the bystander effect?","Point at one specific person and give a clear task, such as asking the person in the green coat to call the crash team.",71,7],[339,"What did Darley and Batson's Good Samaritan study (1973) find?","Being in a hurry was the best predictor of not stopping for a slumped man. About 63% of unhurried trainee ministers helped, against 10% of those rushing.",71,8],[339,"Why are people with first-aid training more likely to help in an emergency?","They feel competent to act, which increases helping (Huston, 1981).",71,9],[340,"What did Piliavin's 1969 subway study find?","A collapsed man with a cane was helped almost every time even in crowds, while a man smelling of drink was helped about half as often. There was little diffusion of responsibility in a clear, real emergency.",71,10],[340,"What does the arousal: cost-reward model propose?","Seeing distress causes unpleasant arousal, and the bystander chooses the response that reduces it at the least net cost.",71,11],[340,"How does the empathy-altruism hypothesis differ from the negative state relief model?","Batson argued strong empathic concern produces genuinely selfless helping. Cialdini argued we help mainly to relieve our own low mood, so helping is self-interested.",71,12],[340,"What is kin selection and what is Hamilton's rule?","Helping relatives passes on shared genes (Hamilton, 1964). Altruism is favoured when relatedness multiplied by benefit exceeds cost, written rB greater than C.",71,13],[340,"What is the coefficient of relatedness between siblings and between first cousins?","Siblings share r of 0.5 and first cousins 0.125.",71,14],[340,"Ground squirrels give risky alarm calls more often when close relatives are nearby. Which explanation fits best?","Kin selection. Sherman (1977) showed Belding's ground squirrels called more when kin were near, so help rises with relatedness.",71,15],[340,"What is reciprocal altruism and what does it require?","Trivers (1971) described helping non-relatives when the favour is likely to be returned, as in vampire bats sharing blood. It needs repeated contact and a way to detect cheats.",71,16],[340,"What does social exchange theory say about why people stay in relationships?","Thibaut and Kelley proposed we stay while rewards outweigh costs compared with available alternatives. Someone may remain in an unhappy marriage if leaving looks worse.",71,17],[340,"How does equity theory differ from social exchange theory?","Equity theory says we want a similar ratio of input to outcome for both partners. It predicts distress in the over-benefited partner (guilt) as well as the under-benefited partner (anger).",71,18],[344,"A new trainee hears that the ward sister thinks highly of him and starts to like her. Which principle explains this?","Reciprocity of liking. We like people who we believe like us, because liking signals approval and reward (Backman and Secord, 1959).",72,1],[344,"What did Festinger, Schachter and Back (1950) find in their study of student housing at MIT?","Physical proximity was the strongest predictor of friendship, with next-door neighbours most often becoming friends.",72,2],[344,"What is functional distance?","How often people's daily routes cross, rather than raw physical distance. Residents near stairs and mailboxes made more friends.",72,3],[344,"What is the mere exposure effect?","Zajonc (1968) showed that repeated exposure to an unfamiliar stimulus increases liking for it. It helps explain why proximity breeds friendship.",72,4],[344,"What did Newcomb's 1961 study of male students in free housing show?","Early friendships formed with those living nearest, but by the end of term friends were those with similar pre-measured attitudes.",72,5],[344,"What most consistently predicts long-term attraction and relationship stability?","Similarity of attitudes and values. Byrne showed liking rises with the proportion of shared attitudes, and complementarity has weaker support.",72,6],[344,"What did Walster's 1966 computer dance study find?","Wanting another date was best predicted by the partner's physical attractiveness, regardless of one's own looks.",72,7],[345,"What is the matching hypothesis?","Established couples tend to be closely matched in physical attractiveness, possibly balancing desirability against fear of rejection. Murstein (1972) supported it.",72,8],[345,"How can the computer dance findings be reconciled with the matching hypothesis?","Attractiveness matters most for wanting a first date, whereas matching describes established couples.",72,9],[345,"What is the 'what is beautiful is good' effect?","Dion, Berscheid and Walster (1972) found attractive faces were rated as kinder and more successful. This halo effect can bias clinical and legal judgements.",72,10],[345,"What are the three components of love in Sternberg's triangular theory?","Intimacy, passion and commitment. Combining them gives eight types of love.",72,11],[345,"In Sternberg's model, what is companionate love?","Love with intimacy and commitment but little passion, typical of a long marriage because passion usually fades first.",72,12],[345,"How does companionate love differ from romantic love in Sternberg's model?","Companionate love lacks passion, while romantic love lacks commitment.",72,13],[345,"What three factors determine commitment in Rusbult's investment model?","Satisfaction, the quality of alternatives and the size of investments, such as years together, a home and shared friends.",72,14],[345,"A 40-year-old woman stays in an unhappy relationship despite low satisfaction. How does the investment model explain this?","High investment and poor alternatives can keep a person committed even when satisfaction is low.",72,15],[345,"What does the complementarity of needs hypothesis propose, and how well is it supported?","It proposes that people with opposite needs attract. Its support is weaker and less consistent than for similarity.",72,16],[346,"Which effect explains why an initially strange word becomes better liked the more often it is seen?","The mere exposure effect described by Zajonc, which needs repeated contact rather than any news of approval.",72,17],[348,"What did the Whitehall studies of British civil servants show?","Mortality, heart disease and poor mental health rose at each step down the employment grades, even though none were poor. This is the social gradient in health.",73,1],[348,"What did Marmot call the pattern found in the Whitehall studies, and what was one key explanation?","He called it the status syndrome. Low control at work was one important explanation.",73,2],[348,"How did Karl Marx define social class?","By relationship to the means of production: whether you own resources such as factories or sell your labour.",73,3],[348,"What did Marx mean by alienation?","Under capitalism the worker is cut off from the product of his work, from fellow workers and from his own creativity.",73,4],[348,"What three dimensions of stratification did Max Weber describe?","Economic class, social status and political power, which he called party.",73,5],[348,"How did the Registrar General's scheme classify social class?","By occupation, from Class I professional to Class V unskilled manual, with Class III split into non-manual and manual.",73,6],[348,"A 45-year-old hospital porter works in an unskilled manual job. Which Registrar General class is he?","Class V, unskilled manual work.",73,7],[348,"What replaced the Registrar General's scheme from the 2001 census, and on what basis does it classify people?","The National Statistics Socio-economic Classification (NS-SEC). It sorts people by employment relations and conditions, such as employer or employee.",73,8],[348,"What four census variables make up the Townsend deprivation index?","Unemployment, no car, not owning your home and household overcrowding.",73,9],[348,"What was the Jarman Underprivileged Area score used for?","It weighted census items by how much GPs said they increased workload, and guided extra funding for GPs in deprived areas.",73,10],[349,"What is the Index of Multiple Deprivation?","It ranks every small area of England on seven weighted domains, with income and employment weighted most heavily.",73,11],[349,"Which deprivation measures describe areas and which describe individuals?","Townsend, Jarman and the Index of Multiple Deprivation describe areas. The Registrar General's scheme and NS-SEC describe individuals by job.",73,12],[349,"What did Faris and Dunham (1939) find in Chicago?","First admissions for schizophrenia were highest in the poor city centre and fell towards the suburbs. This was taken as support for social causation.",73,13],[349,"What did Goldberg and Morrison (1963) find, and which hypothesis does it support?","Men with schizophrenia were mostly in the lowest class but their fathers had a normal class spread. This supports social drift, where illness causes downward mobility.",73,14],[349,"Which psychiatric disorders cluster in lower social classes, and which has been linked to higher class of origin?","Schizophrenia, substance misuse and antisocial personality disorder cluster in lower classes. Bipolar disorder was linked to higher class of origin in older studies.",73,15],[349,"What is the inverse care law?","Julian Tudor Hart (1971) noted that good care is least available where it is most needed, and this worsens where market forces rule.",73,16],[349,"What did the Black Report (1980) show?","A social class gradient in health persisted despite the NHS.",73,17],[349,"What is proportionate universalism, as urged by the Marmot Review (2010)?","Services and help should be universal but scaled in intensity according to need.",73,18],[352,"What theoretical framework did Engel use for his 1977 biopsychosocial model?","General systems theory, viewing health as a hierarchy of linked systems from cells to person to family and society.",74,1],[352,"How did Parsons (1951) view illness in his description of the sick role?","As a form of deviance that society permits under certain rules, with two rights and two duties.",74,2],[352,"What are the two rights of Parsons' sick role?","Exemption from normal duties such as work, and not being held to blame for the illness.",74,3],[352,"What are the two duties of Parsons' sick role?","The person must want to get well, and must seek competent help and cooperate with it.",74,4],[352,"A 50-year-old man with a stroke refuses all doctors and misses physiotherapy. Which sick role element does he break?","The duty to seek competent help and cooperate with treatment.",74,5],[352,"What is the main criticism of Parsons' sick role?","It fits chronic illness and disability poorly because it was built on short, curable illness.",74,6],[352,"What is illness behaviour, as described by Mechanic (1962)?","The varied ways people perceive, evaluate and act on symptoms. It does not depend on whether disease is found.",74,7],[352,"What factors influence illness behaviour?","How visible and serious a symptom seems, how much it disrupts life, personal tolerance, knowledge and access to care.",74,8],[352,"A 55-year-old man remains sure he has heart disease despite normal tests and careful explanation. Which concept fits?","Abnormal illness behaviour, described by Pilowsky (1969) and measured with the Illness Behaviour Questionnaire.",74,9],[353,"How do sociologists distinguish disease, illness and sickness?","Disease is the bodily pathology, illness is the person's experience of being unwell, and sickness is the social role granted by society.",74,10],[353,"Give an example of disease without illness.","Silent high blood pressure, where pathology exists but the person feels well.",74,11],[353,"Did Engel reject the biomedical model?","No. He challenged its reductionism and dualism but said biology is necessary though not sufficient.",74,12],[353,"What are Szasz and Hollender's three models of the doctor-patient relationship?","Active-passive for an unconscious patient, guidance-cooperation for acute illness, and mutual participation for chronic illness such as diabetes.",74,13],[353,"What approach best suits a 35-year-old man with long-term schizophrenia who often disengages?","A mutual participation, long-term relationship built on his strengths and interests rather than demanding passive compliance.",74,14],[353,"In the health belief model, what is a cue to action?","A trigger prompting health behaviour, such as a reminder letter or a friend's diagnosis.",74,15],[353,"What is professional dominance according to Freidson (1970)?","Medicine controls its own work and decides what counts as illness.",74,16],[353,"What is medicalisation?","The process by which normal life experiences, such as shyness, birth or grief, become treated as medical problems (Zola, Illich).",74,17],[354,"How do malingering and factitious disorder differ from illness behaviour?","Both involve deliberate production of symptoms, whereas illness behaviour simply describes how people respond to symptoms.",74,18],[356,"What is expressed emotion (EE)?","The attitudes and feelings relatives show when talking about a family member with mental illness. It was linked to relapse by Brown, Birley and Wing (1972).",75,1],[356,"What observation by Brown in the 1950s led to research on expressed emotion?","Men with schizophrenia discharged to parents or wives relapsed more often than those discharged to lodgings.",75,2],[356,"What three components define a high-EE household?","Critical comments, hostility and emotional over-involvement.",75,3],[356,"What is emotional over-involvement?","Excessive self-sacrifice, overprotection and exaggerated emotional reactions, such as a mother giving up her job and saying she cannot live without her son.",75,4],[356,"Are warmth and positive remarks part of the definition of high EE?","No. They are rated in the interview but do not define high EE.",75,5],[356,"What is the gold standard measure of expressed emotion and how is it carried out?","The Camberwell Family Interview, a semi-structured audio-recorded interview with the key relative without the patient. It rates content and tone and takes one to two hours.",75,6],[356,"What is the Five Minute Speech Sample?","A brief EE measure in which a relative speaks about the patient uninterrupted for five minutes and the recording is rated. It is cheaper but classifies fewer relatives as high EE.",75,7],[357,"What nine-month relapse rates did Vaughn and Leff (1976) find for high versus low EE?","About 50% with high-EE relatives compared with about 12% with low-EE relatives.",75,8],[357,"In Vaughn and Leff's study, which combination gave the highest relapse rate?","High EE, more than 35 hours a week of face-to-face contact and no medication, giving relapse of about 92%.",75,9],[357,"Which two factors protect against relapse in patients living with high-EE relatives?","Taking medication and reducing face-to-face contact to under 35 hours a week.",75,10],[357,"How does sensitivity to criticism in depression compare with schizophrenia?","Depressed patients relapse at lower levels of critical comments, so they are more sensitive to criticism.",75,11],[357,"Apart from schizophrenia and depression, which disorders does EE predict outcome in?","Bipolar disorder and eating disorders.",75,12],[357,"Where is high EE least common, and why might this matter?","In rural north India, as found in WHO studies in Chandigarh. It may partly explain the better outcome of schizophrenia there.",75,13],[357,"A 24-year-old man with schizophrenia is well on the ward but relapses on each home leave with a critical father. What is the next step?","Offer family intervention, including psychoeducation, framed as support rather than blame.",75,14],[357,"What does NICE recommend for family intervention in psychosis?","At least 10 planned sessions over 3 months to 1 year, including the service user where possible.",75,15],[357,"What was the schizophrenogenic mother theory?","Frieda Fromm-Reichmann (1948) blamed cold, domineering yet overprotective mothers for schizophrenia. It has no empirical support and is discredited.",75,16],[357,"What are the double bind and pseudomutuality theories?","Bateson's double bind (1956) blamed contradictory messages with no escape. Wynne's pseudomutuality (1958) described false family harmony hiding conflict. Neither is a proven cause.",75,17],[357,"Who described marital schism and marital skew?","Theodore Lidz.",75,18],[357,"What was the refrigerator mother theory and why is it rejected?","Bettelheim popularised the idea that cold mothers caused autism. It is rejected because autism is highly heritable.",75,19],[357,"How much is the risk of depression raised in a child whose mother has recurrent depression?","About two to three times, through both genetic and environmental factors.",75,20],[360,"What did Durkheim argue in Suicide (1897)?","Using national statistics, he showed suicide rates are stable within each society year on year, so they are a social fact belonging to society, not just individuals.",76,1],[360,"What two social dimensions underlie Durkheim's four types of suicide?","Integration, how tied a person is to a group, and regulation, how much rules limit a person's life.",76,2],[360,"A 70-year-old isolated widower with few social ties dies by suicide. Which Durkheim type fits?","Egoistic suicide, caused by too little integration.",76,3],[360,"Which Durkheim type of suicide is caused by too much integration?","Altruistic suicide, such as a soldier dying for his unit.",76,4],[360,"What is anomic suicide?","Suicide from too little regulation, as after a sudden economic crash or sudden wealth. Anomie means normlessness.",76,5],[360,"Which Durkheim type fits suicide among people in harsh labour camps?","Fatalistic suicide, caused by excessive regulation with blocked futures.",76,6],[360,"What did Wilkinson and Pickett find about income and mental illness in rich countries?","Average income did not predict mental illness, but inequality did. They blamed status anxiety and low trust, though the country-level data limit causal claims.",76,7],[360,"How did Lund and colleagues describe the link between poverty and common mental disorder?","As a bidirectional cycle: social causation, where hardship causes illness, and social selection, where illness causes poverty.",76,8],[361,"What is social capital?","Putnam's term for the trust, mutual help and networks within a community. Cognitive social capital, trust and belonging, matters more than group membership.",76,9],[361,"What did Pedersen and Mortensen show about urban upbringing and schizophrenia?","More childhood years in a city gave a dose-response rise in later schizophrenia risk, up to almost three times.",76,10],[361,"Why can social drift not explain the urban effect on schizophrenia?","The exposure is in childhood, years before illness begins.",76,11],[361,"How does hot weather affect mental health?","Hotter days bring more suicides and more mental health emergency presentations, as Burke and colleagues showed in the USA and Mexico.",76,12],[361,"Why should a 60-year-old woman on lithium be reviewed during a heatwave?","Dehydration can make lithium toxic. Some antipsychotics also impair body temperature control.",76,13],[361,"What is solastalgia and how does it differ from eco-anxiety?","Solastalgia, coined by Albrecht, is distress when your own home land changes around you. Eco-anxiety is worry about future climate threats without direct exposure and is not a mental disorder.",76,14],[361,"Which alcohol policies most effectively reduce alcohol harm?","Raising price works best, followed by limiting where and when alcohol is sold. Education and warning labels change drinking little.",76,15],[361,"What is minimum unit pricing and where was it introduced in the UK?","A floor price per unit of alcohol. Scotland introduced it in 2018.",76,16],[361,"What proportion of people in conflict-affected settings have a mental disorder?","About one in five (22%) have depression, anxiety, PTSD, bipolar disorder or schizophrenia (Charlson). Most survivors do not develop a disorder.",76,17],[361,"How did COVID-19 affect global rates of depression and anxiety in 2020?","Major depression and anxiety rose by about a quarter worldwide.",76,18],[365,"How did Holmes and Rahe (1967) measure life stress?","The Social Readjustment Rating Scale gave each event a fixed score in life change units, with death of a spouse scoring highest, and scores were summed.",77,1],[365,"What is the main weakness of life event checklists?","They ignore the meaning of an event, which can differ greatly between people.",77,2],[365,"Who designed the Life Events and Difficulties Schedule and what kind of instrument is it?","Brown and Harris. It is a semi-structured interview in which trained raters judge events in context, with careful dating and anchored examples.",77,3],[365,"Which women had the most depression in Brown and Harris's Camberwell study (1978)?","Working-class women with young children.",77,4],[365,"What are provoking agents in Brown and Harris's social origins model?","Severe life events and major ongoing difficulties that determine when depression begins.",77,5],[365,"What are Brown and Harris's four vulnerability factors for depression?","No confiding relationship, three or more children under 14 at home, loss of mother before age 11, and no paid job outside the home.",77,6],[365,"When do vulnerability factors increase the risk of depression?","Only in the presence of a provoking agent such as a severe life event.",77,7],[365,"What are symptom formation factors in the social origins model?","Factors that shape the severity and form of depression, such as past loss of someone by death being linked to more severe depression.",77,8],[365,"A 32-year-old woman's husband is made redundant and the family faces eviction. What is this in the social origins model?","A provoking agent, specifically a severe event with major ongoing difficulty.",77,9],[366,"What is contextual threat in the LEDS?","The threat an event would pose to a typical person in those circumstances, ignoring the person's own reaction.",77,10],[366,"Why is the person's own reaction excluded when rating contextual threat?","Depressed people tend to describe events as worse, so including their view would bias the results.",77,11],[366,"What is the difference between independent and dependent life events?","An independent event could not be caused by the person or illness, such as a factory closure. A dependent event may result from the illness, such as dismissal due to poor concentration.",77,12],[366,"Why are dependent events kept separate in life events research?","To guard against reverse causation, where early illness causes the event.",77,13],[366,"What did Brown and Birley (1968) show about life events and schizophrenia?","Using independent events, they showed events cluster in the weeks before onset or relapse of schizophrenia.",77,14],[366,"Which kinds of event carry the highest risk of depression?","Events involving humiliation or entrapment, such as being shamed by a partner's affair and feeling stuck, carry more risk than danger or loss alone.",77,15],[366,"What did Finlay-Jones and Brown (1981) find about types of event and disorder?","Loss events led more to depression, while danger events led more to anxiety.",77,16],[366,"What is a matching event?","An event that hits an area of strong commitment or an existing difficulty, making depression more likely.",77,17],[367,"Is chronic physical illness one of Brown and Harris's vulnerability factors?","No. Physical illness was not among them, and paid work outside the home was protective.",77,18],[369,"A 26-year-old man with schizophrenia has seven brief admissions in two years, each followed by stopping medication and relapse. What is this pattern called?","The revolving door pattern, which became more visible after bed closures. Assertive outreach and crisis teams aim to break the cycle.",78,1],[369,"What is Goffman's total institution?","A closed setting where people sleep, work and relax in one place under one authority, in batches on a fixed timetable, cut off from society with a sharp staff-inmate divide.",78,2],[369,"Apart from mental hospitals, give examples of total institutions.","Prisons, barracks, monasteries and boarding schools.",78,3],[369,"What is the betrayal funnel?","Goffman's term for family and professionals steering a person step by step into hospital, such as a short check-up becoming admission.",78,4],[369,"What are mortification of self and role stripping?","Admission rituals that break down the old identity. In role stripping, clothes, name, possessions and former roles are removed.",78,5],[369,"What did Goffman mean by moral career?","The staged change in how patients see themselves and are seen by others during institutional life.",78,6],[369,"What four modes of adaptation to institutions did Goffman describe?","Situational withdrawal, the intransigent line, colonisation and conversion.",78,7],[369,"How does colonisation differ from conversion?","In colonisation the patient makes the ward a contented home and dreads discharge. In conversion the patient adopts the staff view and acts as the perfect inmate.",78,8],[369,"What is institutional neurosis and who described it?","Russell Barton (1959) described apathy, submissiveness, loss of initiative, stooped posture and no thought of the future, caused by the institution rather than the illness.",78,9],[369,"What causes of institutional neurosis did Barton identify?","Enforced idleness, bossy staff, loss of outside contact, friends and possessions, sedating drugs and lack of prospects.",78,10],[370,"What did Wing and Brown (1970) find in women with schizophrenia in three English hospitals?","Fewer possessions and more time doing nothing went with worse negative symptoms, and improving the ward reduced them. Overstimulation risked relapse, so balance matters.",78,11],[370,"What is deskilling in institutionalisation?","Loss of everyday skills such as shopping, cooking, budgeting or using a bus after years on a ward.",78,12],[370,"What is negotiated order and who described it?","Strauss described ward rules on leave, phones and visits being bargained daily between staff and patients.",78,13],[370,"What was the significance of Enoch Powell's Water Tower speech?","In 1961 the Minister of Health called for the asylums to be run down, starting deinstitutionalisation in England.",78,14],[370,"Which Act established care in the community in the UK?","The NHS and Community Care Act 1990.",78,15],[370,"What did Italy's Law 180 (1978) do?","Inspired by Franco Basaglia, it barred new asylum admissions and set up small units in general hospitals.",78,16],[370,"What is Penrose's law?","Penrose (1939) proposed that psychiatric bed numbers and prison numbers move in opposite directions. Later data support it only loosely.",78,17],[370,"What is transinstitutionalisation?","The movement of people from asylums into other institutions such as prisons, hostels or nursing homes.",78,18],[373,"What is the difference between criminology and penology?","Criminology studies why people break the law. Penology studies the purposes of punishment and whether it works.",79,1],[373,"What did Beccaria's classical school propose about crime and punishment?","Offenders freely weigh pleasure against pain, so punishment should be certain, swift and proportionate rather than cruel.",79,2],[373,"According to the classical school, what deters crime more than a harsh sentence?","Certainty of being caught.",79,3],[373,"What did Lombroso claim about born criminals?","He founded the positivist school (1876), claiming some offenders were atavistic throwbacks with physical signs such as a sloping forehead.",79,4],[373,"How was Lombroso's theory refuted, and what lasting idea did positivism leave?","Goring compared prisoners with non-offenders and found no such physical differences. The lasting idea is that crime has causes that can be studied scientifically.",79,5],[373,"What does Merton's strain theory (1938) propose?","Society urges everyone to pursue wealth but blocks legitimate means for many, producing strain.",79,6],[373,"What are Merton's five adaptations to strain?","Conformity, innovation, ritualism, retreatism and rebellion.",79,7],[373,"How does innovation differ from retreatism in Merton's theory?","Innovation keeps the goal but uses illegal means, such as fraud. Retreatism rejects both goals and means, as in some drug misuse.",79,8],[373,"A 15-year-old boy starts shoplifting after joining a peer group that teaches techniques and pro-theft attitudes. Which theory fits best?","Sutherland's differential association: crime is learned in close groups, and people offend when views favouring law-breaking outweigh those against.",79,9],[373,"Which criminologist coined the term white-collar crime?","Edwin Sutherland, showing crime is not confined to poor people.",79,10],[374,"What are the four bonds in Hirschi's social bond theory (1969)?","Attachment, commitment, involvement and belief, which explain why most people do not offend.",79,11],[374,"What characterises the life-course-persistent group in Moffitt's taxonomy (1993)?","A small, mostly male group starting in early childhood and continuing into adulthood, often with hyperactivity, low verbal IQ and harsh parenting.",79,12],[374,"What characterises the adolescence-limited group in Moffitt's taxonomy?","A large group who offend only in their teens, copying peers during the gap between biological and social maturity, and usually stop.",79,13],[374,"What five purposes of sentencing adults does the Sentencing Act 2020 list in England and Wales?","Punishment, reduction of crime including by deterrence, reform and rehabilitation, protection of the public, and reparation.",79,14],[374,"A judge chooses prison mainly because the offender cannot harm the public while in custody. Which aim is this?","Incapacitation, which protects the public by preventing offending without trying to change the person.",79,15],[374,"What is the difference between general and individual deterrence?","General deterrence discourages other people from offending, while individual deterrence discourages the offender himself.",79,16],[374,"How does retribution differ from the forward-looking aims of sentencing?","Retribution looks back at what the offender deserves. Deterrence, incapacitation and rehabilitation look forward to preventing crime.",79,17],[374,"What is restorative justice and how effective is it?","The victim meets the offender with a trained facilitator to agree how harm will be repaired. Victims are usually satisfied and reoffending falls slightly.",79,18],[374,"What is Braithwaite's reintegrative shaming?","Condemning the act while welcoming back the person. Stigmatising shame pushes offenders towards criminal groups.",79,19],[377,"What did Lemert distinguish between in his 1951 account of deviance?","Primary deviance is the first rule-breaking act with little effect on self-concept. Secondary deviance follows labelling, when the person takes on the deviant identity and acts it out.",80,1],[377,"A 15-year-old boy in Bristol is labelled a thief after one shoplifting episode and then steals repeatedly. Which concept explains this?","Secondary deviance. Society's reaction to the label shapes his identity and later behaviour.",80,2],[377,"How is racism defined?","Disadvantage caused by a person's perceived race or ethnicity. It works through individual prejudice and through whole systems.",80,3],[377,"Which report defined institutional racism, and what event led to it?","The Macpherson report of 1999, which followed the murder of Stephen Lawrence.",80,4],[377,"What is institutional racism and does it require intent?","A collective failure of an organisation to give proper service because of colour, culture or ethnic origin. No intent is needed; it can work through unwitting prejudice, ignorance and stereotyping.",80,5],[377,"How much more often are Black people detained under the Mental Health Act than White people in England?","About 3 to 4 times as often.",80,6],[377,"How did Goffman describe stigma and what were his three types?","He called it a spoiled identity. The types are bodily flaws, blemishes of individual character such as mental disorder, and tribal stigma such as race.",80,7],[377,"In Goffman's terms, what is the difference between discredited and discreditable?","Discredited people have a visible difference, such as a facial scar. Discreditable people have a hidden difference, such as undisclosed depression, and must decide whether to reveal it.",80,8],[377,"What is courtesy stigma?","Stigma that spreads to people associated with the stigmatised person, such as family members shunned by neighbours.",80,9],[377,"What is the difference between enacted and felt stigma in Scambler and Hopkins' epilepsy study?","Enacted stigma is actual discrimination, such as losing a job. Felt stigma is shame and fear of discrimination that has not happened, and it was often more disabling.",80,10],[378,"What are Hayward and Bright's four themes explaining why mental illness is stigmatised?","Dangerousness, blame on the person for the illness, poor prognosis and disruption of social interaction. Infectiousness is not one of them.",80,11],[378,"What components of stigma did Link and Phelan (2001) describe?","Labelling, stereotyping, separation and status loss. These only become stigma when there is an imbalance of social power.",80,12],[378,"Which anti-stigma intervention works best for reducing public stigma in adults?","Social contact with people who have lived experience of mental illness. Time to Change in England (2007 to 2021) used this approach.",80,13],[378,"What did Becker and Scheff add to labelling theory?","Becker (Outsiders, 1963) said deviance lies in the label, not the act. Scheff described residual rule-breaking, where breaking unwritten norms, once labelled, locks people into the mad role.",80,14],[378,"What is androcentrism and how did Gilligan apply it to Kohlberg?","Androcentrism treats men as the norm. Gilligan argued Kohlberg's stages, based on males only, made women appear less morally mature.",80,15],[378,"What is the difference between alpha bias and beta bias?","Alpha bias exaggerates differences between the sexes. Beta bias ignores or minimises them, for example assuming male stress data apply to women.",80,16],[378,"How does ethnocentrism differ from cultural relativism?","Ethnocentrism judges other cultures by the standards of your own. Cultural relativism judges behaviour within the context of its own culture.",80,17],[379,"A 28-year-old woman with schizophrenia in Leeds says there is no point applying to college as people like her cannot cope. What concept is this?","The why-try effect of self-stigma, described by Corrigan. She agrees with the stereotype, which lowers self-esteem and makes her give up goals.",80,18],[379,"What did Rosenhan's 1973 pseudopatient study show, and what caution applies?","Eight healthy people claiming to hear voices were all admitted, stayed a mean 19 days and were mostly discharged with schizophrenia in remission. Later work has questioned how it was done, so details need caution.",80,19],[381,"How is ethnicity defined and how is it recorded?","A self-chosen group identity based on shared ancestry, history, language and religion. It is recorded by self-identification, for example in the census.",81,1],[381,"Why is race not considered a valid biological category?","It is a social category based on perceived physical traits. Most genetic variation lies within groups rather than between them, though racism still harms health.",81,2],[381,"What is nationality?","Legal citizenship of a country. It is distinct from ethnicity and race.",81,3],[381,"What did Odegaard find in 1932 and how did he explain it?","Norwegian migrants to Minnesota had more schizophrenia than those at home. He proposed selective migration, but later evidence weakened this idea.",81,4],[381,"What did Cantor-Graae and Selten's 2005 meta-analysis find about migration and schizophrenia?","First-generation migrants had about 2.7 times the risk and second-generation migrants about 4.5 times. Risk was highest in dark-skinned migrants from developing countries.",81,5],[381,"In the AESOP study, which groups had raised schizophrenia incidence and by how much?","Black Caribbean people had about 9 times and Black African people about 6 times the rate of White British people. South Asian groups were only modestly raised.",81,6],[381,"Which inquiry found institutional racism within mental health services?","The inquiry into the death of David Bennett in a psychiatric unit.",81,7],[382,"What evidence suggests the raised psychosis rate in African-Caribbean people is not genetic?","Their parents have a risk similar to parents of White patients, but UK-born siblings have raised risk.",81,8],[382,"Can misdiagnosis explain the ethnic excess in psychosis?","Not fully. The excess persists when standardised diagnostic interviews are used.",81,9],[382,"What is the best supported explanation for higher psychosis rates in UK migrant groups?","Social adversity and disadvantage, including discrimination, isolation and hardship.",81,10],[382,"What is the ethnic density effect?","Minority people have less psychosis where more of their neighbours share their ethnicity, probably due to less racism and isolation.",81,11],[382,"A 24-year-old Black British man in London has first-episode psychosis. How does his likely route into care differ from a White British man's?","Less GP involvement and more police involvement and compulsory admission, as shown by Morgan (2005).",81,12],[382,"Where do many Black patients first seek help for mental distress?","Often from family, faith leaders or traditional healers, partly because of mistrust of services and different illness beliefs.",81,13],[382,"Under which Mental Health Act measure is the racial disparity largest?","Community treatment orders.",81,14],[382,"What policy response followed the 2018 Independent Review of the Mental Health Act on racial disparity?","NHS England's Patient and Carer Race Equality Framework, aimed at reducing unequal use of the Act.",81,15],[382,"What is the healthy migrant effect and what is its key exception?","Recent migrants often have less chronic illness and lower mortality than the host population, fading with time. Psychosis is the exception, being raised from the start.",81,16],[382,"Do second-generation migrants have lower or higher psychosis risk than first-generation migrants?","Higher. This is a common exam trap.",81,17],[382,"What should a psychiatrist ask about when assessing psychosis in a migrant patient?","Migration history, discrimination and social isolation. Building trust and involving family and faith leaders may reduce coercive pathways.",81,18],[385,"Describe Goldberg and Huxley's pathway to psychiatric care.","Five levels from community distress to psychiatric inpatients, separated by four filters: decision to consult, GP detection, referral and admission.",82,1],[385,"A 40-year-old woman in Birmingham with low mood will not see her GP as it is a private family matter. At which filter has she stopped?","Filter 1, the decision to consult. Culture, identity and stigma act most strongly here.",82,2],[385,"Which Goldberg and Huxley filter depends mainly on GP skill?","Filter 2, recognition or detection of the disorder by the GP.",82,3],[385,"How did Kleinman distinguish disease from illness?","Disease is the doctor's biomedical view of what is wrong. Illness is the person's lived experience and meaning of being unwell.",82,4],[385,"What is an explanatory model?","A person's own account of what the problem is, what caused it, why it began, what it does and how it should be treated.",82,5],[385,"Give some questions Kleinman suggested for eliciting a patient's explanatory model.","What do you call this problem? What caused it? Why now? What treatment do you want? What do you fear most?",82,6],[385,"What is an idiom of distress and who introduced the term?","A shared, culturally specific way of expressing suffering. Nichter introduced the term in 1981.",82,7],[385,"How should 'thinking too much' in a 35-year-old Zimbabwean man be classified and handled?","As an idiom of distress, not a diagnosis or cause. Explore it, as it may overlap with depression or anxiety, rather than translating it straight into a diagnosis.",82,8],[385,"How does DSM-5-TR group cultural concepts of distress?","Into cultural syndromes, idioms of distress and cultural explanations. The older term culture-bound syndromes was dropped.",82,9],[385,"What is Kleinman's category fallacy?","Applying a Western diagnostic category in a culture where it has no local meaning. It is a validity error that can produce many false cases.",82,10],[386,"How many filters does the Goldberg and Huxley model have?","Four filters between five levels. There is no fifth filter.",82,11],[386,"How many questions and domains does the DSM-5 Cultural Formulation Interview core have?","16 core questions in 4 domains. There is also an informant version and supplementary modules.",82,12],[386,"What are the four domains of the Cultural Formulation Interview?","Cultural definition of the problem; cultural perceptions of cause, context and support; cultural factors in self-coping and past help-seeking; current help-seeking.",82,13],[386,"Which CFI domain includes the question 'Why do you think this is happening to you?'","Cultural perceptions of cause, context and support.",82,14],[386,"Is the Cultural Formulation Interview only for ethnic minority patients?","No. It is intended for every patient.",82,15],[386,"How do Black Caribbean people with first-episode psychosis in England more often reach care?","Through police or criminal justice contact and compulsory admission, and less through GPs. Mistrust and coercion reinforce each other.",82,16],[387,"What happens when the patient's and doctor's explanatory models clash?","Adherence tends to be poor and people often stop treatment. Openly discussing both models improves engagement.",82,17],[389,"What is enculturation?","Learning your own family's culture while growing up, such as a child in a Punjabi home in Leicester learning its language and customs.",83,1],[389,"What are acculturation and acculturative stress?","Acculturation is the change that occurs through close contact with another culture. Acculturative stress is the strain this causes.",83,2],[389,"What two questions underlie Berry's acculturation model?","Is the heritage culture maintained? Is contact with the host society sought?",83,3],[389,"A 32-year-old Syrian refugee in Leeds keeps her language and faith but volunteers locally and has British friends. Which Berry strategy is this?","Integration, keeping heritage and joining the host society. It is linked to the least stress and best wellbeing.",83,4],[389,"What is separation in Berry's model?","Keeping the heritage culture while choosing to avoid contact with the host society.",83,5],[389,"Which Berry strategy is linked to the highest stress and poorest mental health?","Marginalisation, losing the heritage culture and failing to connect with the host culture.",83,6],[389,"Which family members usually find acculturation hardest and which adapt fastest?","The oldest members find it hardest because of fixed habits, language barriers and no school or work. Children usually adapt fastest.",83,7],[390,"A 25-year-old Polish man in the UK drops his language, anglicises his name and avoids other Poles. Which strategy is this?","Assimilation, giving up heritage culture and adopting only the host culture.",83,8],[390,"Which host society strategy matches integration?","Multiculturalism, or the salad bowl, where groups keep their identities within shared civic life.",83,9],[390,"What is the melting pot and which migrant strategy does it match?","Cultures blending into one new national culture, as in the USA, matching assimilation. Zangwill's 1908 play popularised the phrase.",83,10],[390,"What is the difference between separation and segregation?","Separation is chosen by the minority group. Segregation is imposed by the dominant group.",83,11],[390,"Which host strategy corresponds to marginalisation and is marginalisation usually chosen?","Exclusion. Marginalisation is rarely chosen and usually results from exclusion and discrimination.",83,12],[390,"What did Boydell and colleagues (2001) find in London?","Ethnic minority residents had more schizophrenia where their group formed a smaller share of the local population, the ethnic density effect.",83,13],[390,"Why might living among your own ethnic group protect against psychosis?","It may reduce discrimination and isolation and increase social support, pointing to social rather than purely genetic causes.",83,14],[390,"Is deculturation one of Berry's four acculturation strategies?","No. The four are integration, assimilation, separation and marginalisation.",83,15],[390,"What should clinicians ask migrant patients about acculturation?","How they balance their two cultures and whether they feel they belong anywhere. Marginalised and older migrants may need community and language support.",83,16],[391,"A 30-year-old man brought to the UK as a child has lost his family's traditions and feels rejected by the host community. Which strategy?","Marginalisation. He belongs to neither culture and is at high risk of distress.",83,17],[393,"What does pathoplasticity mean in cultural psychiatry?","Culture shapes the content and form of symptoms but does not cause the illness itself.",84,1],[393,"Give an example of pathoplasticity in persecutory delusions over time.","In the 1950s patients described radio waves; today they describe phone hacking, microchips or 5G. The illness is the same but the content changes.",84,2],[393,"What did the WHO International Pilot Study of Schizophrenia (1973) show about culture?","Across 9 countries the core syndrome was recognisable everywhere, while delusional content reflected local beliefs.",84,3],[393,"What is a pathogenic effect of culture in Tseng's scheme?","A cultural belief directly causes the disorder, as in koro or dhat.",84,4],[393,"What is a pathoselective effect of culture and which syndrome illustrates it?","Culture selects which reaction a stressed person shows. Amok is the classic example.",84,5],[393,"What is a pathoelaborating effect of culture and which syndrome illustrates it?","Onlookers reinforce a behaviour until it becomes exaggerated. Latah is the classic example.",84,6],[393,"What are pathofacilitating and pathoreactive effects of culture?","Pathofacilitating makes a disorder more common without changing its form, such as heavy drinking norms. Pathoreactive shapes how people respond to illness and so its course.",84,7],[393,"Which term did DSM-5 use to replace culture-bound syndromes?","Cultural concepts of distress. Its glossary lists nine, including dhat, susto, ataque de nervios, shenjing shuairuo and taijin kyofusho.",84,8],[393,"A 30-year-old man in Singapore attends A&E terrified his penis is retracting into his body and he will die. What syndrome is this?","Koro. Epidemics have occurred in China, Singapore and India, and women may fear retraction of the breasts or vulva.",84,9],[394,"What is pa-leng?","A morbid fear of cold and wind, believed to drain vitality, described in Chinese culture.",84,10],[394,"Describe amok.","A period of brooding in a Malay man followed by a sudden violent frenzy, with later amnesia.",84,11],[394,"Describe latah.","An exaggerated startle reaction in Malay women with shouted obscenities and echo phenomena such as copying others.",84,12],[394,"What is shenjing shuairuo?","Chinese neurasthenia: fatigue, headache, poor sleep and poor concentration attributed to weak nerves. It appeared in the Chinese Classification of Mental Disorders.",84,13],[394,"How does taijin kyofusho differ from Western social anxiety?","In taijin kyofusho the core fear is offending or embarrassing others. In social anxiety the fear is of embarrassing oneself.",84,14],[394,"What is jikoshu-kyofu and what is hikikomori?","Jikoshu-kyofu is a taijin kyofusho subtype with fear of giving off foul body odour. Hikikomori is withdrawal at home for 6 months or more.",84,15],[394,"A 20-year-old Nigerian student has head burning, blurred vision and cannot take in his reading. What syndrome is this?","Brain fag, described by Prince in 1960 in West African students.",84,16],[394,"What are susto and ataque de nervios?","Susto is soul loss after a fright in Latin America. Ataque de nervios is a Latino outburst of screaming, crying, trembling and heat, often after family stress.",84,17],[394,"Describe piblokto and windigo.","Piblokto affects Inuit women with screaming, stripping and running onto ice, then sleep and amnesia. Windigo is an Algonquian fear of becoming a cannibal monster, of debated validity.",84,18],[394,"How should a clinician approach a patient presenting with a cultural concept of distress?","Ask what the symptoms mean in the person's culture, consider underlying depression, anxiety or PTSD, and treat with respect, reassurance and education.",84,19],[395,"A 22-year-old man from northern India has fatigue and anxiety, convinced he loses semen in his urine. What is this and how is it managed?","Dhat. Management combines reassurance, psychoeducation and treating any comorbid depression or anxiety.",84,20],[397,"A 35-year-old man with treatment-resistant schizophrenia is advised to take clozapine for the best chance of recovery. Which ethical principle drives this?","Beneficence, the duty to do good, weighed against the risks of harm.",85,1],[397,"What is utilitarianism and who are its main proponents?","Judging an act by its consequences, choosing what gives the greatest total benefit. Bentham and Mill are the key figures.",85,2],[397,"What is deontology and what is Kant's categorical imperative?","Deontology holds some duties bind regardless of outcome, such as not lying. The categorical imperative says never treat humanity merely as a means.",85,3],[397,"What is virtue ethics and what is phronesis?","Virtue ethics, from Aristotle, asks what a good, wise and honest doctor would do. Phronesis is practical wisdom.",85,4],[397,"What are Beauchamp and Childress's four principles?","Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice, together called principlism (1979).",85,5],[397,"What does it mean that the four principles are prima facie?","Each binds unless outweighed by another. They are mid-level principles below higher theories such as deontology.",85,6],[397,"Stopping clozapine after severe neutropenia illustrates which principle?","Non-maleficence, the duty to avoid harm.",85,7],[397,"What are paternalism and casuistry?","Paternalism is overriding a person's wishes for their own good. Casuistry is reasoning by comparison with similar past cases.",85,8],[398,"What is the functional test of capacity under the Mental Capacity Act 2005?","Whether the person can understand, retain, use or weigh the information, and communicate a decision. Capacity is presumed and an unwise choice does not prove incapacity.",85,9],[398,"What was established in Re C (1994)?","A man with schizophrenia had capacity to refuse leg amputation because he understood, believed and weighed the information. Capable adults may refuse life-saving treatment.",85,10],[398,"What is Gillick competence and how does it differ from the Fraser guidelines?","A child under 16 with enough understanding can consent to treatment. Fraser guidelines are narrower, applying to contraception advice.",85,11],[398,"What did W v Egdell (1990) establish about confidentiality?","Disclosure of a dangerous patient's psychiatric report was allowed in the public interest.",85,12],[398,"A 50-year-old man with epilepsy keeps driving against advice. What should his doctor do?","Inform the DVLA medical adviser and tell the patient that this is being done.",85,13],[398,"What must regulated professionals do on discovering FGM in a girl under 18?","Since 2015 they must report known FGM in under 18s to the police.",85,14],[398,"What are the Nuremberg Code and the Declaration of Helsinki?","The Nuremberg Code (1947) followed the Nazi doctors' trial and made voluntary consent essential. Helsinki (1964), from the World Medical Association, is today's standard and requires ethics committee review.",85,15],[398,"What led to the Belmont Report and what are its principles?","US abuses such as Tuskegee and Willowbrook. Its principles are respect for persons, beneficence and justice.",85,16],[398,"What is required for a UK trial involving NHS patients?","Approval by a Research Ethics Committee and clinical equipoise. Participants can withdraw at any time, and a personal consultee is consulted for adults lacking capacity.",85,17],[398,"What were the views of Szasz, Laing and Cooper?","Szasz called mental illness a myth but rejected the anti-psychiatry label. Laing saw psychosis as a sane response to an unliveable situation. Cooper coined anti-psychiatry.",85,18],[399,"A trust panel shares fewer beds fairly between adult, older adult and CAMHS services by need. Which principle is applied?","Justice, specifically distributive justice in allocating scarce resources fairly.",85,19],[399,"Why did Popper criticise psychoanalysis?","It could explain any outcome and so was not falsifiable. Falsifiability was his criterion for a scientific theory.",85,20],[402,"Describe Bronfenbrenner's ecological systems model.","The child sits within nested systems: microsystem, mesosystem, exosystem, macrosystem and chronosystem.",86,1],[402,"What is the mesosystem in Bronfenbrenner's model?","The links between the child's settings, such as parents attending a meeting with teachers. It is not a setting itself.",86,2],[402,"What are the exosystem, macrosystem and chronosystem?","The exosystem affects the child indirectly, like a parent's workplace. The macrosystem is the wider culture. The chronosystem adds time and life events.",86,3],[402,"How do stage theories differ from continuous views of development?","Stage theories (Piaget, Freud, Erikson) propose qualitative leaps. Continuous views, such as Siegler's information processing, propose gradual quantitative gains.",86,4],[402,"What is a critical period and what is the classic example?","A fixed window after which a capacity cannot develop normally. Hubel and Wiesel showed this for binocular vision in kittens.",86,5],[402,"What is a sensitive period and which human abilities fit it?","An optimal but not exclusive time for learning. Language and attachment fit, as later learning is possible but harder.",86,6],[402,"What is equifinality?","Many different early pathways lead to the same outcome, such as depression. Cicchetti described it.",86,7],[402,"Which Erikson, Piaget and Freud stages fit a 4-year-old?","Initiative versus guilt, the preoperational stage and the phallic stage.",86,8],[403,"What is multifinality?","One starting point leads to many outcomes. Child abuse, for example, may lead to depression, PTSD, substance misuse or resilience.",86,9],[403,"Which Erikson, Piaget and Freud stages fit infancy?","Trust versus mistrust, the sensorimotor stage and the oral stage.",86,10],[403,"Which stages fit a child aged about 18 months to 3 years?","Erikson's autonomy versus shame and doubt and Freud's anal stage. Piaget's preoperational stage begins around age 2.",86,11],[403,"Which stages fit a 9-year-old?","Industry versus inferiority, concrete operations and Freud's latency.",86,12],[403,"Which stages fit adolescence?","Identity versus role confusion, formal operations with hypothetico-deductive reasoning, and Freud's genital stage.",86,13],[403,"What can a typical 2-year-old and 3-year-old do with bricks, drawing and speech?","At 2, a tower of six and two-word phrases. At 3, a tower of nine, copies a circle and uses simple sentences.",86,14],[403,"How did Erikson define maturity?","Through the adult tasks of intimacy, generativity and integrity.",86,15],[403,"What did Vaillant find in his Harvard study of men?","Mature defences such as altruism, sublimation, suppression, anticipation and humour predicted good health and work.",86,16],[403,"Which two adult stages did Vaillant add to Erikson's model?","Career consolidation and keeper of the meaning.",86,17],[403,"Is suppression or repression a mature defence?","Suppression is mature. Repression is not.",86,18],[403,"Which traits did Allport list for a mature person?","Traits including self-objectification, which involves insight and humour.",86,19],[404,"A girl copies a circle and a cross but not a triangle and is dry day and night. What is her likely age?","About 4 years. A triangle follows at 5 and a diamond at 6.",86,20],[406,"What is heritability and is it fixed?","The proportion of variation in a trait within a population linked to genetic differences. It is not fixed and changes with setting and age.",87,1],[406,"What did Turkheimer (2003) find about IQ heritability and family income?","In poor US families heritability of IQ was near zero, with shared environment explaining most variation. In affluent families heritability was high.",87,2],[406,"What is the Scarr-Rowe effect and where is it weaker?","Deprivation suppresses the expression of genetic potential for IQ. It is clear in the USA but weaker in Western Europe and Australia with stronger safety nets.",87,3],[406,"Why does heritability of IQ rise from childhood to adulthood?","People increasingly choose environments that suit their genetic tendencies.",87,4],[406,"What did Duyme (1999) find in French children adopted after abuse or neglect?","Children with low IQ adopted at 4 to 6 years had substantial IQ gains by adolescence, larger in higher socioeconomic adoptive homes.",87,5],[406,"What did Teicher and Andersen find about the timing of childhood sexual abuse?","Brain changes in the hippocampus, corpus callosum or frontal cortex depended on age at abuse, fitting region-specific sensitive periods.",87,6],[407,"Why should a developmental formulation record when adversity occurred?","Brain regions are most vulnerable while maturing, so timing, duration and number of adversities all shape outcome, not just presence.",87,7],[407,"What deprivation-specific pattern did ERA children deprived beyond 6 months show?","Autism-like features, disinhibited social engagement, inattention and overactivity, persisting into adulthood despite loving homes.",87,8],[407,"What is a sleeper effect?","Harm from an early experience that only becomes visible years later. A child who is well at 11 can still develop problems.",87,9],[407,"What did Ann and Alan Clarke argue in 1976?","In Early Experience: Myth and Evidence they argued early experience is not destiny and later care can alter the path.",87,10],[407,"What did Sameroff's Rochester study show about cumulative risk?","Children's IQ at 4 fell as the number of family risks rose, regardless of which risks were present.",87,11],[407,"What is Rutter's steeling effect?","Coping successfully with a manageable stress protects against later stress, much like a vaccine.",87,12],[407,"What is a turning point in development after adversity?","An event, such as a supportive marriage, that redirects a person's life path towards better outcomes.",87,13],[407,"How should services support a 7-year-old adopted after prolonged early institutional deprivation?","Expect possible recovery but provide long follow-up and support, since sleeper effects can emerge years later.",87,14],[408,"In the ERA study, how did children who left Romanian institutions before 6 months fare?","Broadly similar to UK children adopted without early deprivation, with little lasting harm.",87,15],[408,"What change occurred in longer-deprived ERA adoptees in their twenties?","Emotional problems such as depression and anxiety increased despite years in nurturing homes.",87,16],[410,"What is a cross-sectional developmental study and its main flaw?","People of different ages are tested once at the same time. Age is confounded with cohort, so differences may reflect generation rather than ageing.",88,1],[410,"What are the strengths and weaknesses of longitudinal studies?","They show true change within individuals but are slow and suffer attrition and practice effects.",88,2],[410,"What is a cohort-sequential design and what did Schaie find with it?","Several birth cohorts are followed over time with new cohorts added each wave. Schaie's Seattle study found real intellectual decline starts later than cross-sectional data suggest.",88,3],[410,"A study asks depressed adults about their parents' harshness. Which bias is likely and how is it avoided?","Recall bias, as current mood colours memories. Prospective studies recording exposures before the outcome avoid much of it.",88,4],[410,"What is selective attrition and how is it handled?","The most chaotic families drop out, skewing the sample towards better-functioning families. Researchers trace participants and use methods such as multiple imputation.",88,5],[410,"What is the Dunedin study?","A New Zealand cohort of about 1,000 people born in 1972 to 1973 with very high retention. It produced Caspi's gene-environment findings.",88,6],[410,"What is ALSPAC and what is its key strength?","The Avon Longitudinal Study of Parents and Children recruited about 14,500 pregnant women near Bristol due in 1991 to 1992. Starting in pregnancy allows study of prenatal exposures.",88,7],[410,"Whom does ALSPAC now include besides the original children?","The original children's own offspring.",88,8],[411,"What was the Cambridge Study in Delinquent Development?","West and Farrington followed 411 South London boys from age 8, beginning in 1961, into middle adulthood to study offending.",88,9],[411,"What childhood factors predicted offending in the Cambridge Study?","Disruptive behaviour, family criminality, low IQ and attainment, poor parenting, deprivation and impulsivity.",88,10],[411,"What did Rutter's Isle of Wight survey find?","About 7% of 10- and 11-year-olds had a psychiatric disorder, with much higher rates in children with brain disorders such as epilepsy.",88,11],[411,"How did inner London rates compare with the Isle of Wight in Rutter's work?","Rates of child psychiatric disorder were about double in inner London.",88,12],[411,"What did Rutter find about the number of family adversities?","One adversity alone added little risk, but two or more together raised risk about fourfold.",88,13],[411,"What is a microgenetic design?","Siegler's method of observing the same children many times during a short period of rapid change, recording every trial to show how change happens.",88,14],[411,"Which British cohorts are the Millennium Cohort Study and the National Child Development Study?","The Millennium Cohort is UK-wide, born around 2000 to 2001. The National Child Development Study follows people born in one week of 1958.",88,15],[412,"Was childhood anxiety a main predictor of delinquency in the Cambridge Study?","No. Anxiety and nervousness were not among the main predictors.",88,16],[412,"What is a natural experiment and what example comes from the Great Smoky Mountains study?","An external event changes exposure independently of individual choice. Casino income lifting families out of poverty reduced their children's conduct problems.",88,17],[412,"Why do natural experiments strengthen causal inference?","People do not choose their exposure, so confounding is reduced. The Great Smoky Mountains findings supported social causation over selection.",88,18],[414,"What does a heritability of 0.6 mean?","About 60% of the variation in the trait in that population and setting relates to genetic differences. It says nothing about one individual.",89,1],[414,"What is Falconer's formula and what is heritability if rMZ is 0.70 and rDZ is 0.40?","Heritability is about twice the difference between MZ and DZ correlations. Here it is 2 x 0.30, giving 0.60.",89,2],[414,"Which design most directly separates genetic from rearing influences?","Adoption studies, comparing adoptees with birth parents (genes) and adoptive parents (rearing).",89,3],[414,"How does the children-of-twins design work?","Children of identical twins discordant for depression are compared. If only the depressed twin's children fare worse, transmission is environmental.",89,4],[414,"Why do siblings in the same family often differ in personality?","Non-shared environment, meaning experiences unique to each child, as described by Plomin and Daniels.",89,5],[414,"What do genome-wide association studies of psychiatric traits typically show?","Many common variants, each with a very small effect.",89,6],[414,"What is a polygenic score and what are its limits?","A sum of risk variants weighted by effect size. It predicts group risk but is weak for individuals and less accurate outside European-ancestry samples.",89,7],[414,"When was polygenic scoring first applied on a large scale to schizophrenia?","In 2009.",89,8],[414,"What is passive gene-environment correlation?","Parents provide both genes and an environment linked to them, such as musical parents giving a home full of instruments.",89,9],[414,"A musically gifted 15-year-old girl joins a band and seeks out musician friends. Which gene-environment correlation is this?","Active correlation, or niche-picking. It grows with age and autonomy and helps explain rising heritability.",89,10],[415,"What is evocative gene-environment correlation?","A child's genetically influenced traits draw particular responses from others, such as a sociable child getting warm replies from teachers.",89,11],[415,"What did Caspi (2002) find in the Dunedin cohort?","Maltreated boys became antisocial mainly if they carried the low-activity MAOA variant, an early gene-environment interaction finding.",89,12],[415,"What is the status of Caspi's 2003 5-HTTLPR and life stress finding?","Short allele carriers seemed more prone to depression after stress, but replications are mixed and large genome-wide studies have largely failed to confirm it.",89,13],[415,"What is Belsky's differential susceptibility theory?","Some children, called orchids, are more sensitive to environment, doing worst in harsh settings and best in supportive ones.",89,14],[415,"What is an epigenetic change?","An alteration in gene expression without change to the DNA sequence, for example DNA methylation.",89,15],[415,"What did cross-fostering show in Weaver's rat studies?","The effect followed the care received, not the birth mother, showing non-genomic transmission of maternal behaviour.",89,16],[415,"What were the effects of conception during the Dutch Hunger Winter of 1944 to 1945?","About double the risk of schizophrenia and more metabolic disease in offspring.",89,17],[415,"What is the Barker hypothesis?","Low birth weight is linked to adult heart disease and diabetes, the basis of developmental origins of health and disease or fetal programming.",89,18],[415,"A mother asks if her son's depression is purely genetic. How should you explain genetic risk?","Genes raise or lower risk but rarely decide outcome, and care and environment really matter. Polygenic scores are not yet useful for one person.",89,19],[416,"What did Weaver find in rat pups that received high maternal licking and grooming?","Reduced methylation of the glucocorticoid receptor gene promoter in the hippocampus, more receptors and calmer stress responses.",89,20],[418,"What pattern of synapse formation and elimination is seen in the human cortex after birth?","Synaptic density climbs quickly after birth to above adult levels. Unused synapses are then removed by pruning, which continues longest in the prefrontal cortex.",90,1],[418,"Whose post-mortem synapse counts showed that visual cortex is pruned earlier than prefrontal cortex?","Huttenlocher. Sensory areas such as visual cortex finish pruning early, while prefrontal pruning continues through adolescence.",90,2],[418,"What is myelination and in what direction does it proceed across the brain?","It is fatty insulation of nerve fibres that speeds signals. It runs from back to front, sensory and motor areas first, prefrontal cortex last into the third decade.",90,3],[418,"At roughly what age has the brain reached about 90% of its adult volume?","At about 6 years. Rewiring through pruning and myelination continues for many years after this.",90,4],[418,"What did Greenough mean by experience-expectant plasticity?","Brain change that depends on input every member of the species normally receives, such as patterned light, at the right sensitive time.",90,5],[418,"How does experience-dependent plasticity differ from experience-expectant plasticity?","It reflects each person's own unique learning, such as learning the piano. It is not tied to a critical period and works throughout life.",90,6],[419,"What did Giedd's longitudinal MRI studies show about cortical grey matter across development?","Grey matter follows an inverted U. It rises in childhood, peaks in late childhood or early adolescence, then falls as pruning proceeds.",90,7],[419,"How does white matter volume change from childhood into adulthood?","It keeps increasing steadily into adulthood, unlike grey matter which peaks and then declines.",90,8],[419,"What does fractional anisotropy on diffusion tensor imaging measure and how does it change in adolescence?","It reflects how directionally water moves along fibre tracts. It rises through childhood and adolescence as tracts become more myelinated and organised.",90,9],[419,"At about what ages does total brain volume peak in girls and in boys?","About 10.5 years in girls and 14.5 years in boys. Grey matter also peaks about 1 to 2 years earlier in girls.",90,10],[419,"How do resting-state brain networks change with development?","They shift from local to distributed. Regions first connect with neighbours, then with distant partners in the same network, such as the default mode network.",90,11],[419,"What does the dual systems model of adolescent risk-taking propose?","A reward-sensitive limbic system matures earlier than the prefrontal control system. Risk-taking therefore peaks when teenagers are emotionally aroused or with peers.",90,12],[419,"What was Feinberg's hypothesis about schizophrenia and brain development?","That schizophrenia reflects abnormal, probably excessive, adolescent synaptic pruning. This fits late-teen onset and later links with the complement gene C4.",90,13],[419,"Which gene has been linked to excessive synaptic pruning in schizophrenia?","The complement component 4 (C4) gene, whose product tags synapses for removal.",90,14],[419,"What did Shaw find about cortical thickness in children with ADHD?","Cortical thickness peaked about 3 years later than in controls, around 10.5 versus 7.5 years, most clearly in prefrontal regions.",90,15],[419,"Can current developmental brain scans be used to diagnose ADHD in an individual child?","No. Imaging findings describe group averages and cannot yet diagnose an individual child.",90,16],[420,"Does Shaw's ADHD imaging work support deviant or delayed brain maturation?","Delayed maturation. The order in which regions matured was normal, it simply happened later.",90,17],[420,"Which brain region is the last to prune and myelinate?","The prefrontal cortex, which continues maturing into the mid-twenties. It is a classic trap to say it matures first.",90,18],[424,"Which psychoanalyst founded the British object relations tradition through play-based work with young children?","Melanie Klein. Fairbairn, Winnicott and Bion later extended the tradition.",91,1],[424,"What are the three parts of Freud's topographical model of the mind and when was it described?","Conscious, preconscious and unconscious, described in 1900. The preconscious holds material that can be recalled with effort.",91,2],[424,"What are the components of Freud's structural model and their roles?","The id holds drives and seeks pleasure, the ego deals with reality and uses defences, and the superego is the conscience that creates guilt. It dates from 1923.",91,3],[424,"A 4-year-old girl starts bedwetting again after her baby brother is born. Which defence mechanism is this?","Regression, a return to earlier patterns of behaviour under stress.",91,4],[424,"List Freud's psychosexual stages in order.","Oral, anal, phallic, latency and genital.",91,5],[424,"In which stage does the Oedipus complex arise and how is it resolved in boys?","In the phallic stage, about 3 to 5 years. Castration anxiety leads the boy to identify with his father, and the superego forms as its heir.",91,6],[424,"Who coined the term Electra complex and what was Freud's view of it?","Jung coined it for the girl's version. Freud rejected the term and spoke of the female Oedipus complex.",91,7],[424,"Fixation at which psychosexual stages was linked to dependence and to obsessional traits?","Oral fixation was linked to dependence and drinking. Anal fixation was linked to obsessional traits and OCD.",91,8],[424,"In what order did Freud rank childhood anxieties from least to most mature?","Loss of the object, loss of the object's love, castration anxiety, then superego anxiety or guilt as the most mature.",91,9],[425,"What is Erikson's epigenetic principle?","Each psychosocial strength has its own critical time to emerge but builds on those that came before.",91,10],[425,"Name Erikson's first and last psychosocial crises and their virtues.","The first is trust versus mistrust, giving hope. The last is integrity versus despair, giving wisdom.",91,11],[425,"An 8-year-old boy is absorbed in schoolwork and hobbies. Which Freudian, Eriksonian and Piagetian stages apply?","Latency (Freud), industry versus inferiority (Erikson) and concrete operations (Piaget).",91,12],[425,"Which theorist founded individual psychology and what did it emphasise?","Alfred Adler. He stressed feelings of inferiority, striving to overcome them and the effect of birth order.",91,13],[425,"Describe Klein's paranoid-schizoid position.","The infant splits the mother into a wholly good and wholly bad breast and projects feelings into her. Persecutory anxiety dominates.",91,14],[425,"What is the central task of Klein's depressive position and when does it begin?","From about the middle of the first year the infant sees one whole mother. The task is tolerating ambivalence, with guilt and a wish to repair.",91,15],[425,"What did Winnicott mean by a good-enough mother?","A carer who adapts closely to the baby at first, then gradually allows small, manageable frustrations. Perfection is not required.",91,16],[425,"What is a transitional object and whose concept is it?","Winnicott's idea of the first not-me possession, such as a blanket. It helps the child cope with stress and separation and is given up naturally.",91,17],[425,"What happens in Mahler's rapprochement subphase and at what age?","At about 18 to 24 months the toddler repeatedly returns to the mother and becomes clingy after a period of joyful exploring.",91,18],[425,"What is affect attunement as described by Daniel Stern?","A parent matching the baby's feeling state in a different form, for example responding with the voice to the baby's movement.",91,19],[426,"How does repression differ from suppression?","Repression pushes painful material out of awareness unconsciously. Suppression is a conscious decision not to think about something.",91,20],[429,"A 2-year-old boy is admitted to hospital with no familiar carer. What sequence of reactions did the Robertsons describe?","Protest, with crying and searching, then despair, then detachment, where the child seems to cope but has given up on the parent.",92,1],[429,"How did Bowlby explain why attachment exists?","As an evolved survival system. Crying, clinging and following keep a helpless infant close to a protective adult.",92,2],[429,"Why may a maltreated toddler cling more to the carer who frightens her?","Fear activates the attachment system, so threat increases proximity-seeking even towards the abusive carer.",92,3],[429,"What is monotropy and who becomes the principal attachment figure?","Bowlby's idea of one main figure at the top of a ranked hierarchy. It is whoever gives the most sensitive, responsive care, which can be the father.",92,4],[429,"What are internal working models?","Inner mental maps of self and others built from repeated experiences of care, which guide later relationships.",92,5],[429,"What are the key features of imprinting described by Lorenz?","Goslings follow the first moving object after hatching. Imprinting is time-limited, specific, hard to reverse and long-lasting.",92,6],[429,"What did Harlow's monkey experiments show?","Infant monkeys clung to a soft cloth mother and visited the wire one only to feed, showing contact comfort matters more than food. Isolated monkeys had severe social deficits.",92,7],[430,"What did Schaffer and Emerson find in their Glasgow study of infants?","Attachment strength followed sensitive, prompt responsiveness rather than feeding. By 18 months about 87% had multiple attachments.",92,8],[430,"List Bowlby's four phases of attachment in order.","Pre-attachment, attachment in the making, clear-cut attachment and goal-corrected partnership.",92,9],[430,"For what age group is the Strange Situation used and which part of it matters most for classification?","Infants aged 12 to 18 months. Behaviour on reunion is key, not crying at separation.",92,10],[430,"How do insecure-avoidant and insecure-resistant infants behave at reunion in the Strange Situation?","Avoidant infants ignore the carer and treat the stranger alike. Resistant infants seek contact then angrily resist it.",92,11],[430,"What behaviour characterises disorganised attachment and what is its later risk?","Infants freeze or approach then turn away. It raises the risk of later externalising problems and should prompt safeguarding thoughts.",92,12],[430,"What are the four categories of the Adult Attachment Interview?","Autonomous, dismissing, preoccupied (entangled) and unresolved. Coding rests on how coherent the account is, not what happened.",92,13],[430,"How well does a parent's Adult Attachment Interview predict the infant's attachment and what is the transmission gap?","It predicts the infant's pattern in about 75% of cases. Parental sensitivity explains only part of this, which is the transmission gap.",92,14],[430,"What is Spitz's anaclitic depression?","Depression in attached infants separated from the carer for about 3 months, which reverses on reunion.",92,15],[430,"How did Rutter distinguish deprivation from privation?","Deprivation is losing an attachment bond that had formed. Privation is never forming a bond at all.",92,16],[430,"What did the ERA study of Romanian adoptees find about indiscriminate friendliness, and what is its ICD-11 name?","The longer the deprivation, the more the child was friendly with any stranger. ICD-11 calls this disinhibited social engagement disorder.",92,17],[430,"What did the NICHD study conclude about the effect of hours in day care?","Day care hours on their own did not harm attachment or development.",92,18],[431,"A 6-week-old girl smiles at strangers and parents alike and is soothed by any voice. Which attachment phase is she in?","Pre-attachment, from birth to about 8 to 12 weeks, when the baby signals to anyone.",92,19],[432,"A woman in an Adult Attachment Interview angrily recounts old arguments with her mother and loses track of questions. Classification?","Preoccupied (entangled). Her infant is more likely to be classified as resistant.",92,20],[434,"A 10-year-old boy still longs for his dead father 14 months later, beyond cultural norms, with impairment. What is the ICD-11 diagnosis?","Prolonged grief disorder, marked by persistent longing or preoccupation with the deceased and intense emotional pain.",93,1],[434,"What duration criterion does ICD-11 prolonged grief disorder require?","Symptoms must persist at least 6 months after the loss and clearly exceed social, cultural or religious norms, with impairment.",93,2],[434,"What did Felitti and Anda's ACE study find?","A graded dose-response relationship: the more categories of childhood adversity, the higher the risk of later physical and mental ill health.",93,3],[434,"What excess risk was seen in adults with 4 or more adverse childhood experiences?","About 4 to 12 times the risk of alcoholism, drug misuse, depression and suicide attempt.",93,4],[434,"Why should ACE scores not be used to screen individual children?","They are a simple retrospective count that ignores severity and support. They are a population measure too imprecise to predict one person's outcome.",93,5],[434,"What did Baldwin find when comparing prospective records with retrospective reports of childhood maltreatment?","Agreement was poor, and the two methods largely identified different people.",93,6],[434,"What stress hormone response did Heim find in women abused in childhood?","Exaggerated ACTH and cortisol responses to stress, indicating a sensitised HPA axis.",93,7],[434,"Which adult psychiatric disorder has the highest reported rates of childhood abuse and early separation?","Borderline personality disorder, often over half of patients, though many with such histories never develop it.",93,8],[435,"What proportion of people abused in childhood go on to abuse their own children?","About one third, so two thirds break the cycle.",93,9],[435,"What is the commonest reason for a child protection plan in England?","Neglect. Children with disabilities are also at higher risk of abuse and neglect.",93,10],[435,"Which factor most reduces the risk of child maltreatment, and who most often fabricates or induces illness?","A supportive network of relatives and friends most reduces risk. In fabricated or induced illness the perpetrator is most often the mother.",93,11],[435,"What did Brown and Harris find about losing a mother in childhood?","Loss of the mother before age 11 was a vulnerability factor for adult depression in working-class London women.",93,12],[435,"What did the Walthamstow follow-up show about the mechanism of risk after maternal death?","The risk came mainly from inadequate care after the loss, not from the death itself.",93,13],[435,"How long do most children take to adapt after parental divorce and what reactions may occur?","Most adapt within 2 to 3 years. Some show regression, aggression, self-blame, physical complaints and falling school work.",93,14],[435,"Which sex was found more adversely affected by parental divorce in early studies?","Boys were generally more affected. Saying girls are more affected is a common trap.",93,15],[435,"What factors protect children after parental separation?","Low conflict between parents, stable finances and regular contact with both parents.",93,16],[435,"A 12-year-old girl comforts her lonely mother and runs the household. What is this called?","Parentification, a role reversal where the child meets a parent's emotional or practical needs.",93,17],[435,"What does trauma-informed care involve in practice?","Acting as if any patient may have past trauma. Staff explain procedures, offer choices and avoid re-traumatising practices for everyone.",93,18],[436,"How do outcomes compare between children bereaved of a parent and those from high-conflict separations?","High-conflict separation brings more behaviour problems. Bereaved children are more at risk of later depression but fewer conduct problems.",93,19],[439,"Which parenting style is most consistently linked with good self-esteem, social skills and school achievement in Western studies?","Authoritative parenting, which is warm and firm, sets clear expectations and explains the reasons for rules.",94,1],[439,"On which two dimensions are parenting styles now described?","Responsiveness, meaning warmth, and demandingness, meaning rules and control.",94,2],[439,"Who first described parenting styles and how many did she name?","Baumrind in 1967, from observing parents with young children. She named authoritative, authoritarian and permissive styles.",94,3],[439,"What outcomes are associated with authoritarian parenting?","Children may be obedient but tend to become anxious, low in self-esteem or hostile.",94,4],[439,"How does permissive parenting differ from neglectful parenting?","Permissive parents are warm but set few limits, giving children poor self-control. Neglectful parents are low in both warmth and demands.",94,5],[439,"Who added the neglectful style and what outcomes does it carry?","Maccoby and Martin in 1983. Neglectful parenting has the worst outcomes, including behaviour problems, drug use and low achievement.",94,6],[439,"How did Darling and Steinberg distinguish parenting style from parenting practice?","Practices are specific acts such as a homework rule. Style is the emotional climate and attitudes, which changes how a child receives a practice.",94,7],[440,"What did Chao show about strict parental control in Chinese families?","Strict control can be experienced as care in some cultures, so the meaning of a style depends on cultural context.",94,8],[440,"What is Patterson's coercion theory?","A child's whining is rewarded when the parent gives in, and the parent is rewarded when the noise stops. Mutual negative reinforcement escalates the cycle.",94,9],[440,"Which intervention targets the coercive cycle described by Patterson?","Parent training programmes, which aim to break the cycle of mutual negative reinforcement.",94,10],[440,"In Minuchin's structural family theory, how do enmeshment and disengagement differ?","Enmeshment means blurred boundaries, with members speaking for each other and no privacy. Disengagement means rigid, distant boundaries.",94,11],[440,"What is triangulation in family systems theory and how may the child present?","A child is drawn into the parents' conflict to calm the couple. The child often presents with physical symptoms such as headaches.",94,12],[440,"What two scales does Parker's Parental Bonding Instrument measure?","Care, ranging from warmth to rejection, and overprotection, ranging from control to freedom, as recalled by the adult.",94,13],[440,"What is the main methodological weakness of the Parental Bonding Instrument?","It depends on adults' memories of their parents, so it is open to recall bias.",94,14],[440,"What did Golombok conclude from studying different family structures?","Family relationship quality matters more than structure. Warmth, conflict, parental mental health and stigma predict children's outcomes.",94,15],[440,"What explains most of the extra risk seen in children from single-parent families?","Poverty and conflict, rather than single parenthood itself.",94,16],[440,"What is the family life cycle described by Carter and McGoldrick and why is it clinically relevant?","A series of stages from leaving home to later life. Each transition brings stress, so symptoms often appear at these points.",94,17],[441,"A father demands strict obedience, gives no reasons and shows little warmth. Which parenting style is this?","Authoritarian parenting, high in control and low in warmth, summed up by because I said so.",94,18],[441,"A 35-year-old man with recurrent depression recalls his mother as cold yet intrusive. What Parental Bonding Instrument pattern is this?","Affectionless control, meaning low care with high overprotection, which is linked with adult depression and anxiety.",94,19],[441,"Which family life cycle stage begins when the last child leaves home?","Launching children and moving on.",94,20],[443,"In Thomas and Chess's framework, what is temperament?","The how of behaviour, a child's own characteristic style of reacting. It appears from early infancy and is partly inborn.",95,1],[443,"What was the New York Longitudinal Study and how were data collected?","Thomas and Chess followed 133 mostly middle-class New York children from 1956, using structured parent interviews about everyday behaviour.",95,2],[443,"What proportions of children were easy, difficult and slow-to-warm-up in the New York Longitudinal Study?","Easy about 40%, difficult about 10% and slow-to-warm-up about 15%. About a third did not fit any group.",95,3],[443,"A 10-month-old boy has irregular sleep and hunger, screams at new things and adapts slowly with intense crying. Which temperament?","Difficult temperament, marked by irregularity, negative mood and intense reactions.",95,4],[443,"What is goodness of fit?","The match between a child's temperament and the demands of their setting. A difficult temperament raises the risk of behaviour problems mainly when fit is poor.",95,5],[443,"According to Rutter, which children are most affected by family discord?","Young, male, overactive children with a difficult temperament.",95,6],[444,"How does a slow-to-warm-up child differ from a difficult child?","The slow-to-warm-up child withdraws quietly and adapts slowly but reacts mildly. The difficult child reacts intensely and explosively.",95,7],[444,"What were Buss and Plomin's EASI traits and which was dropped?","Emotionality, activity, sociability and impulsivity. Impulsivity was dropped because it was less clearly inherited.",95,8],[444,"What is behavioural inhibition and what is its later risk?","Kagan's term for the 15 to 20% of toddlers who are shy and wary of novelty. They are more likely to develop social anxiety in adolescence.",95,9],[444,"What did Rothbart mean by effortful control?","The capacity to hold back a strong, dominant response, an important aspect of temperament and self-regulation.",95,10],[444,"How did Cloninger define personality?","As temperament plus character. Low self-directedness and cooperativeness together suggest a personality disorder of any type.",95,11],[444,"What did Caspi's Dunedin study find about children rated at age 3?","Undercontrolled children had more crime, alcohol problems and antisocial traits as adults. Inhibited children were more often depressed.",95,12],[444,"What are Eysenck's PEN dimensions and how did he explain extraversion?","Psychoticism, extraversion and neuroticism. Extraverts have low baseline cortical arousal, so they seek stimulation and company.",95,13],[444,"Which personality model dominates today and is Eysenck's psychoticism included in it?","The Big Five (OCEAN), measured by the NEO-PI-R. Psychoticism is not one of the five and does not measure psychosis.",95,14],[444,"What did Werner's Kauai study show about resilience?","About one third of high-risk children became competent, caring adults. An easy temperament and one caring adult helped most.",95,15],[444,"What did Murray find about the effects of maternal postnatal depression on infants?","It slowed cognitive and emotional development, most in boys and poorer families, acting through disturbed mother-infant interaction.",95,16],[445,"How does rank-order consistency of personality change across the lifespan?","It rises steadily with age and plateaus between about 50 and 70, so personality is not fixed by 30.",95,17],[446,"How many temperament dimensions were rated in the New York Longitudinal Study?","Nine dimensions, which grouped into three temperament types.",95,18],[448,"Two 4-year-olds build a hospital together, agreeing roles and taking turns. What type of play is this?","Cooperative play, which needs perspective-taking, language and emotional regulation.",96,1],[448,"How do babies first play with objects?","They explore objects with their hands and mouths, a sensorimotor form of play.",96,2],[448,"At what age does symbolic play appear and what is an example?","At about 2 years. One object stands for another, such as using a block as a spoon to feed a teddy.",96,3],[448,"How did Piaget interpret the emergence of pretend play?","As the beginning of thinking in symbols.",96,4],[448,"A 3-year-old boy shows little or no pretend play. Which developmental condition should be considered?","Autism spectrum disorder, as absent or limited pretend play at 2 to 3 years is a warning sign.",96,5],[448,"At what age do children enjoy games with fixed rules and how did Piaget explain this?","At about 5 to 7 years. Piaget linked it to concrete thinking and a growing sense of fairness.",96,6],[448,"What are Parten's stages of social play in order?","Unoccupied, solitary, onlooker, parallel, associative and cooperative play.",96,7],[448,"What is parallel play and at what age is it typical?","Children play side by side without interacting, typically at about 2 to 3 years.",96,8],[448,"How does associative play differ from cooperative play?","In associative play children share toys and chat but have no shared plan. Cooperative play has a common goal with roles and turns.",96,9],[448,"What is onlooker play?","The child watches other children playing without joining in.",96,10],[449,"How common are imaginary companions and what are they linked with?","Up to half of children have one at some time between 3 and 10 years. They are linked with creativity and understanding other minds.",96,11],[449,"A well 4-year-old girl sets a place at the table for her invisible friend. What is the best response?","Reassure the parents that imaginary companions are common and normal at this age. It is not psychosis.",96,12],[449,"By what age do imaginary companions usually fade?","By about 10 to 12 years.",96,13],[449,"What five sociometric groups did Coie and Dodge describe?","Popular, rejected, neglected, controversial and average, based on classmates' like and dislike nominations.",96,14],[449,"What characteristics do popular children tend to show?","They tend to be kind, helpful and skilled at joining in with others.",96,15],[449,"How do boys' and girls' peer groups differ in middle childhood according to Maccoby?","Play is strongly sex-segregated. Boys play in larger, rougher groups and girls in smaller groups that talk more.",96,16],[449,"What do friendships come to rest on in middle childhood?","Trust and loyalty.",96,17],[450,"Which sociometric group carries the highest risk of later delinquency and school drop-out?","Rejected children, who get many dislike and few like votes, especially if they are also aggressive.",96,18],[452,"How did young children in Piaget's stories judge which child was naughtier?","By the amount of damage done, so breaking 15 cups by accident seemed worse than breaking 1 while sneaking a treat.",97,1],[452,"What is Piaget's heteronomous morality, also called moral realism?","An early stage where rules are fixed and come from adults, and wrongness is judged by consequences rather than intention.",97,2],[452,"When do children reach Piaget's autonomous morality and what characterises it?","From about 9 to 10 years. Children judge by intention and see rules as agreements that can be changed.",97,3],[452,"How did Kohlberg study moral development and what did he score?","He presented moral dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma and scored the reasoning given, not the decision itself.",97,4],[452,"Describe Kohlberg's original longitudinal sample.","72 Chicago boys aged 10 to 16, followed for about 20 years. The all-male sample was later criticised.",97,5],[452,"What are Kohlberg's three levels of moral reasoning?","Preconventional, conventional and postconventional, each containing two stages.",97,6],[452,"A 10-year-old girl helps wash up because good daughters help their mums. Which Kohlberg stage is this?","Stage 3, interpersonal concordance or good boy, good girl, at the conventional level.",97,7],[453,"What reasoning characterises Kohlberg's stage 4?","Law and order: laws should be obeyed so that society does not fall apart.",97,8],[453,"At which Kohlberg level do most adolescents and adults reason?","The conventional level, where right is what others approve of and what upholds group rules.",97,9],[453,"What are Kohlberg's postconventional stages and how common is this level?","Stage 5 social contract and stage 6 universal ethical principles. Only about 10 to 15% of adults reach it.",97,10],[453,"What happened to Kohlberg's stage 6?","Very few people ever reached it, so Kohlberg later stopped scoring it.",97,11],[453,"What was Gilligan's main criticism of Kohlberg?","That he ranked an ethic of justice above an ethic of care, which she felt women voiced more, partly because his first sample was all male.",97,12],[453,"Did later research support large sex differences in moral reasoning as Gilligan suggested?","No. Later reviews found only small sex differences, though her work broadened the field.",97,13],[453,"List Selman's levels of social perspective-taking in order.","Egocentric, social-informational, self-reflective, third-person or mutual, and societal.",97,14],[453,"What can a child do at Selman's third-person perspective-taking level?","Step outside a two-person interaction and imagine how a neutral observer sees both people.",97,15],[453,"Why is perspective-taking clinically relevant in children with conduct problems?","Poor perspective-taking is common in conduct problems and can be targeted in therapy. It also matters in forensic and capacity assessments.",97,16],[454,"A 6-year-old boy says taking a toy is wrong because the teacher will tell him off. Which Kohlberg level is this?","Preconventional, where right and wrong are judged by punishment and reward. It is typical up to about 9 or 10 years.",97,17],[454,"A 9-year-old boy will help his brother only if he can use his games console. Which Kohlberg stage is this?","Stage 2, instrumental exchange, a preconventional stage based on you scratch my back and I scratch yours.",97,18],[457,"Which fears are characteristic of infancy?","Fear of loud noises and of falling or loss of support, which are largely reflexive or perceptual.",98,1],[457,"When does the social smile appear and how does it differ from earlier smiles?","At about 6 weeks, in response to a face or voice. Newborn smiles in sleep are only reflexes.",98,2],[457,"According to Lewis, which primary emotions are present in the first months of life?","Joy, interest, anger, sadness, fear, disgust and surprise.",98,3],[457,"When do embarrassment, envy and empathy emerge and what do they require?","From about 18 months. They need a sense of self, shown by mirror self-recognition at about the same age.",98,4],[457,"When do evaluative emotions such as pride, guilt and shame appear?","At about 2.5 to 3 years, once the child can judge herself against standards.",98,5],[457,"A 3-year-old girl hides and says she is a bad girl after breaking a vase. Which emotion is this?","Shame, a self-conscious evaluative emotion.",98,6],[457,"What is social referencing and when does it develop?","Looking to a trusted adult's expression to decide how to feel about an uncertain situation. It develops towards the end of the first year.",98,7],[457,"What did Sorce and colleagues show using the visual cliff?","Most 12-month-olds crossed when their mother smiled, but none crossed when she looked afraid, demonstrating social referencing.",98,8],[457,"What do emotional literacy and emotional regulation mean and when do they develop?","The ability to name and manage feelings. They continue to grow through childhood and adolescence.",98,9],[457,"When does stranger anxiety start, peak and fade?","It starts at about 6 to 9 months, peaks at about 12 months and fades during the second year.",98,10],[458,"What developmental advance does stranger anxiety reflect?","The baby can now distinguish familiar faces from unfamiliar ones.",98,11],[458,"What makes separation anxiety possible, and to what did Bowlby link it?","The baby must see the mother as a separate person who can leave. Bowlby linked it to the development of attachment.",98,12],[458,"At about what age does fear of animals typically appear?","At about 3 years.",98,13],[458,"A child newly insists on a night light, afraid of the dark. At what age is this most typical?","At about 4 years. It would be unusual in a 10-month-old.",98,14],[458,"When do fears of monsters, ghosts and imaginary creatures typically appear?","At about 5 to 7 years, once imagination has become rich.",98,15],[458,"Which fears emerge from adolescence with abstract thought?","Fears of illness, death and failure.",98,16],[458,"When do social-evaluative fears peak and why is this relevant?","In early to mid-adolescence, especially in girls. This matches the usual onset of social anxiety disorder.",98,17],[458,"What distinguishes a normal childhood fear from a phobia?","Normal fears are age-appropriate and pass. A fear that persists, is excessive or impairs life suggests a phobia.",98,18],[458,"Whose review established the normal developmental order of childhood fears?","Gullone, who found the order of fears tracks the growth of thinking skills.",98,19],[459,"Which emotion is present in the first six months: anger, pride or shame?","Anger, a primary emotion. Pride and shame are self-conscious emotions that come much later.",98,20],[461,"What did Kinsey's 0 to 6 scale suggest about sexual orientation?","That orientation lies on a continuum from exclusively heterosexual to exclusively homosexual, with many people between the extremes.",99,1],[461,"What did the extra category X on Kinsey's scale denote?","People with no sociosexual contacts or reactions.",99,2],[461,"What three components of sexual orientation does later work separate?","Attraction, behaviour and identity, which do not always match.",99,3],[461,"Define gender identity and the age by which most children have it.","A person's inner sense of their own gender and the label they give themselves. Most children have it by 2 to 3 years.",99,4],[461,"How does gender role differ from sexual orientation?","Gender role is the behaviour a culture expects of a gender. Sexual orientation is who a person is attracted to, and the two vary independently.",99,5],[461,"What term does DSM-5-TR use, and what does it focus on?","Gender dysphoria, which focuses on clinically significant distress.",99,6],[461,"What are the three steps in Kohlberg's cognitive account of gender development and their ages?","Gender labelling at about 2 years, gender stability at about 4 and gender constancy at about 5 to 7.",99,7],[461,"A 4-year-old boy thinks wearing a dress would make him a girl. Which stage has he not reached?","Gender constancy, the understanding that gender does not change with clothes, hair or play.",99,8],[462,"What does gender schema theory propose?","Children actively sort the world into gender categories, for example asking if a toy is for boys or girls before playing.",99,9],[462,"How does social learning theory explain gender behaviour?","Children learn through reward, punishment and copying same-gender models, using modelling and self-efficacy. Archetypes belong to Jung, not this theory.",99,10],[462,"What did Freud propose about the origin of gender identity?","It came from identifying with the same-sex parent as the Oedipus complex resolves. There is little evidence for this.",99,11],[462,"What do studies of girls with congenital adrenal hyperplasia show?","Prenatal androgen exposure leads to more male-typical toy and activity preferences on average, yet most identify as girls.",99,12],[462,"What is the fraternal birth order effect?","In men, each older biological brother from the same mother raises the chance of being gay. It is the most reliable family finding.",99,13],[462,"What did Bogaert show about the fraternal birth order effect and what does it suggest?","It holds for biological brothers raised apart but not for step or adopted brothers, suggesting a prenatal cause.",99,14],[462,"What are Cass's six stages of gay identity formation and a criticism of the model?","Confusion, comparison, tolerance, acceptance, pride and synthesis. Critics say it is too linear.",99,15],[462,"How does Meyer's minority stress model explain higher rates of depression in lesbian, gay and bisexual people?","External stressors such as discrimination and internal ones such as expecting rejection, concealment and internalised homophobia. Stigma, not orientation, causes the risk.",99,16],[462,"A 24-year-old gay man presents with depression in a UK clinic. Which stressors should you explore?","Discrimination, abuse, concealment of identity, expected rejection and internalised stigma, which drive much of the extra risk.",99,17],[463,"A 16-year-old boy identifies as male, enjoys masculine hobbies and is attracted to boys. Which feature is his sexual orientation?","His attraction to boys. His male identity is gender identity and his hobbies reflect gender role.",99,18],[463,"How does ICD-11 classify gender incongruence?","As a condition related to sexual health, not as a mental disorder, while keeping a code to support access to care.",99,19],[466,"What is usually the first visible sign of puberty in girls, and at about what age does it appear?","Breast budding (thelarche) is usually first, at about 10 to 11 years.",100,1],[466,"At roughly what age does menarche occur on average in UK girls?","Menarche occurs at about 12.5 to 13 years.",100,2],[466,"In girls, how is menarche timed in relation to the growth spurt and Tanner breast staging?","Menarche usually comes after peak height velocity, at about Tanner breast stage 4. Most height gain has therefore already happened.",100,3],[466,"What does Tanner staging rate and how many stages does it use?","It rates breast or genital development and pubic hair in five stages, from stage 1 (child) to stage 5 (adult).",100,4],[466,"When do boys usually start puberty, and what is the first sign?","Boys start at about 11 to 12 years, roughly a year after girls. The first sign is testicular enlargement to 4 ml or more.",100,5],[466,"Below what ages is puberty defined as precocious in girls and in boys?","Puberty is precocious if it starts before 8 years in girls or before 9 years in boys.",100,6],[466,"How has the age of menarche changed over the last 150 years, and why?","It has fallen from about 16 to about 13 years. This secular trend mainly reflects better nutrition and general health.",100,7],[466,"What problems are linked to early pubertal maturation in girls compared with their peers?","Early maturing girls have more depression, alcohol and drug use and early sexual activity. They meet adult pressures, often via older friends, before they can cope.",100,8],[466,"According to Belsky, which early childhood experiences bring puberty forward in girls?","Family stress and father absence in early childhood are linked to earlier puberty in girls.",100,9],[466,"What did Angold and colleagues find predicted the rise of depression in adolescent girls?","The higher rate of depression in girls appeared after Tanner stage III. It tracked pubertal status more closely than chronological age.",100,10],[467,"Who described adolescence as storm and stress, and does research support this view?","G Stanley Hall (1904). Research does not support it, as most adolescents pass through without major turmoil.",100,11],[467,"What did Offer find when following ordinary schoolboys into adulthood?","Most passed through adolescence without major turmoil. Only about a fifth had a stormy course.",100,12],[467,"What did Rutter's Isle of Wight study show about 14 to 15 year olds?","Inner feelings of misery were fairly common and often unseen by adults. However, alienation from parents was uncommon.",100,13],[467,"What is parent-adolescent conflict usually about, and when is it most frequent?","It is mostly bickering about everyday matters such as chores, curfews and appearance, while core values are shared. It is most frequent in early adolescence.",100,14],[467,"Define Elkind's imaginary audience and personal fable in adolescent thinking.","The imaginary audience is feeling that everyone is watching and judging you. The personal fable is believing you are unique and that bad things only happen to others.",100,15],[467,"What did Steinberg show about peers and risk-taking in teenagers?","In a driving game, the presence of peers increased risk-taking much more in teenagers than in adults.",100,16],[467,"How does the timing of brain maturation explain adolescent risk-taking?","The reward system matures early while frontal self-control matures later, so drive outpaces restraint.",100,17],[467,"What change in sleep timing occurs at puberty and what is its practical effect?","Teenagers develop a delayed sleep phase, falling asleep and waking later. Early school starts therefore cause tiredness.",100,18],[467,"According to Kessler, by what age have half of all lifetime mental disorders begun?","About half of all lifetime mental disorders begin by around 14 years of age.",100,19],[467,"A 15-year-old girl in a UK clinic has marked, persistent distress; parents call it normal teenage turmoil. How should you respond?","Severe turmoil is not the norm, so it needs proper assessment rather than reassurance. Ask about pubertal timing, sleep and peer pressures.",100,20],[470,"What is homogamy (assortative mating)?","It is the tendency to choose partners similar to oneself in age, education, class, attitudes and values.",101,1],[470,"Which factors bring partners together, and which keep them together, in partner choice research?","Living nearby (proximity) and physical attractiveness bring people together. Similarity keeps them together.",101,2],[470,"What do Murstein's stimulus-value-role theory and Kerckhoff and Davis's filter theory propose?","We screen potential partners in stages, moving from background to values to roles. The idea that opposites attract has weak support.",101,3],[470,"What happens on average to couple relationship satisfaction after the first child is born?","It declines on average, linked to less sleep, less time and new roles.",101,4],[470,"What is empty nest distress, and how common is poor adaptation?","Feeling flat and without purpose when the last child leaves, while still functioning. Most parents adapt well, and distress is likelier when parenting was their main role.",101,5],[470,"Who are the sandwich generation and what stresses do they face?","Midlife adults caring for both their children and their ageing parents. They face stress, money worries and carer burden.",101,6],[471,"How did Moos and Schaefer conceptualise serious illness, and name their general adaptive tasks.","Illness is a life crisis with adaptive tasks. General tasks include keeping emotional balance, preserving self-image, keeping relationships and preparing for an uncertain future.",101,7],[471,"List Kübler-Ross's five reactions and state which group she studied.","Denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance. She studied dying patients, not bereaved relatives, and the stages are not fixed or universal.",101,8],[471,"In Kübler-Ross's model, what is bargaining and where is anger often directed?","Bargaining is trying to delay death through a deal, such as living to see a wedding. Anger is often directed at staff and family.",101,9],[471,"What did Lindemann describe after the Cocoanut Grove fire?","He described acute grief: physical distress, preoccupation with the dead person, guilt and anger.",101,10],[471,"What are the four phases of grief described by Bowlby and Parkes, in order?","Shock and numbness, then yearning and searching with angry protest, then disorganisation and despair, and finally reorganisation.",101,11],[471,"A 70-year-old Welsh widow occasionally sees her late husband in his chair and knows he is dead. Is this pathological?","No. Rees found about half of widowed people sensed or saw their dead spouse; with insight this is normal and often comforting.",101,12],[471,"What features are normal in the first months of grief?","Pining, weight loss and brief glimpses of the dead person are part of a normal intermediate phase of grief.",101,13],[471,"What are Worden's four tasks of mourning?","Accept the reality of the loss, work through the pain, adjust to a world without the person, and find an enduring connection while starting a new life.",101,14],[471,"What does Stroebe and Schut's dual process model of grief propose?","Healthy grief oscillates between facing the loss and attending to new tasks and life changes.",101,15],[471,"What is the minimum time since death for prolonged grief disorder in ICD-11 and in DSM-5-TR?","ICD-11 requires at least 6 months. DSM-5-TR requires 12 months in adults and 6 months in children.",101,16],[471,"What are the core features of prolonged grief disorder?","Persistent, pervasive longing for or preoccupation with the deceased, with intense emotional pain and impairment beyond cultural norms.",101,17],[471,"Which circumstances carry the highest risk of prolonged grief disorder?","A sudden or violent death, or when the survivor was highly dependent on the deceased.",101,18],[472,"A 58-year-old man feels numb days after his wife's sudden death and says it does not feel real. Which grief phase is this?","Shock and numbness, which lasts hours to a few weeks and may protect against being overwhelmed.",101,19],[472,"How does Worden's task model differ from older stage models of grief?","It frames the bereaved as active rather than passive and sees continuing bonds, not complete detachment, as healthy.",101,20],[474,"A 30-year-old man develops nausea, abdominal pain and weight gain during his partner's pregnancy, with normal tests. What is this?","Couvade syndrome, a recognised phenomenon in expectant fathers rather than a formal diagnosis. Symptoms resolve after the birth.",102,1],[474,"What is the typical time course of symptoms in Couvade syndrome?","Symptoms usually appear in the first trimester, settle, recur late in pregnancy and resolve after delivery.",102,2],[474,"How does pseudocyesis differ from Couvade syndrome?","In pseudocyesis the person believes they are pregnant and shows signs such as amenorrhoea and abdominal distension.",102,3],[474,"What is meant by the all or none effect in the pre-embryonic period?","In weeks 0 to 2 after conception, harm either causes loss of the embryo or full recovery.",102,4],[474,"When is the embryo at greatest risk of major structural birth defects from teratogens?","In the embryonic period, about weeks 3 to 8 after conception, when the organs form.",102,5],[474,"What type of harm do teratogens mainly cause in the fetal period from week 9?","Harm mainly affects growth and brain function and size rather than physical structure.",102,6],[474,"When does the neural tube close, and what does this mean for folic acid?","By about day 28 after conception, often before pregnancy is known. Folic acid must therefore start before conception.",102,7],[474,"Which psychotropic is most linked to neural tube defects, and what UK safeguard applies?","Sodium valproate, which also causes later learning problems. It requires a pregnancy prevention programme in those who could become pregnant.",102,8],[474,"What is the main teratogenic risk of first-trimester lithium?","A raised relative risk of cardiac defects, including Ebstein's anomaly, though the absolute risk is small.",102,9],[475,"How do lithium levels change during pregnancy and after delivery, and why?","Levels fall in pregnancy as plasma volume and GFR rise. After birth these drop back quickly, so levels can rise sharply with a risk of toxicity.",102,10],[475,"What facial features suggest fetal alcohol spectrum disorder?","A smooth philtrum, thin upper lip and short palpebral fissures, with growth and learning problems.",102,11],[475,"How common are baby blues, and when do symptoms peak and resolve?","They affect 50 to 80% of mothers, peak on days 3 to 5 and settle within about two weeks.",102,12],[475,"What proportion of mothers and fathers develop postnatal depression?","About 10 to 15% of mothers in the first year, and about 8 to 10% of fathers.",102,13],[475,"How common is postpartum psychosis, when does it start and who is most at risk?","About 1 to 2 per 1,000 births, with sudden onset usually within two weeks. Risk is much higher with a bipolar history.",102,14],[475,"A 29-year-old woman with bipolar disorder becomes acutely confused and psychotic five days postpartum. How urgent is this?","Postpartum psychosis is a psychiatric emergency needing immediate assessment, usually with admission to a mother and baby unit.",102,15],[475,"Distinguish primary from secondary tokophobia.","Tokophobia is severe fear of childbirth. Primary tokophobia occurs before any birth, while secondary follows a traumatic delivery.",102,16],[475,"What are the features of postpartum PTSD, and what best predicts it?","Intrusive memories, nightmares, avoidance and hyperarousal. The key factor is how frightening and out of control the birth felt.",102,17],[476,"Why is the history of drinking in pregnancy important when assessing for fetal alcohol spectrum disorder?","Many affected children lack the facial signs, so prenatal alcohol history and neurodevelopmental assessment are key. No safe amount of alcohol in pregnancy is known.",102,18],[476,"What did O'Connor find in the Avon cohort about antenatal anxiety?","High anxiety at 32 weeks predicted more child behavioural and emotional problems at age 4, even after adjusting for postnatal mood.",102,19],[476,"What mechanism is proposed for antenatal stress programming, and what are its limitations?","Altered fetal HPA axis development, possibly via placental cortisol transfer. Effects are modest and genetic and postnatal confounding cannot be excluded.",102,20],[478,"A 34-year-old who saw himself chiefly as a soldier leaves the army after injury and feels lost. What explains this?","A role transition has disrupted an adult identity built around a single role.",103,1],[478,"Why do some people adapt better than others to losing a major life role?","People with several valued roles adapt more easily to losing one.",103,2],[478,"What did Erikson describe as the developmental task of adolescence?","Identity versus role confusion.",103,3],[478,"Which two dimensions did Marcia cross to produce his four identity statuses?","Exploration (which he called crisis) and commitment.",103,4],[478,"Define identity achievement in Marcia's model.","Commitment to a choice after a period of exploration.",103,5],[478,"Define moratorium in Marcia's model.","Currently exploring options without yet committing. It is anxious but healthy.",103,6],[478,"A 22-year-old follows his father into law without considering anything else. Which Marcia status is this?","Foreclosure: commitment without exploration, often taking on a parent's path.",103,7],[478,"Define identity diffusion and its associated features.","Neither exploration nor commitment, drifting with a whatever-happens attitude. It is linked to apathy, low self-esteem and peer vulnerability, and can be temporary.",103,8],[478,"What is Arnett's emerging adulthood, and what is it characterised by?","The phase from about 18 to 25 in industrialised societies, marked by exploring, change, self-focus and feeling in between.",103,9],[478,"Why has emerging adulthood been stretched out in modern societies?","Longer education and later marriage extend this phase.",103,10],[478,"What did Levinson describe in his 1978 study of 40 men?","Seasons of adult life in which periods of building alternate with periods of transition.",103,11],[478,"When do Levinson's age-30 transition and midlife transition occur?","The age-30 transition is about 28 to 33 years and the midlife transition about 40 to 45 years.",103,12],[479,"Is the midlife crisis universal according to large surveys?","No. Only a minority, about a quarter in the US MIDUS survey, report one, often linked to life events rather than age.",103,13],[479,"How did Super view a career, and what are his five career stages?","As gradual expression of the self-concept. Stages are growth, exploration, establishment, maintenance and disengagement.",103,14],[479,"What does Super's maintenance stage involve and at what age?","At about 45 to 64, holding one's position and keeping skills up to date, not seeking promotion. People may cycle back after redundancy.",103,15],[479,"What are Phinney's stages of ethnic identity development?","Unexamined (adopting others' views), ethnic identity search (moratorium, often after discrimination) and achieved ethnic identity.",103,16],[479,"What is achieved ethnic identity associated with in Phinney's model?","Feeling secure in one's ethnic heritage, which is associated with higher self-esteem.",103,17],[479,"A 66-year-old man in a UK clinic becomes apathetic after retirement and identity loss. What should you consider?","Role loss commonly disrupts identity; help build other valued roles, and screen for depression, which apathy may mask.",103,18],[479,"What is the key distinction between foreclosure and moratorium?","Foreclosure is commitment without exploration. Moratorium is exploration without commitment.",103,19],[479,"A 21-year-old woman explored courses and beliefs for years, then committed to nursing. Which Marcia status applies?","Identity achievement, because she committed after a period of exploration.",103,20],[482,"What did disengagement theory of Cumming and Henry (1961) propose about older people and society?","Older people and society withdraw from each other in a mutual, inevitable process. The theory saw this as functional, preparing both for the person's death.",104,1],[482,"How does Havighurst's activity theory differ from disengagement theory?","Activity theory says withdrawal is imposed on older people rather than chosen. Staying active and engaged is what keeps them well.",104,2],[482,"What does Atchley's continuity theory say about how people adapt to ageing?","People adapt by keeping their usual habits, relationships and sense of identity. Later life is a continuation of earlier patterns.",104,3],[482,"An 80-year-old woman now sees only her two closest friends and is content. Which theory explains this?","Socioemotional selectivity theory (Carstensen). As time feels limited, people choose fewer but emotionally closer relationships.",104,4],[482,"What is the positivity effect in older adults?","Older adults attend to and remember positive information more than negative information.",104,5],[482,"What is Tornstam's gerotranscendence?","A shift in very old age towards a more cosmic, less self-centred view of life.",104,6],[482,"How do fluid and crystallised intelligence change with normal ageing?","Fluid intelligence, solving new problems quickly, declines from early adulthood. Crystallised intelligence, knowledge and vocabulary, is maintained into the 60s and 70s.",104,7],[482,"What did Schaie's Seattle Longitudinal Study show about cognitive decline with age?","There was little true decline before about age 60. Cross-sectional studies exaggerate decline because of cohort effects.",104,8],[483,"Which type of memory is most affected by normal ageing, and which are relatively spared?","Episodic memory for recent events, especially free recall, is most affected. Semantic and procedural memory are relatively spared.",104,9],[483,"What is cognitive reserve?","People with more education and mental activity can tolerate more brain pathology before dementia becomes clinically apparent.",104,10],[483,"What structural brain and sleep changes are typical of healthy ageing?","Gradual volume loss, most in prefrontal cortex and hippocampus, with enlarged ventricles. Sleep has less slow-wave sleep, more night waking and an earlier sleep phase.",104,11],[483,"What is the Hayflick limit?","Normal cells can divide only a limited number of times, as telomeres at chromosome ends shorten with each division.",104,12],[483,"A 74-year-old man becomes withdrawn and suspicious in noisy company. What sensory change should be checked?","Presbycusis, bilateral high-frequency hearing loss. It also matters because midlife hearing loss is among the largest modifiable risk factors for dementia.",104,13],[483,"Why should medicines be started at low doses in older adults?","Older people clear drugs more slowly and have more body fat, so drugs accumulate. Start low and go slow.",104,14],[483,"When is a woman most at risk of new depressive symptoms around the menopause, and what is the domino hypothesis?","Risk is highest during the perimenopause, even without past depression. In the domino hypothesis, night sweats disrupt sleep and poor sleep then lowers mood.",104,15],[483,"What does NICE say about HRT for low mood linked to the menopause?","HRT can be considered for menopause-related low mood, though evidence is limited. It does not replace treatment for established depression.",104,16],[483,"What is Baltes' model of selective optimisation with compensation?","Older people choose fewer goals, practise and optimise them, and use strategies to compensate for losses.",104,17],[483,"How did Rowe and Kahn define successful ageing?","Low risk of disease and disability, high physical and cognitive function, and active engagement with life.",104,18],[483,"Who coined the term ageism, and how much does loneliness raise the risk of death?","Robert Butler coined ageism in 1969. Loneliness raises mortality risk by about a quarter, comparable to well-known risks such as smoking.",104,19],[483,"A 78-year-old woman reports aches, poor sleep and memory complaints but denies sadness. What should be considered?","Late-life depression, which often presents with physical and memory complaints rather than low mood. Also ask about loneliness and abuse, which affects about 1 in 6 older people yearly.",104,20],[484,"What did Salthouse propose as the main cause of age-related cognitive decline?","Slowing of processing speed, which limits how much can be done in the time available.",104,21],[484,"A healthy 70-year-old with excellent vocabulary reports more tip-of-the-tongue moments. What is the likely explanation?","A normal age-related increase in tip-of-the-tongue states, from weaker links between meaning and word form. Progressive comprehension or functional problems would need assessment.",104,22]];